ForgeData.register("gcse-econ",{"GCSE-ECON-P1-FOUND":{"label":"Paper 1: Economic Foundations","color":"#166534","questions":[{"id":"GCSE-P1-FND-01","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-FOUND","stem":"A town has enough land to build either a health centre or 120 new homes, but not both. Which economic idea is illustrated when the council chooses the health centre?","options":{"A":"Scarcity and opportunity cost","B":"Unlimited resources","C":"High-income countries have no wants","D":"A decrease in demand"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-SCARCITY","scaffold":"Resources are limited relative to wants, so a choice must be made. The opportunity cost is the next best alternative given up - here, the homes - rather than every possible alternative.","reforge":{"stem":"A student spends Saturday revising rather than working a paid shift. What is the student's opportunity cost?","options":{"A":"The opportunity cost is the medical equipment produced","B":"The revision notes produced","C":"The wages from the next best work shift given up","D":"The cost of the textbook"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-found"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-FND-02","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-FOUND","stem":"Which item is a factor of production classified as capital?","options":{"A":"The owner who organises the bakery","B":"A delivery van used by a bakery","C":"The flour used to make bread","D":"A baker's time and skill"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-FACTORS","scaffold":"Capital means man-made resources used to produce other goods and services, such as machinery, tools, buildings and vehicles. It does not mean money itself. Land is natural resources, labour is human effort and enterprise organises production and takes risk.","reforge":{"stem":"A farmer buys a new irrigation system for the fields. Which factor of production has increased?","options":{"A":"Land","B":"Labour","C":"Enterprise","D":"Capital"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-found"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-FND-03","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-FOUND","stem":"Why can a country experience scarcity even when its average income is high?","options":{"A":"High-income countries have no wants","B":"Consumers have no influence at all on the level of demand","C":"Resources remain finite while wants continue to expand","D":"Scarcity means a country has low GDP"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-SCARCITY","scaffold":"Scarcity is universal: wealth can increase the range of goods people can buy, but it does not make land, skilled workers, time or raw materials unlimited. Scarcity is therefore different from poverty.","reforge":{"stem":"Which option best distinguishes scarcity from poverty?","options":{"A":"Scarcity is limited resources relative to wants; poverty is low income or resources.","B":"They are two names for exactly the same problem","C":"Poverty exists in rich countries but scarcity does not","D":"It guarantees that all children in the country start out with exactly the same resources"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-found"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-FND-04","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-FOUND","stem":"A country moves workers and machines from producing food to producing medical equipment. What is the main trade-off?","options":{"A":"The opportunity cost is the medical equipment produced","B":"The opportunity cost is the country's population","C":"There is no opportunity cost because both are useful","D":"The opportunity cost is the food no longer produced"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-SCARCITY","scaffold":"Opportunity cost is the benefit of the next best alternative forgone. When resources are switched into medical equipment, the relevant sacrificed benefit is the food those resources would have produced.","reforge":{"stem":"A bakery uses its only oven to make celebration cakes instead of bread for one afternoon. The opportunity cost is:","options":{"A":"The cakes sold","B":"The bread that could have been produced and sold","C":"The price of the oven","D":"The money value of the extra health spending itself"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-found"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-FND-05","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-FOUND","stem":"A firm separates production into small tasks so each worker repeats one specialised job. Which is a likely benefit?","options":{"A":"Workers may become faster and more skilled through repetition","B":"That every firm in the market has exactly the same share of sales","C":"The firm must produce fewer units","D":"Every worker must buy their own machinery"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-SPECIALISATION","scaffold":"Division of labour can raise productivity because repetition improves skill and saves time moving between tasks. It may also allow specialised machinery, although boredom and dependence on a production line are possible drawbacks.","reforge":{"stem":"Which is a possible disadvantage of extensive division of labour?","options":{"A":"Training can never be focused","B":"Scarcity affects only households; poverty affects only firms","C":"Workers may become bored doing a narrow, repetitive task","D":"The firm loses all economies of scale"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-found"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-FND-06","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-FOUND","stem":"Which example is an economic service rather than a physical product?","options":{"A":"A packet of rice","B":"A train journey","C":"A bicycle","D":"A refrigerator"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-SECTORS","scaffold":"A service is an intangible activity provided to a consumer, such as transport, insurance or a cinema visit. Products are physical goods that can be handled, although some purchases combine both goods and services.","reforge":{"stem":"Which activity is part of the tertiary sector?","options":{"A":"Mining copper","B":"Building a railway station","C":"Growing wheat","D":"Providing car insurance"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-found"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-FND-07","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-FOUND","stem":"An entrepreneur notices demand for low-cost school lunches, organises workers and invests savings to open a cafe. Which role is being described?","options":{"A":"Charging exactly the same flat amount of tax to every household in the country each year","B":"Labour - supplying physical effort only","C":"Enterprise - organising resources and taking the risk of running the business","D":"Capital - supplying a machine only"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-FACTORS","scaffold":"Enterprise brings the other factors of production together, makes decisions and accepts the risk that the business may not succeed. The entrepreneur may also provide labour or capital, but the organising and risk-taking role is enterprise.","reforge":{"stem":"Which action best shows enterprise?","options":{"A":"A founder identifies an opportunity, hires staff and accepts the risk of loss","B":"A bank lends money for a new machine","C":"A field provides space for a crop","D":"A deficit is measured across ten years while debt is measured within a single year"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-found"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-FND-08","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-FOUND","stem":"A country is producing inside its production possibility frontier. What does this suggest?","options":{"A":"It is using all resources at maximum efficiency","B":"Its resources are unlimited","C":"It has no opportunity costs","D":"It has unused or inefficiently used resources"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-PPF","scaffold":"A PPF shows the maximum combinations of two goods possible with current resources and technology. A point inside it indicates spare capacity or inefficiency; a point on the frontier is productively efficient. Moving along the frontier still involves opportunity cost.","reforge":{"stem":"What could shift a country's PPF outward?","options":{"A":"A rise in consumer confidence only","B":"Investment in better technology or worker training","C":"A fall in demand for its exports","D":"Previously unemployed workers finding existing jobs"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-found"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-FND-09","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-FOUND","stem":"Which action best illustrates enterprise as a factor of production?","options":{"A":"Organising resources and accepting business risk","B":"Doing a routine job for a wage","C":"A worker follows instructions on a production line","D":"Supplying a machine for production"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-FACTORS","scaffold":"Enterprise is the factor that organises land, labour and capital and accepts the risk of making a loss. An entrepreneur is the person who performs this role; enterprise describes the economic function rather than the person's name.","reforge":{"stem":"A student wants to show enterprise rather than simply name an entrepreneur. Which example works best?","options":{"A":"Working behind the cafe counter for an hourly wage","B":"Naming a famous business founder","C":"Starting a cafe, hiring staff and risking savings","D":"Owning the building used by the cafe"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-found"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-FND-10","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-FOUND","stem":"A country uses scarce resources to produce more hospitals. What must it give up?","options":{"A":"Every other possible output","B":"The next best alternative use of those resources","C":"There is no opportunity cost because both are useful","D":"The country's entire capital stock"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-SCARCITY","scaffold":"Scarcity forces choices because resources are limited relative to wants. Opportunity cost is the benefit of the next best alternative forgone, not all alternatives or the money price alone.","reforge":{"stem":"A council uses land for a school instead of a sports centre. The opportunity cost is:","options":{"A":"The opportunity cost is the country's population","B":"The school that is built","C":"The council's total annual budget","D":"The sports centre that could have been built"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-found"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-FND-11","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-FOUND","stem":"What is a likely disadvantage when workers repeat one narrow task for a long period?","options":{"A":"Specialisation becomes impossible","B":"The firm cannot use machinery","C":"Workers may become bored and less motivated","D":"Workers become less familiar with their task"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-SPECIALISATION","scaffold":"Division of labour can raise productivity through repetition, but narrow tasks may cause boredom, lower motivation and dependence on other workers or machines. The effect depends on how the work is organised.","reforge":{"stem":"A hotel gives each employee one very narrow role. Which drawback should managers consider?","options":{"A":"Workers may become bored with repetitive work","B":"Specialisation always removes all training needs","C":"The hotel can no longer measure output","D":"Every worker must become a manager"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-found"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-FND-12","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-FOUND","stem":"Six workers produce 900 units in a day. What is output per worker?","options":{"A":"6 units","B":"5,400 units","C":"894 units","D":"150 units"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-PRODUCTIVITY","scaffold":"Labour productivity is output per worker or per hour. Here, 900 units divided by 6 workers equals 150 units per worker. Productivity is different from total production, which is 900 units.","reforge":{"stem":"Output rises from 900 to 1,200 units while six workers remain employed. What happens to output per worker?","options":{"A":"It stays at 900 units","B":"It rises from 150 to 200 units","C":"It falls from 200 to 150 units","D":"It becomes 300 units per worker"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-found"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-FND-13","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-FOUND","stem":"An economy moves from an inefficient point inside its PPF to a point on the frontier. What has happened?","options":{"A":"Existing resources are being used more fully","B":"It is using all resources at maximum efficiency","C":"Scarcity has disappeared","D":"Potential output has definitely fallen"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-PPF","scaffold":"Moving from inside the PPF to the frontier uses existing resources more fully or efficiently. An outward shift requires increased productive capacity, such as better technology, more capital or improved skills.","reforge":{"stem":"Which change is most likely to shift a PPF outward rather than move the economy towards its existing frontier?","options":{"A":"Previously unemployed workers finding existing jobs","B":"A rise in consumer confidence","C":"Investment in new productive machinery","D":"Firms using spare factory space"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-found"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-FND-14","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-FOUND","stem":"Which activity belongs to the tertiary sector?","options":{"A":"Extracting iron ore","B":"Providing hotel accommodation","C":"Growing wheat","D":"All future earnings from employment"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-SECTORS","scaffold":"The tertiary sector provides services, including transport, retail, banking, insurance and hospitality. The primary sector extracts or grows resources, while the secondary sector manufactures goods.","reforge":{"stem":"A business that provides accountancy advice is operating in which sector?","options":{"A":"Primary","B":"Extractive","C":"Secondary","D":"Tertiary"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-found"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-FND-15","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-FOUND","stem":"A student gives up a paid shift to attend an economics lesson. What is the opportunity cost?","options":{"A":"The lesson attended","B":"The student's future career","C":"The wages from the shift forgone","D":"All wages the student could ever earn"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-SCARCITY","scaffold":"The opportunity cost of a choice is the benefit of the next best alternative given up. In this case, it is the pay from the shift, not every possible future income.","reforge":{"stem":"A firm uses its only delivery van for a new contract instead of its usual route. The opportunity cost is:","options":{"A":"The benefit from the usual route forgone","B":"No output, because hospitals are essential","C":"The profit from the new contract","D":"The firm's entire revenue"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-found"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-FND-16","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-FOUND","stem":"Which item is labour rather than capital?","options":{"A":"A warehouse","B":"A delivery truck","C":"Land - providing the natural resources only","D":"A machine operator's time and skill"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-FACTORS","scaffold":"Labour is human effort, including physical work, skills and knowledge. Capital consists of man-made resources used to produce other goods and services, such as machinery, vehicles and buildings.","reforge":{"stem":"Which example is capital in a school?","options":{"A":"A teacher's lesson planning","B":"A science laboratory and its equipment","C":"A pupil's effort","D":"Labour - supplying physical effort only"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-found"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-FND-17","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-FOUND","stem":"Which description best defines a market?","options":{"A":"An arrangement bringing buyers and sellers together","B":"Increasing output regardless of what it costs to produce","C":"A government list of fixed prices","D":"A business with no competitors"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-MARKET-DEF","scaffold":"A market is any arrangement that allows buyers and sellers to exchange goods, services or resources. It can be physical, online or financial.","reforge":{"stem":"What is a market economy?","options":{"A":"An economy with no scarcity","B":"A compulsory payment that households must make to the government by law each year","C":"One where supply and demand help allocate scarce resources","D":"One that produces only services"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-found"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-FND-18","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-FOUND","stem":"Which activity is in the primary sector?","options":{"A":"Designing a banking app","B":"Extracting timber from a managed forest","C":"Every worker must buy their own machinery","D":"Providing legal advice"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-SECTORS","scaffold":"The primary sector uses natural resources directly, including extraction of materials from land or sea. Manufacturing and services belong to later sectors.","reforge":{"stem":"Which activity belongs to the tertiary sector?","options":{"A":"Assembling smartphones","B":"Making cement","C":"Growing wheat","D":"Providing insurance"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-found"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-FND-19","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-FOUND","stem":"What is traded in a factor market?","options":{"A":"Only finished food","B":"Shares in a public company","C":"Services of land, labour, capital or enterprise","D":"Product markets exist only online and nowhere else"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-MARKET-DEF","scaffold":"Factor markets exchange the services of factors of production. Product markets instead offer final goods and services to consumers, firms and the public sector.","reforge":{"stem":"Which transaction takes place in a product market?","options":{"A":"A consumer buying a finished meal","B":"A household selling land","C":"A place where only firms can trade","D":"A firm hiring a worker"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-found"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-FND-20","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-FOUND","stem":"What does specialisation involve?","options":{"A":"It makes the firm's product identical to that of every rival in the market","B":"Avoiding all exchange","C":"Using unlimited resources","D":"Concentrating on products a person or economy makes relatively well"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-SPECIALISATION","scaffold":"Specialisation means concentrating production on goods or services that a person, firm, region or country is relatively good at producing. Exchange allows other wants to be met.","reforge":{"stem":"What is division of labour?","options":{"A":"Consumers buying only local goods","B":"Workers concentrating on one part of a production process","C":"Firms producing without employees","D":"Scarcity affects only households; poverty affects only firms"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-found"}]},"GCSE-ECON-P1-MARKETS":{"label":"Paper 1: The Role of Markets","color":"#15803d","questions":[{"id":"GCSE-P1-MKT-01","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-MARKETS","stem":"What is a market?","options":{"A":"Any arrangement that allows buyers and sellers to exchange goods, services or resources","B":"Only a physical shop or store where goods are sold to buyers","C":"A government department that decides and sets all of the prices charged anywhere in the economy","D":"A business that faces no competitors at all in its industry"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-MARKET-DEF","scaffold":"A market can be physical, such as a high street, or online and financial. Its essential feature is interaction between buyers and sellers, allowing exchange and helping determine price and quantity.","reforge":{"stem":"Which is an example of a factor market?","options":{"A":"Households buying washing machines","B":"Consumers buying cinema tickets","C":"Firms hiring software engineers","D":"Tourists booking hotel rooms"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-markets"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-MKT-02","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-MARKETS","stem":"A price rises because demand for a product has increased while supply is unchanged. Which function of the price mechanism is most directly shown?","options":{"A":"Guaranteeing that every consumer can buy the product","B":"Signalling that the product is in greater demand","C":"Removing all competition from the market","D":"Making the resources available unlimited"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-PRICE-FUNC","scaffold":"Prices signal information to buyers and sellers. A higher price can communicate scarcity or stronger demand. It may also ration the product and create an incentive for firms to supply more, but the information role is the clearest here.","reforge":{"stem":"A rise in the price of scarce concert tickets prevents some people from buying them. Which price function is this?","options":{"A":"Specialisation - workers repeat one narrow task","B":"Production - the price creates more venues almost immediately","C":"Redistribution - income is transferred to every buyer in the ticket market","D":"Rationing - price allocates the limited tickets among competing buyers"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-markets"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-MKT-03","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-MARKETS","stem":"How can a higher market price act as an incentive to producers?","options":{"A":"It forces consumers to buy more of the good","B":"It removes the firm's production costs entirely","C":"It may increase potential profit, encouraging firms to supply more","D":"It guarantees the firm a profit whatever it chooses to produce and sell"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-PRICE-FUNC","scaffold":"The price mechanism can encourage suppliers when higher prices make production more profitable. The incentive is not a guarantee: costs, competition and the eventual response of other firms still matter.","reforge":{"stem":"The price of strawberries rises after a poor harvest. What incentive might this create for growers next season?","options":{"A":"To consider planting more strawberries if expected returns justify the cost","B":"To stop responding to information coming from the market","C":"To make consumers automatically demand fewer strawberries in the following season","D":"To reduce all investment in growing strawberries"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-markets"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-MKT-04","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-MARKETS","stem":"Which option correctly compares factor and product markets?","options":{"A":"Firms sell their own labour in the product markets, and households buy resources there too","B":"Product markets exist only online and nowhere else","C":"Factor markets sell only food and nothing else","D":"Households buy final goods; firms and households exchange resources in factor markets."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-MARKET-DEF","scaffold":"Product markets involve final goods and services. Factor markets involve the resources used to produce them, including labour, land and capital. Households can supply factors and demand products; firms do the opposite.","reforge":{"stem":"A company pays rent for a warehouse and wages to its employees. These payments are made in:","options":{"A":"A product market and nothing else","B":"Factor markets, because property and labour are productive resources","C":"A market in which there is no economic cost to anyone involved at all","D":"A market used only by consumers"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-markets"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-MKT-05","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-MARKETS","stem":"What is a likely strength of allowing prices to be determined by demand and supply?","options":{"A":"Prices can communicate changing shortages and surpluses","B":"No consumer ever faces a rise in price","C":"The government knows every consumer's preference","D":"The market always produces a socially fair outcome for everyone"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-PRICE-FUNC","scaffold":"The price mechanism coordinates dispersed information and can respond to changing conditions. It does not guarantee fairness or prevent price rises, and it may fail where external costs or benefits are ignored.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might a free market outcome be criticised as unfair?","options":{"A":"Free markets always provide far too much public transport in every region of the country, at all times","B":"Prices never change when the level of demand changes","C":"Prices may ration goods according to ability to pay, so low-income households may be excluded","D":"Firms cannot respond to price incentives at all"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-markets"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-MKT-06","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-MARKETS","stem":"A government charges 10p for each single-use carrier bag. If the main purpose is to encourage reusable bags, the charge is acting mainly as:","options":{"A":"A subsidy paid to producers of plastic bags","B":"A signal and incentive to change behaviour","C":"A guarantee of free bags for shoppers","D":"A fixed cost faced by every retailer"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-PRICE-FUNC","scaffold":"A small charge can signal the environmental cost of the choice and create an incentive to avoid it. The policy may also raise revenue, but its behavioural purpose is central.","reforge":{"stem":"A congestion charge makes driving into a city centre more expensive. Its intended price-mechanism effect is to:","options":{"A":"Make the amount of road space unlimited","B":"Increase the demand for driving into the city at every possible price","C":"Remove the need for any public transport at all","D":"Signal and discourage the use of scarce road space at busy times"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-markets"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-MKT-07","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-MARKETS","stem":"At a price below equilibrium, what is likely to occur in a competitive market?","options":{"A":"A surplus, because sellers offer far more than buyers want to buy","B":"No change, because price has no role in the market","C":"A shortage because quantity demanded exceeds quantity supplied","D":"A fall in demand caused by the lower price"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-DISEQ","scaffold":"Below equilibrium, more buyers want the product than firms are willing to supply. This excess demand creates upward pressure on price, moving the market towards equilibrium unless a control prevents adjustment.","reforge":{"stem":"At a price above equilibrium, which outcome is most likely?","options":{"A":"A surplus, with quantity supplied greater than quantity demanded","B":"A shortage of inputs and nothing more","C":"An automatic increase in the incomes of all consumers in the market","D":"Excess demand, with queues forming for the good"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-markets"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-MKT-08","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-MARKETS","stem":"Why may a government intervene in a market instead of relying entirely on the price mechanism?","options":{"A":"Prices never convey any information to buyers","B":"Consumers have no influence at all on the level of demand","C":"Every market in the economy has exactly one seller operating within it at any time","D":"Markets can create external costs or fail to provide socially important goods"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-INTERVENTION","scaffold":"Market failure occurs when private decisions do not produce the best outcome for society, for example pollution from production or under-provision of merit goods. Intervention may help, although government failure is also possible.","reforge":{"stem":"Pollution from a factory harms nearby residents who are not involved in the sale. This is an example of:","options":{"A":"A positive externality of production","B":"A negative externality and possible market failure","C":"A factor market clearing at the going price","D":"A benefit that is received only by the producer of the good"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-markets"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-MKT-09","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-MARKETS","stem":"Which feature is most associated with an oligopoly?","options":{"A":"A few large firms hold much of the market","B":"Many tiny firms with no influence on price","C":"One firm faces no possible rivals at all","D":"The government supplies every product in the market"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-MARKET-STRUCT","scaffold":"An oligopoly has a small number of large firms whose decisions are interdependent. Firms may compete on price, quality or branding, while barriers to entry can protect their market shares.","reforge":{"stem":"Which comparison between an oligopoly and a competitive market is accurate?","options":{"A":"Firms in competition always set exactly identical prices to each other in every market","B":"Both markets must contain exactly one seller","C":"An oligopoly has fewer major firms, while competition usually involves more rivals","D":"Oligopolies cannot use any form of non-price competition"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-markets"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-MKT-10","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-MARKETS","stem":"Which is an example of a product market?","options":{"A":"A firm hiring a cleaner","B":"Consumers buying train tickets","C":"A household renting out its land","D":"Workers offering their labour to firms"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-MARKET-DEF","scaffold":"Product markets are where final goods and services are bought and sold. Factor markets trade resources used in production, such as labour, land and capital.","reforge":{"stem":"A family buys a meal from a restaurant. This transaction takes place in:","options":{"A":"A capital market","B":"A land market","C":"A labour market","D":"A product market"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-markets"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-MKT-11","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-MARKETS","stem":"A price rises when a good becomes scarce. Which function of the price mechanism is shown most directly?","options":{"A":"Removing all inequality between buyers","B":"Making the good no longer scarce at all for buyers","C":"Rationing the limited good among buyers","D":"Guaranteeing a lower price for everyone"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-PRICE-FUNC","scaffold":"A higher price can ration a scarce product by reducing quantity demanded and allocating the remaining supply among buyers willing and able to pay. It may also signal scarcity and encourage suppliers.","reforge":{"stem":"A rise in the price of concert tickets means some fans cannot buy one. This is the price mechanism acting as:","options":{"A":"A rationing device for scarce tickets","B":"A signal only, with no effect on buyers","C":"A subsidy to consumers","D":"A guarantee of equal access"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-markets"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-MKT-12","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-MARKETS","stem":"What does the price mechanism signal to producers when demand for a product rises?","options":{"A":"That the product has become less valuable","B":"That consumers no longer want substitutes","C":"That supply must immediately become zero","D":"That potential returns may have increased"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-PRICE-FUNC","scaffold":"A higher price can signal stronger demand and create an incentive for firms to supply more, provided they expect the extra revenue to cover extra costs. The signal is not a guaranteed profit.","reforge":{"stem":"A rise in demand for gym memberships causes prices to increase. What incentive might this create for suppliers?","options":{"A":"Reduce every service that the firm offers down to a lower quality","B":"Consider expanding capacity if expected returns cover costs","C":"Stop monitoring the level of consumer demand","D":"Assume that profit is guaranteed in future"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-markets"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-MKT-13","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-MARKETS","stem":"A factory imposes noise costs on nearby households that do not buy its product. This is a:","options":{"A":"Negative externality","B":"Positive externality","C":"Private benefit only","D":"Factor payment"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-EXTERNALITIES","scaffold":"A negative externality is a cost imposed on third parties outside the market transaction. Because the producer and buyer may ignore that cost, the market can produce more than is socially efficient.","reforge":{"stem":"A firm provides training that makes workers more productive for other employers too. This could be a:","options":{"A":"Negative externality","B":"Budget deficit","C":"Positive externality","D":"Movement along supply"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-markets"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-MKT-14","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-MARKETS","stem":"Why might the government support consumption of a merit good such as education?","options":{"A":"It is impossible for the good to be produced privately at all","B":"It creates benefits for others as well as the consumer","C":"It always creates a private loss for the consumer","D":"It removes scarcity from the whole economy"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-MERIT-GOODS","scaffold":"Merit goods create wider benefits that consumers may undervalue, such as a more skilled workforce from education. Without intervention, consumption may be below the socially efficient level.","reforge":{"stem":"Why may vaccination be under-consumed in an unregulated market?","options":{"A":"It creates no private benefit for the person treated","B":"It has no effect at all on the general level of public health","C":"It is always classed as a demerit good","D":"People may not consider all the protection given to others"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-markets"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-MKT-15","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-MARKETS","stem":"A maximum price below equilibrium is enforced in a rental market. What is the likely result?","options":{"A":"A surplus of homes","B":"A guaranteed rise in supply","C":"A shortage of homes","D":"No effect on either buyers or sellers"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-DISEQ","scaffold":"A binding maximum price is below equilibrium. It increases quantity demanded and reduces quantity supplied, creating excess demand or a shortage. Queues or other non-price rationing may result.","reforge":{"stem":"A legal minimum price is set above equilibrium for farm produce. What is likely?","options":{"A":"A surplus because sellers offer more than buyers want","B":"A shortage, because buyers want more than is offered","C":"No change, because the price floor is already binding here","D":"Demand must shift to the right"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-markets"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-MKT-16","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-MARKETS","stem":"Why can the price mechanism fail to produce the best outcome for society?","options":{"A":"Prices never respond to a change in demand","B":"Firms cannot receive any information from the market","C":"Consumers always have perfect information about prices and incomes","D":"Prices can ignore costs or benefits affecting third parties"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-EXTERNALITIES","scaffold":"Market failure can occur when external costs or benefits are not reflected in the market price, or when goods with wider social benefits are under-consumed. Prices still perform useful information and incentive functions, but not perfectly in every market.","reforge":{"stem":"Which is a reason a free market may under-provide a good with social benefits?","options":{"A":"Consumers receive no private benefit from it at all","B":"The wider benefits are not fully considered in private decisions","C":"The good is not scarce in any way","D":"Prices always include every external benefit that the good creates"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-markets"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-MKT-17","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-MARKETS","stem":"In a market economy, scarce resources are mainly allocated by:","options":{"A":"Supply and demand","B":"A national production target","C":"A fixed wage for every worker","D":"A ban on private exchange"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-MARKET-DEF","scaffold":"A market economy uses market forces, especially supply and demand, to influence prices and allocate scarce resources.","reforge":{"stem":"What are market forces?","options":{"A":"Government orders that fix the level of output and the price charged for every single good","B":"A firm's own internal accounts","C":"Factors influencing prices and availability without direct government price setting","D":"A list of the public services provided"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-markets"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-MKT-18","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-MARKETS","stem":"At equilibrium in a market:","options":{"A":"Demand is always greater than supply in a market","B":"Quantity supplied equals quantity demanded","C":"No one is willing to trade at all","D":"The government has fixed the market price"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-EQUILIBRIUM","scaffold":"Equilibrium price and quantity occur where quantity supplied exactly matches quantity demanded. There is no excess demand or excess supply at that point.","reforge":{"stem":"What does allocation of resources describe?","options":{"A":"How firms advertise their products to consumers","B":"How prices are printed onto product labels","C":"How a bank calculates its interest","D":"How scarce resources and goods are distributed"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-markets"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-MKT-19","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-MARKETS","stem":"What is meant by price?","options":{"A":"The quantity that a firm produces","B":"A firm's total sales revenue","C":"The money paid for a good or service","D":"The number of firms operating in a market"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-MARKET-DEF","scaffold":"Price is the sum of money paid for a good or service. In a free market, its level is influenced by the interaction of supply and demand.","reforge":{"stem":"What is exchange?","options":{"A":"Giving something up to receive something wanted","B":"A fall in the level of market demand","C":"A government subsidy to firms","D":"Producing goods and then not selling any of them"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-markets"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-MKT-20","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-MARKETS","stem":"What does economic efficiency involve?","options":{"A":"Every firm in the market charging exactly the same price as the others","B":"Producing output without using any labour","C":"Removing all opportunity costs from every choice","D":"The optimal production and distribution of scarce resources"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-MARKET-DEF","scaffold":"Efficiency concerns using scarce resources in an optimal way, including how goods are produced and distributed. It does not mean every price or outcome is identical.","reforge":{"stem":"Adam Smith's invisible hand is another way of describing:","options":{"A":"A firm's own production plan","B":"The operation of market forces","C":"A direct order issued by government","D":"A shortage of money in the economy"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-markets"}]},"GCSE-ECON-P1-DS":{"label":"Paper 1: Demand, Supply & Price","color":"#16a34a","questions":[{"id":"GCSE-P1-DS-01","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-DS","stem":"The price of coffee rises and consumers buy less coffee, with no other change. What is this called?","options":{"A":"A contraction in quantity demanded - movement along the existing demand curve","B":"A decrease in demand","C":"An increase in supply","D":"Government spending on public services replacing imported goods with domestically produced ones"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-SHIFT-VS-MOVE","scaffold":"A price change causes a movement along a demand curve, changing quantity demanded. A decrease in demand means the whole curve shifts left because a non-price determinant changes, such as income, tastes or the price of a related good.","reforge":{"stem":"Household incomes fall and fewer restaurant meals are wanted at every possible price. This is:","options":{"A":"An extension of demand","B":"The theatre can add unlimited seats when price rises","C":"A decrease in demand - the demand curve shifts left","D":"A shift of supply right"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-ds"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-DS-02","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-DS","stem":"Which change would shift the demand curve for bus travel to the right?","options":{"A":"A fall in the price of bus tickets","B":"A rise in the price of train travel, a substitute","C":"The theatre can add unlimited seats when price rises","D":"A fall in the number of bus routes"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-DS-DETERMINANTS","scaffold":"A rise in the price of a substitute makes bus travel relatively more attractive, increasing demand for buses at each price. Ticket price itself causes movement along the curve; operator costs affect supply.","reforge":{"stem":"If cars and petrol are complements, what is likely to happen to demand for petrol when the price of cars rises sharply?","options":{"A":"Demand for petrol rises","B":"To ensure that every product in the shop is sold below cost forever","C":"There is no relationship","D":"Demand for petrol falls because fewer cars are bought or used"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-ds"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-DS-03","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-DS","stem":"Which change is most likely to shift the supply curve for bread to the left?","options":{"A":"Cheaper flour","B":"Improved oven technology","C":"A rise in baker wages and energy costs","D":"Have no possible effect on petrol demand"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-DS-DETERMINANTS","scaffold":"Higher costs reduce the quantity firms are willing and able to supply at each price, shifting supply left. Lower input costs, better technology or more firms would tend to shift supply right.","reforge":{"stem":"A government subsidy lowers the cost of producing solar panels. What happens to market supply, other things equal?","options":{"A":"Supply shifts right","B":"Quantity demanded falls along demand","C":"Demand becomes perfectly inelastic","D":"Supply shifts left"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-ds"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-DS-04","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-DS","stem":"The equilibrium price in a market is the price at which:","options":{"A":"Demand is always greater than supply in a market","B":"The government has fixed the price","C":"Firms make the highest possible profit","D":"Quantity demanded equals quantity supplied"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-EQUILIBRIUM","scaffold":"Equilibrium is the market-clearing position where planned purchases equal planned sales. It does not necessarily maximise profit or produce a fair distribution.","reforge":{"stem":"If demand increases while supply is unchanged, what is the usual new equilibrium effect?","options":{"A":"Lower price and lower quantity","B":"Higher price and higher quantity","C":"Higher price and lower quantity","D":"No change in either price or quantity"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-ds"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-DS-05","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-DS","stem":"A shop sells 2,000 notebooks at £4 each. Its total revenue is:","options":{"A":"£8,000","B":"£500","C":"£2,000","D":"£8"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-REV-COST-PROFIT","scaffold":"Total revenue equals price multiplied by quantity sold: £4 x 2,000 = £8,000. Revenue is not the same as profit because costs have not yet been subtracted.","reforge":{"stem":"A price falls from £5 to £4 and quantity sold rises from 1,000 to 1,400 units. What happens to total revenue?","options":{"A":"It stays at £5,000","B":"It falls from £5,000 to £4,000","C":"It rises from £5,000 to £5,600","D":"It cannot be calculated"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-ds"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-DS-06","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-DS","stem":"The price of a product rises by 10% and quantity demanded falls by 2%. The demand is:","options":{"A":"Perfectly mobile construction resources","B":"Price inelastic, with PED of -0.2","C":"Unit elastic, with PED of 1","D":"Perfectly elastic"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-ELASTICITY","scaffold":"PED = percentage change in quantity demanded divided by percentage change in price: -2% / 10% = -0.2. The absolute value is below 1, so demand is price inelastic. Because quantity changes proportionally less than price, revenue would tend to rise after the price increase.","reforge":{"stem":"Demand has a PED of -2. Which description is correct?","options":{"A":"It is price inelastic","B":"Demand does not respond to price","C":"Government departments are required to wait a full decade before changing any spending","D":"It is price elastic: quantity demanded responds proportionally more than price"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-ds"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-DS-07","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-DS","stem":"Which factor is likely to make the supply of new houses price inelastic in the short run?","options":{"A":"Houses can be built instantly","B":"Large stocks of completed houses","C":"Planning restrictions and long construction times","D":"Working behind the cafe counter for an hourly wage"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-ELASTICITY","scaffold":"Low PES means quantity supplied changes by a smaller percentage than price. Construction takes time, land is limited and planning rules can restrict rapid expansion, so supply is relatively inelastic in the short run.","reforge":{"stem":"A theatre has only 500 seats for tonight's performance. Why is seat supply likely to be perfectly inelastic for tonight?","options":{"A":"The number of seats is fixed, so a higher price cannot increase tonight's capacity","B":"Demand is always inelastic","C":"The seats are a substitute for tickets","D":"To raise the price so that consumption falls to the level the market originally chose"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-ds"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-DS-08","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-DS","stem":"A maximum price is set below the equilibrium price for rented homes. What is the likely result if it is effective?","options":{"A":"A surplus of homes","B":"No effect on the market","C":"A surplus, because sellers offer far more than buyers want to buy","D":"A shortage because quantity demanded exceeds quantity supplied"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-DISEQ","scaffold":"A binding maximum price keeps rent below the market-clearing level. More people want homes, while landlords may supply fewer, creating excess demand. Non-price rationing such as queues may follow.","reforge":{"stem":"A minimum price for farm produce is set above equilibrium and enforced. What is most likely?","options":{"A":"A shortage of produce","B":"A surplus because quantity supplied exceeds quantity demanded","C":"An automatic increase in the incomes of all consumers in the market","D":"A shift in demand left only"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-ds"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-DS-09","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-DS","stem":"The price of train travel rises. If bus travel is a substitute, what is likely to happen to demand for bus travel?","options":{"A":"It shifts right","B":"It shifts left","C":"It becomes supply","D":"It is unchanged by related prices"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-DS-DETERMINANTS","scaffold":"When a substitute becomes more expensive, consumers may switch towards the alternative. This raises demand for bus travel at each price, shifting its demand curve right rather than causing movement along it.","reforge":{"stem":"Cars and petrol are complements. A sharp rise in car prices is likely to:","options":{"A":"Raise supply of petrol","B":"Raise demand for petrol","C":"Lower demand for petrol","D":"Have no possible effect on petrol demand"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-ds"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-DS-10","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-DS","stem":"Which change would shift the supply curve for bread to the left?","options":{"A":"Cheaper flour","B":"Higher energy costs","C":"Better ovens","D":"More bakeries entering"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-DS-DETERMINANTS","scaffold":"Higher production costs reduce the quantity firms are willing and able to supply at each price, shifting supply left. Lower input costs, improved technology and more firms usually shift supply right.","reforge":{"stem":"A subsidy lowers the cost of producing solar panels. What is the likely supply effect?","options":{"A":"Quantity demanded falls along demand","B":"Supply shifts left","C":"Demand shifts left","D":"Supply shifts right"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-ds"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-DS-11","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-DS","stem":"Price rises by 10% and quantity demanded falls by 30%. What is the PED classification?","options":{"A":"Unit elastic","B":"Price inelastic","C":"Price elastic","D":"Perfectly inelastic"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-ELASTICITY","scaffold":"PED is the percentage change in quantity demanded divided by the percentage change in price. The absolute value here is 30% divided by 10% = 3, so demand is price elastic because quantity responds proportionally more than price.","reforge":{"stem":"A product has PED of -0.4. Which statement is correct?","options":{"A":"Demand is price inelastic","B":"Demand is price elastic","C":"Demand is perfectly elastic","D":"Quantity never responds to price"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-ds"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-DS-12","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-DS","stem":"Why is the short-run supply of seats at tonight's concert likely to be price inelastic?","options":{"A":"Quantity demanded changes by less than price","B":"Ticket prices cannot change","C":"Demand is fixed at every price","D":"The number of seats is fixed for tonight"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-ELASTICITY","scaffold":"Price inelastic supply means quantity supplied changes by a smaller percentage than price. The venue's capacity is fixed for tonight, so a higher price cannot quickly create many more seats.","reforge":{"stem":"Which factor is likely to make new housing supply price inelastic in the short run?","options":{"A":"Unlimited land and instant construction","B":"Long building times and planning limits","C":"A fall in household income","D":"A rise in the price of substitutes"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-ds"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-DS-13","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-DS","stem":"Why might oil price changes affect the price of petrol?","options":{"A":"Oil is a raw material used to produce petrol","B":"Petrol is a factor of production for oil","C":"No change because the price is below equilibrium","D":"Oil prices only affect wages"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-DS-DETERMINANTS","scaffold":"Oil is an input into petrol production, so a change in oil prices can alter refiners' costs and shift the supply of petrol. Oil and petrol are related but not identical goods.","reforge":{"stem":"If the supply of crude oil falls, the short-run effect on petrol supply is likely to be:","options":{"A":"A benefit that is received only by the producer of the good","B":"No effect because petrol is a service","C":"A leftward shift, putting upward pressure on petrol prices","D":"A rise in demand for every other good"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-ds"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-DS-14","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-DS","stem":"At equilibrium in a market, which relationship holds?","options":{"A":"Price is always at its legal maximum","B":"Quantity demanded equals quantity supplied","C":"Firms make no profit","D":"Every buyer receives an identical quantity"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-EQUILIBRIUM","scaffold":"Market equilibrium is where planned quantity demanded equals planned quantity supplied. It does not guarantee equal access, zero profit or the absence of government regulation.","reforge":{"stem":"Demand for a product rises while supply is unchanged. The usual new equilibrium is:","options":{"A":"A lower price and lower quantity","B":"The government has fixed the market price","C":"The same price and quantity","D":"A higher price and higher quantity"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-ds"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-DS-15","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-DS","stem":"A firm has revenue of £40,000 and total costs of £31,000. What is its profit?","options":{"A":"£71,000","B":"£31,000","C":"£9,000","D":"£40,000"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-REV-COST-PROFIT","scaffold":"Profit is total revenue minus total cost: £40,000 - £31,000 = £9,000. Revenue is the money from sales; it is not the same as profit because costs must be deducted.","reforge":{"stem":"A business's revenue is £52,000 and its costs are £47,000. Its profit is:","options":{"A":"£5,000","B":"£52,000","C":"£47,000","D":"£99,000"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-ds"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-DS-16","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-DS","stem":"A rise in demand causes a movement up an unchanged supply curve. What usually happens?","options":{"A":"Supply shifts left","B":"Price falls and quantity supplied rises","C":"Demand becomes perfectly inelastic","D":"Price and quantity supplied rise"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-SHIFT-VS-MOVE","scaffold":"A rightward shift in demand raises the equilibrium price and quantity. The higher price causes an extension of quantity supplied, which is a movement along the existing supply curve rather than a shift of supply.","reforge":{"stem":"Which change would cause a movement along, rather than a shift of, a supply curve?","options":{"A":"A change in wage costs","B":"A change in the product's own price","C":"A contraction caused by a higher meal price","D":"A subsidy to producers"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-ds"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-DS-17","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-DS","stem":"Which option best defines demand?","options":{"A":"Willingness and ability to buy at a stated price and time","B":"The amount firms produce","C":"A consumer's income only","D":"No change, because the price floor is already binding here"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-SHIFT-VS-MOVE","scaffold":"Demand requires both willingness and ability to purchase. It refers to a particular price and time period, not just a desire for a product.","reforge":{"stem":"What is individual demand?","options":{"A":"Demand from all consumers in a country","B":"The amount supplied by one firm","C":"Demand for a good by one consumer","D":"The price chosen by a government"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-ds"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-DS-18","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-DS","stem":"The law of demand states that, for most products:","options":{"A":"Oligopolies cannot use any form of non-price competition","B":"Quantity demanded usually varies inversely with price","C":"Demand never changes","D":"Supply determines every purchase"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-SHIFT-VS-MOVE","scaffold":"The law of demand describes an inverse relationship: as price rises, quantity demanded generally falls, other things equal.","reforge":{"stem":"What is a movement along a demand curve caused by?","options":{"A":"A change in population","B":"A change in advertising","C":"Price falls and quantity supplied rises","D":"A change in the product's own price"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-ds"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-DS-19","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-DS","stem":"What does a shift of the demand curve mean?","options":{"A":"A barrier that prevents any new firm from entering into this market","B":"A change in quantity supplied only","C":"The whole demand curve moves because a non-price factor changes","D":"The market reaches equilibrium"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-SHIFT-VS-MOVE","scaffold":"A shift is a complete movement of the existing demand curve. It can result from factors such as income, tastes, population or the price of related goods.","reforge":{"stem":"What is a subsidy?","options":{"A":"Government money given directly to firms to encourage production or consumption","B":"A firm's total cost","C":"A price charged to a producer","D":"A policy that controls only the level of imports coming into the country each year"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-ds"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-DS-20","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-DS","stem":"Price elasticity of demand measures the responsiveness of:","options":{"A":"Supply to a change in wages","B":"Quantity supplied to a change in income","C":"Total revenue to a change in tax","D":"Quantity demanded to a change in price"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-ELASTICITY","scaffold":"PED measures how responsive quantity demanded is to a change in the product's price. Elasticity compares percentage changes rather than absolute changes.","reforge":{"stem":"When is demand elastic?","options":{"A":"The rate of pay that a firm gives to each of its own workers for every hour worked","B":"The percentage change in quantity demanded exceeds the percentage change in price","C":"Price never changes","D":"Demand is unrelated to price"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-ds"}]},"GCSE-ECON-P1-COMP":{"label":"Paper 1: Competition","color":"#22c55e","questions":[{"id":"GCSE-P1-CMP-01","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-COMP","stem":"Which is a barrier to entry into the pharmaceutical industry?","options":{"A":"A patent protecting a new medicine","B":"A large number of potential customers","C":"Low start-up costs","D":"Easy access to identical technology"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-BARRIERS","scaffold":"A patent gives the holder legal protection from direct copying for a period, making entry harder. Other barriers include large capital requirements, economies of scale, strong brands and access to specialist skills.","reforge":{"stem":"Why can economies of scale be a barrier to entry for a new supermarket?","options":{"A":"Economies of scale increase every new entrant's costs by exactly the same amount each year","B":"Small firms automatically receive bigger discounts from their suppliers","C":"An established large chain may have lower average costs and can price aggressively","D":"They prevent consumers from comparing prices between the rival stores"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-comp"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-CMP-02","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-COMP","stem":"Which is an example of non-price competition?","options":{"A":"Cutting the selling price charged to customers","B":"Offering a stronger after-sales service","C":"Reducing the tax charged on a product","D":"Lowering the firm's total wage bill"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-NONPRICE-COMP","scaffold":"Non-price competition tries to attract customers through quality, branding, advertising, loyalty schemes, design or service rather than a lower price. Firms may use it to avoid a damaging price war.","reforge":{"stem":"A supermarket launches a loyalty app with personalised offers but leaves shelf prices unchanged. This is mainly:","options":{"A":"A price ceiling","B":"A fall in supply","C":"A barrier caused by a patent","D":"Non-price competition"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-comp"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-CMP-03","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-COMP","stem":"What is a likely benefit to consumers of more competition between firms?","options":{"A":"Less choice for consumers and a weaker standard of service overall","B":"Guaranteed identical products from every supplier","C":"Greater pressure on firms to keep prices low or improve quality","D":"No need for any firm in the market to innovate"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-MARKET-STRUCT","scaffold":"Competition can benefit consumers through lower prices, more choice, improved quality and innovation. The size of these benefits depends on how easily consumers can switch and how many effective rivals exist.","reforge":{"stem":"If several firms compete to supply broadband, which consumer benefit is most likely?","options":{"A":"Consumers may gain from better service, choice or lower prices","B":"All firms must charge exactly the same price","C":"The market becomes a monopoly almost immediately","D":"Firms face much less pressure to improve their broadband speeds now"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-comp"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-CMP-04","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-COMP","stem":"A market has one dominant seller and very high barriers to entry. Which concern is most relevant?","options":{"A":"Consumers will always receive far too much choice in the market as a result","B":"Supply in the market must be perfectly elastic","C":"The firm cannot earn any revenue at all","D":"The firm may have less pressure to control prices or improve quality"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-BARRIERS","scaffold":"A monopoly or dominant firm may gain market power. It could exploit consumers through higher prices, restrict output or become X-inefficient, although large scale and investment may create some benefits.","reforge":{"stem":"Which is a possible benefit of a natural monopoly in a network industry such as water?","options":{"A":"A monopoly can never invest in its own network","B":"Duplicating pipes by many firms may be wasteful, so one network can reduce average cost","C":"Consumers automatically pay the lowest possible price for their water supply in every case","D":"There are no fixed costs in supplying water"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-comp"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-CMP-05","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-COMP","stem":"A firm has 40% of sales in a market. What does this figure measure?","options":{"A":"Its market share","B":"Its total revenue","C":"Its price elasticity","D":"Its average cost"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-MARKET-STRUCT","scaffold":"Market share is a firm's sales as a percentage of total market sales, measured by value or volume. A high share may indicate market power but does not by itself prove that competition is weak.","reforge":{"stem":"A market's four largest firms account for 80% of sales. This suggests:","options":{"A":"Perfect competition between many firms","B":"A market that has no barriers to entry at all","C":"A highly concentrated market with a small number of major firms","D":"That every firm in the market has exactly the same share of sales"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-comp"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-CMP-06","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-COMP","stem":"Why might firms use a loss-leading strategy?","options":{"A":"To ensure that every product in the shop is sold below cost forever","B":"To attract customers who may also buy profitable products","C":"To remove all of the firm's fixed costs","D":"To reduce the size of the market they serve"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-NONPRICE-COMP","scaffold":"A loss leader is sold at a very low price, sometimes below cost, to attract customers and increase sales of other items. Its success depends on the extra purchases and whether competitors respond.","reforge":{"stem":"A cinema offers very cheap tickets but charges high prices for drinks. The ticket is most likely acting as:","options":{"A":"A fixed cost of running the shop","B":"A barrier that prevents any new firm from entering into this market","C":"A supply-side tax on the product","D":"A loss leader to attract customers who may buy other products"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-comp"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-CMP-07","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-COMP","stem":"Which is most likely to increase competition in a market?","options":{"A":"Granting one firm an exclusive licence to operate","B":"Making every patent permanent","C":"Removing unnecessary restrictions that stop new firms entering","D":"Increasing the switching costs that consumers face when they move"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-BARRIERS","scaffold":"Lowering avoidable barriers can make entry easier and increase the number of rivals. Competition policy may also prevent anti-competitive mergers or abuse of market power.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might a regulator block a merger between the two largest firms in a market?","options":{"A":"It could reduce rivalry and allow the merged firm to raise prices or restrict choice","B":"It would make entry into the market much easier for every small firm that wants to join","C":"Mergers cannot affect market power in any way","D":"It would guarantee lower prices for every consumer"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-comp"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-CMP-08","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-COMP","stem":"A firm spends heavily on branding while keeping its price unchanged. What might the branding achieve?","options":{"A":"It makes the firm's product identical to that of every rival in the market","B":"It prevents consumers making any choice at all","C":"It removes the firm's variable costs completely","D":"It can differentiate the product and make demand less sensitive to price"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-NONPRICE-COMP","scaffold":"Branding and product differentiation can create loyalty and make substitutes seem less close. This may give the firm some pricing power, although advertising is costly and competitors can imitate it.","reforge":{"stem":"Which outcome is a possible drawback of intense advertising competition?","options":{"A":"It always lowers the firm's average costs","B":"It can raise firms' costs and may persuade consumers without improving the product","C":"It guarantees perfect information for every consumer","D":"It removes any need for the firm to offer product quality, because buyers stop caring"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-comp"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-CMP-09","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-COMP","stem":"Which feature makes an oligopoly different from a highly competitive market?","options":{"A":"A small number of firms hold substantial market shares","B":"Firms have no influence over each other's decisions","C":"There are no barriers to entry at all","D":"The market has no product differentiation between the firms"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-MARKET-STRUCT","scaffold":"Oligopoly is a market structure dominated by a few large firms. Their decisions are interdependent, so each firm considers how rivals may respond to changes in price, output or promotion.","reforge":{"stem":"A market is dominated by four firms that watch each other's pricing closely. This is most consistent with:","options":{"A":"Monopolistic competition only","B":"Perfect competition","C":"An oligopoly","D":"A factor market"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-comp"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-CMP-10","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-COMP","stem":"Which is a barrier to entry into a market?","options":{"A":"Low start-up costs for a new firm","B":"A legal patent protecting an existing product","C":"Many potential customers already active in the market","D":"Easy access to established suppliers"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-BARRIERS","scaffold":"Patents can prevent rivals copying a product for a period, creating a legal barrier to entry. Other barriers include high capital needs, economies of scale and strong established brands.","reforge":{"stem":"Why can large economies of scale discourage new firms from entering?","options":{"A":"They guarantee that any new firm will win a large share of the market","B":"They remove the need for any start-up capital","C":"They make all firms in the market equally efficient","D":"New firms may face higher average costs than established producers"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-comp"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-CMP-11","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-COMP","stem":"A single dominant firm faces high barriers to entry. What is a possible concern?","options":{"A":"It cannot earn any revenue at all","B":"It has no reason to choose any output level","C":"It may face less pressure to keep prices low","D":"It must have a perfectly elastic supply curve always"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-BARRIERS","scaffold":"Market power may allow a dominant firm to raise prices, restrict output or reduce quality. However, large scale can also lower costs or fund investment, so evaluation depends on the market and regulation.","reforge":{"stem":"Which is a possible benefit of a natural monopoly in a network industry?","options":{"A":"One network may avoid wasteful duplication of infrastructure","B":"The firm has no fixed costs to cover","C":"Customers must always end up paying the lowest possible price available","D":"Entry barriers become completely irrelevant"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-comp"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-CMP-12","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-COMP","stem":"A supermarket improves its loyalty scheme but leaves shelf prices unchanged. This is:","options":{"A":"Price competition","B":"A fall in market supply","C":"A price ceiling","D":"Non-price competition"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-NONPRICE-COMP","scaffold":"Non-price competition uses service, branding, quality, design or loyalty rewards to attract customers without directly lowering the selling price. It is common where firms want to avoid a price war.","reforge":{"stem":"Which is an example of non-price competition by a hotel?","options":{"A":"Offering a lower nightly price for each room that is booked","B":"Providing a stronger breakfast and loyalty service","C":"Reducing the hotel's tax bill","D":"Buying fewer inputs from suppliers"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-comp"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-CMP-13","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-COMP","stem":"What is a likely benefit to consumers when several firms compete?","options":{"A":"More choice and pressure for better quality","B":"Guaranteed equal incomes for all firms","C":"No need for any firm to innovate","D":"Higher prices in every part of the market concerned"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-MARKET-STRUCT","scaffold":"Competition can encourage firms to lower prices, improve quality, widen choice and innovate. The effect depends on the number of effective rivals and how easily consumers can switch.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might competition improve broadband services?","options":{"A":"Firms have much less reason to improve the service they offer","B":"Consumers must buy from one supplier only","C":"Rivals may compete through speed, reliability or price","D":"The market quickly becomes a monopoly"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-comp"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-CMP-14","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-COMP","stem":"Why might strong branding make demand for a firm's product less price elastic?","options":{"A":"Branding makes production costless for the firm","B":"Customers may see the branded product as less replaceable","C":"Branding removes all substitutes from the market","D":"Customers stop responding to every price change in the market entirely"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-NONPRICE-COMP","scaffold":"A trusted brand can create loyalty and make close substitutes seem less attractive. Demand may therefore respond less to price, although advertising costs money and rival brands can compete.","reforge":{"stem":"A branded product has many loyal customers. A likely effect is that its demand:","options":{"A":"Becomes far more sensitive to even the smallest changes in its own price","B":"Becomes perfectly elastic at every price","C":"No longer depends on the preferences of consumers","D":"Becomes less sensitive to price than an identical unbranded product"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-comp"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-CMP-15","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-COMP","stem":"A firm sells 30,000 units in a market with total sales of 100,000 units. Its market share is:","options":{"A":"3%","B":"300%","C":"30%","D":"70%"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-MARKET-STRUCT","scaffold":"Market share is the firm's sales divided by total market sales, multiplied by 100. Here, 30,000 divided by 100,000 equals 30%.","reforge":{"stem":"Four firms each hold 25% of a market. What does this suggest?","options":{"A":"The market is evenly divided among four firms","B":"The market is necessarily a pure monopoly","C":"The firms have no market share at all between them","D":"One firm supplies all of the output"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-comp"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-CMP-16","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-COMP","stem":"Why might a competition authority block a merger between the two largest firms?","options":{"A":"Mergers always reduce prices for consumers","B":"The firms would have no customers left","C":"Competition authorities prevent every merger from happening","D":"The merger could reduce rivalry and increase market power"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-BARRIERS","scaffold":"A merger between major rivals can increase concentration, reduce consumer choice and make it easier to raise prices. Authorities assess the likely effect rather than blocking every merger automatically.","reforge":{"stem":"A regulator requires a dominant firm to allow rivals access to its network. This aims to:","options":{"A":"Remove every fixed cost from the industry","B":"Reduce barriers and increase effective competition","C":"Guarantee the dominant firm a permanent monopoly position","D":"Stop consumers from changing their supplier"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-comp"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-CMP-17","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-COMP","stem":"What is competition between firms?","options":{"A":"Different firms trying to sell similar products to consumers","B":"One firm setting all of the rules in a market","C":"A government department buying goods for itself","D":"Consumers producing all of their own services at home themselves"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-MARKET-STRUCT","scaffold":"Competition exists when firms try to attract buyers for similar products. It can encourage lower prices, better quality and innovation.","reforge":{"stem":"What is a monopoly?","options":{"A":"A market containing many equal-sized rival firms overall","B":"A market that has no buyers at all","C":"A sole producer or seller of a good or service","D":"A government budget surplus this year"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-comp"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-CMP-18","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-COMP","stem":"What is an oligopoly?","options":{"A":"A market with no barriers to entry whatsoever","B":"A market where a small number of firms hold most market share","C":"A market with only one household as buyer","D":"A product that is supplied by the public sector and nobody else"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-MARKET-STRUCT","scaffold":"An oligopoly is dominated by a small number of firms. Their decisions may be interdependent because each firm watches its rivals.","reforge":{"stem":"Which market structure has one main seller?","options":{"A":"Perfect competition","B":"An oligopoly","C":"A factor market","D":"A monopoly"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-comp"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-CMP-19","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-COMP","stem":"Which feature distinguishes a monopoly from an oligopoly?","options":{"A":"A monopoly has both buyers and sellers in it, while an oligopoly has only sellers","B":"An oligopoly sells products while a monopoly does not","C":"A monopoly has one main producer, while an oligopoly has a few dominant firms","D":"An oligopoly cannot earn any revenue at all"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-MARKET-STRUCT","scaffold":"A monopoly has a sole producer or seller. An oligopoly has a small number of firms controlling most of the market.","reforge":{"stem":"Which description fits an oligopoly?","options":{"A":"A few firms control most market share","B":"No firms compete","C":"Only consumers make decisions","D":"One seller supplies the entire market"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-comp"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-CMP-20","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-COMP","stem":"What is consistent with competition?","options":{"A":"The government fixes every price charged in the market","B":"One firm is the only seller in the market","C":"A market that has no products in it","D":"Different firms seek sales from similar customers"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-MARKET-STRUCT","scaffold":"Competition means firms try to sell similar products to consumers. The number of competitors depends on the market structure.","reforge":{"stem":"Which situation is closest to a monopoly?","options":{"A":"Many firms sell differentiated products","B":"One producer is the sole seller of the product","C":"Six firms each have a broadly similar market share","D":"Households supply labour to many firms"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-comp"}]},"GCSE-ECON-P1-PROD":{"label":"Paper 1: Production","color":"#4ade80","questions":[{"id":"GCSE-P1-PRD-01","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-PROD","stem":"Which cost is most likely to be fixed for a small furniture manufacturer in the short run?","options":{"A":"Rent for the factory building","B":"Timber used for each table","C":"Electricity used by each machine","D":"Packaging for each item sold"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-REV-COST-PROFIT","scaffold":"Fixed costs do not change directly with output in the short run, such as rent or some insurance. Variable costs change as output changes, such as materials, packaging and power used in production.","reforge":{"stem":"Which is a variable cost for a bakery?","options":{"A":"Rent for the shop","B":"Annual building insurance","C":"Flour used in each batch of bread","D":"When total revenue equals total cost"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-prod"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-PRD-02","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-PROD","stem":"A firm has fixed costs of £6,000 and variable costs of £4,000 at an output of 2,000 units. What is its average cost per unit?","options":{"A":"£2","B":"£5","C":"£3","D":"£10"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-REV-COST-PROFIT","scaffold":"Total cost is fixed cost plus variable cost: £6,000 + £4,000 = £10,000. Average cost is total cost divided by output: £10,000 / 2,000 = £5 per unit.","reforge":{"stem":"Total cost is £18,000 at an output of 3,000 units. What is average cost?","options":{"A":"£5 per unit","B":"£15 per unit","C":"£54,000 per unit","D":"£6 per unit"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-prod"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-PRD-03","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-PROD","stem":"A business sells 500 units at £12 each and has total costs of £4,500. What is its profit?","options":{"A":"£4,500","B":"£6,000","C":"£1,500","D":"£10,500"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-REV-COST-PROFIT","scaffold":"Revenue is price multiplied by quantity: £12 x 500 = £6,000. Profit is total revenue minus total cost: £6,000 - £4,500 = £1,500.","reforge":{"stem":"A firm's revenue is £24,000 and total cost is £27,000. Which statement is correct?","options":{"A":"It makes a £3,000 loss","B":"It breaks even","C":"Its average cost is zero","D":"It makes a £3,000 profit"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-prod"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-PRD-04","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-PROD","stem":"A factory produces 1,200 units using 6 workers. What is labour productivity?","options":{"A":"6 units per worker","B":"7,200 units per worker","C":"1,206 units per worker","D":"200 units per worker"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-PRODUCTIVITY","scaffold":"Labour productivity is output per worker: 1,200 / 6 = 200 units per worker. Higher productivity means more output is produced for each unit of labour input.","reforge":{"stem":"Output rises from 900 to 1,200 units while the number of workers stays at 6. By how much does output per worker rise?","options":{"A":"25 units","B":"50 units","C":"150 units","D":"300 units"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-prod"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-PRD-05","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-PROD","stem":"Which change is most likely to raise labour productivity?","options":{"A":"Better worker training and improved technology","B":"Less training and older equipment","C":"More machine breakdowns","D":"Reducing output while keeping workers unchanged"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-PRODUCTIVITY","scaffold":"Training raises human capital and technology can allow each worker to produce more. Productivity may also improve through specialisation, motivation, better management or investment.","reforge":{"stem":"A firm introduces a safer, faster machine but keeps its workforce unchanged. The most likely immediate effect is:","options":{"A":"No possible change in output","B":"Lower labour productivity by definition","C":"Higher output per worker","D":"A shift in consumer demand only"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-prod"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-PRD-06","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-PROD","stem":"Which is a purchasing economy of scale?","options":{"A":"A firm hires a specialist manager","B":"A large firm buys raw materials in bulk at a lower price per unit","C":"A firm spreads risk across several products","D":"The fall in total output that follows a reduction in the workforce"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-EOS","scaffold":"Purchasing economies arise because large firms can negotiate bulk discounts. Other economies include technical, managerial, financial, marketing and risk-bearing economies.","reforge":{"stem":"A large company can borrow at a lower interest rate than a small start-up because lenders see it as safer. This is a:","options":{"A":"Diseconomy of scale","B":"Variable cost","C":"A firm borrows from a bank at a lower interest rate","D":"Financial economy of scale"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-prod"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-PRD-07","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-PROD","stem":"What is a likely diseconomy of scale?","options":{"A":"Bulk buying lowers input prices","B":"A bank offers a lower loan rate","C":"Communication problems make decisions slower as the organisation grows","D":"Customers must always end up paying the lowest possible price available"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-EOS","scaffold":"Diseconomies of scale occur when average costs rise as a firm becomes too large. Coordination, communication, motivation and control can become more difficult.","reforge":{"stem":"A company's average cost rises after it expands rapidly and managers struggle to coordinate many sites. This suggests:","options":{"A":"Diseconomies of scale","B":"A fall in fixed costs to zero","C":"Perfect competition","D":"A purchasing economy"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-prod"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-PRD-08","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-PROD","stem":"Why might a firm invest in employee wellbeing facilities even though they cost money?","options":{"A":"It guarantees every unemployed worker is matched within one month","B":"They reduce the need for any management","C":"They turn all costs into revenue","D":"They may improve morale, reduce absence and raise productivity"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-PRODUCTIVITY","scaffold":"Wellbeing can motivate workers and reduce sickness or staff turnover. The benefit is not automatic: the effect depends on whether the productivity gain is greater than the cost.","reforge":{"stem":"Which chain best explains how better working conditions could improve a firm's performance?","options":{"A":"Higher morale -> unlimited resources -> no costs","B":"Improved morale -> lower absence or higher effort -> higher output per worker","C":"The fall in average cost that happens purely by producing fewer units each year","D":"Higher wages -> no need for training ever"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-prod"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-PRD-09","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-PROD","stem":"A hotel trains staff to perform one specialised task each. What is a possible benefit?","options":{"A":"Workers may become quicker and more skilled at their task","B":"That every firm in the market has exactly the same share of sales","C":"Output cannot be measured","D":"Specialisation removes all costs"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-SPECIALISATION","scaffold":"Division of labour allows repetition and focused training, which may raise labour productivity. It can also create boredom or dependence, so the overall effect depends on the organisation of work.","reforge":{"stem":"Which is a possible drawback of narrow specialisation in a hotel?","options":{"A":"Specialisation always removes all training needs","B":"Each worker must become an entrepreneur","C":"Workers may become bored with repetitive duties","D":"Training can never be focused"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-prod"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-PRD-10","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-PROD","stem":"A firm has fixed costs of £6,000 and variable costs of £4,000. It produces 2,000 units. What is average cost?","options":{"A":"£2 per unit","B":"£5 per unit","C":"£4 per unit","D":"£10 per unit"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-REV-COST-PROFIT","scaffold":"Total cost is £6,000 plus £4,000, giving £10,000. Average cost is total cost divided by output: £10,000 divided by 2,000 equals £5 per unit.","reforge":{"stem":"Total cost is £18,000 for 3,000 units. Average cost is:","options":{"A":"£21 per unit","B":"£3 per unit","C":"£5 per unit","D":"£6 per unit"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-prod"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-PRD-11","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-PROD","stem":"A firm earns £40,000 revenue and pays £31,000 in total costs. What is its profit?","options":{"A":"£31,000","B":"£71,000","C":"£9,000","D":"£40,000"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-REV-COST-PROFIT","scaffold":"Profit is revenue minus total costs, so £40,000 minus £31,000 equals £9,000. Revenue is the value of sales before costs are deducted.","reforge":{"stem":"A firm has revenue of £52,000 and costs of £47,000. Its profit is:","options":{"A":"£5,000","B":"£47,000","C":"£52,000","D":"£99,000"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-prod"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-PRD-12","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-PROD","stem":"How can technical economies of scale lower a firm's average cost?","options":{"A":"Placing money into a savings account in order to earn interest","B":"The firm stops using capital","C":"Every worker performs every task","D":"Specialist machinery can spread fixed costs over more output"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-EOS","scaffold":"Technical economies can arise when large firms use specialised machinery efficiently. The fixed cost of that equipment is spread across a larger output, reducing average cost if utilisation is high.","reforge":{"stem":"Which is an example of a managerial economy of scale?","options":{"A":"A firm ends up paying much more for each and every input it buys","B":"A large firm employs specialist managers","C":"A firm loses all coordination between its separate departments","D":"A small shop buys one product"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-prod"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-PRD-13","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-PROD","stem":"What may cause diseconomies of scale as a firm becomes very large?","options":{"A":"Communication becomes slower and harder to coordinate","B":"Bulk buying lowers unit costs","C":"A firm ends up paying much more for each and every input it buys","D":"Loans become cheaper"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-EOS","scaffold":"Diseconomies of scale occur when average costs rise as the firm grows too large. Communication, coordination, control and motivation may become more difficult.","reforge":{"stem":"A business expands quickly and managers struggle to coordinate distant sites. This suggests:","options":{"A":"A purchasing economy","B":"A fall in average costs","C":"Diseconomies of scale","D":"Perfect competition"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-prod"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-PRD-14","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-PROD","stem":"Eight workers produce 1,200 units in a day. What is output per worker?","options":{"A":"8 units","B":"150 units","C":"96 units","D":"1,208 units"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-PRODUCTIVITY","scaffold":"Output per worker is total output divided by the number of workers: 1,200 divided by 8 equals 150 units per worker. This measures labour productivity rather than total production.","reforge":{"stem":"Output rises from 1,200 to 1,440 units while eight workers remain employed. Output per worker becomes:","options":{"A":"120 units","B":"240 units","C":"150 units","D":"180 units"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-prod"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-PRD-15","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-PROD","stem":"Why may specialisation improve a hotel's performance?","options":{"A":"Specialisation guarantees higher profit","B":"It makes the firm's product identical to that of every rival in the market","C":"Workers can build skill and complete tasks more efficiently","D":"The hotel can remove all fixed costs"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-SPECIALISATION","scaffold":"Specialisation may improve speed, skill and consistency, raising productivity and quality. It does not guarantee profit because training, supervision and loss of flexibility may add costs.","reforge":{"stem":"Which factor could limit the benefit of specialisation for hotel staff?","options":{"A":"Repetitive work may reduce motivation or flexibility","B":"Every customer receives a higher wage","C":"That every firm in the market has exactly the same share of sales","D":"The hotel stops needing managers"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-prod"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-PRD-16","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-PROD","stem":"Which cost is most likely variable for a bakery?","options":{"A":"Annual building insurance","B":"Monthly shop rent","C":"A fixed licence fee","D":"Flour used in each batch"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-REV-COST-PROFIT","scaffold":"Variable costs change with the level of output. Flour used per batch rises as more bread is produced, while rent, insurance and fixed licence fees may remain unchanged in the short run.","reforge":{"stem":"Which cost is most likely fixed in the short run for a furniture maker?","options":{"A":"Timber for each table","B":"Factory rent","C":"Packaging per item","D":"Electricity used by each machine"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-prod"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-PRD-17","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-PROD","stem":"What is production?","options":{"A":"The output made by a firm","B":"It falls from 200 to 150 units","C":"The price of a product","D":"The number of consumers"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-PRODUCTIVITY","scaffold":"Production is the output produced by a firm. It may be measured in units of goods or in the value of services provided.","reforge":{"stem":"What is profit?","options":{"A":"Total cost minus revenue","B":"The number of units made","C":"Money left after a producer pays its costs","D":"Reducing output while keeping workers unchanged"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-prod"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-PRD-18","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-PROD","stem":"How is productivity commonly measured?","options":{"A":"Price divided by demand","B":"Output divided by input","C":"Revenue divided by tax","D":"Profit divided by population"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-PRODUCTIVITY","scaffold":"Productivity is a measure of efficiency in using factors of production. A common measure is output per unit of input, such as output per worker.","reforge":{"stem":"What does average cost mean?","options":{"A":"Total revenue from all sales","B":"The firm's total profit","C":"It becomes 300 units per worker","D":"The cost of producing one unit"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-prod"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-PRD-19","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-PROD","stem":"What is total revenue?","options":{"A":"The total spending of a firm on all of its output","B":"Revenue per unit sold","C":"Total income from selling goods or services","D":"Profit after tax"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-REV-COST-PROFIT","scaffold":"Total revenue is the firm's total income from sales. Average revenue is revenue per unit sold.","reforge":{"stem":"When does a firm make a loss?","options":{"A":"When total revenue is less than total cost","B":"The cost of the last unit a firm chooses to produce","C":"When average revenue rises","D":"When output increases"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-prod"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-PRD-20","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-PROD","stem":"What are economies of scale?","options":{"A":"The costs of all firms in an industry","B":"Supply-side policy — improving the economy's long-run productive capacity","C":"A fixed cost paid by consumers","D":"Cost advantages from increasing production scale that lower average cost"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-EOS","scaffold":"Economies of scale are cost advantages gained as a firm increases its scale of production. They can reduce average cost per unit.","reforge":{"stem":"What is total cost?","options":{"A":"Revenue per unit","B":"All costs of a firm added together","C":"A firm spreads risk across several products","D":"Profit before costs"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-prod"}]},"GCSE-ECON-P1-LABOUR":{"label":"Paper 1: The Labour Market","color":"#86efac","questions":[{"id":"GCSE-P1-LAB-01","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-LABOUR","stem":"Why is the demand for labour called derived demand?","options":{"A":"It is derived from the demand for the goods and services labour helps produce","B":"The rate of interest that every shop charges its customers on its credit deals","C":"It is unrelated to a firm's output","D":"It is always higher than labour supply"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-DERIVED-DEMAND","scaffold":"Firms demand workers because workers help produce output that can be sold. If demand for the final product rises, the demand for the labour used to make it may rise too, though productivity and wage costs also matter.","reforge":{"stem":"Demand for delivery drivers increases after online shopping becomes more popular. This is because:","options":{"A":"Drivers are a factor of land","B":"Increasing the switching costs that consumers face when they move","C":"Demand for labour is linked to demand for the delivery service","D":"Wages automatically fall when demand rises"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-labour"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-LAB-02","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-LABOUR","stem":"Which factor is likely to increase the supply of qualified nurses?","options":{"A":"Fewer people of working age","B":"More funded training places and improved qualification opportunities","C":"Every market in the economy has exactly one seller operating within it at any time","D":"A ban on workers moving region"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-LABOUR-SUPPLY","scaffold":"Labour supply depends on population, participation, skills, qualifications, wages, working conditions and mobility. Training can increase the number of workers able to enter a skilled occupation.","reforge":{"stem":"Which change would most likely reduce the supply of construction workers to a particular city?","options":{"A":"Firms in competition always set exactly identical prices to each other in every market","B":"Higher wages and improved conditions","C":"More local training courses","D":"A shortage of affordable housing near the city"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-labour"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-LAB-03","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-LABOUR","stem":"At the equilibrium wage in a labour market:","options":{"A":"Every worker earns the same wage","B":"Every market in the economy has exactly one seller operating within it at any time","C":"The quantity of labour demanded equals the quantity supplied","D":"The government must employ every worker"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-LABOUR-SUPPLY","scaffold":"The labour market uses demand and supply to determine an equilibrium wage and employment level. Different occupations can have different equilibria because their skills, demand and working conditions differ.","reforge":{"stem":"If demand for software developers rises while supply is unchanged, the usual short-run effect is:","options":{"A":"A higher equilibrium wage and employment","B":"No change because labour is not a market","C":"A fall in demand for software","D":"A lower wage and lower employment"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-labour"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-LAB-04","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-LABOUR","stem":"Why might specialist surgeons earn more than workers in occupations requiring fewer qualifications?","options":{"A":"Surgeons have no opportunity cost","B":"Are protected from competition from every other firm that is based inside the currency area","C":"All high wages are set by law","D":"Training and qualification requirements restrict supply while demand for the skill is high"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-WAGE-DIFFERENTIALS","scaffold":"Wage differentials can reflect skill and qualification requirements, scarcity, demand, responsibility, risk, union strength and non-monetary advantages. A restricted supply with strong demand can raise wages.","reforge":{"stem":"Why may a night-shift worker receive a wage premium?","options":{"A":"Night work always requires no skill","B":"Unsocial hours can reduce labour supply, so employers may pay more to attract workers","C":"Every market in the economy has stopped operating altogether and no trade now takes place","D":"Demand for labour is zero at night"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-labour"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-LAB-05","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-LABOUR","stem":"A legal minimum wage is set below the equilibrium wage. What is its likely effect?","options":{"A":"It is non-binding and may have little effect on the market wage","B":"It raises workers' incomes and therefore always lowers firms' unit costs","C":"It makes employment illegal","D":"It always cuts employment"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-MIN-WAGE","scaffold":"A minimum wage only changes the market if it is set above equilibrium, where it is binding. A lower-than-equilibrium minimum does not constrain the wage, although the law may still reassure workers.","reforge":{"stem":"A binding minimum wage is set above the equilibrium wage. Which outcome is possible?","options":{"A":"Every firm hires more workers","B":"Public ownership always delivers lower costs and better service than private firms","C":"More people want to work than firms want to employ, creating excess labour supply","D":"The equilibrium wage is forced lower"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-labour"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-LAB-06","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-LABOUR","stem":"Which is an example of geographical labour immobility?","options":{"A":"A worker learns a new skill","B":"A qualified worker cannot afford to move to a region with vacancies","C":"They guarantee that any new firm will win a large share of the market","D":"A firm advertises a vacancy nationally"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-IMMOBILITY","scaffold":"Geographical mobility is the ability to move location for work. Housing costs, family commitments, transport and regional information can prevent workers moving even when vacancies exist elsewhere.","reforge":{"stem":"A worker is unwilling to move from retail into engineering because they lack the required qualifications. This is:","options":{"A":"A student changes their career preference","B":"Derived demand","C":"A fixed cost","D":"Occupational immobility"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-labour"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-LAB-07","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-LABOUR","stem":"Gross pay is £2,400 and deductions are £500. What is net pay?","options":{"A":"£2,900","B":"£500","C":"£1,900","D":"£1,200"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-PAY","scaffold":"Net pay is what the worker receives after deductions such as income tax, National Insurance and pension contributions: £2,400 - £500 = £1,900. Gross pay is before deductions.","reforge":{"stem":"A worker's gross monthly pay is £2,800. Tax and other deductions total £650. Net pay is:","options":{"A":"£2,150","B":"£650","C":"£2,800","D":"£3,450"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-labour"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-LAB-08","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-LABOUR","stem":"A professional footballer's international call-up makes the player's skills more valuable to clubs. In a labour-market diagram, this is most likely to:","options":{"A":"Guaranteeing that every consumer can buy the product","B":"Make wages unrelated to demand","C":"Reduce the player's productivity","D":"Shift demand for the player's labour to the right"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-DERIVED-DEMAND","scaffold":"If a worker becomes more valuable to employers, demand for that type of labour rises. With supply unchanged, the equilibrium wage and employment may increase. Demand for labour is affected by the value of marginal product and the demand for the final output.","reforge":{"stem":"A new qualification makes a group of technicians more productive, while the number of qualified technicians is unchanged. What is likely?","options":{"A":"Yes, because imports are funded directly out of the government's own budget","B":"Demand for their labour may rise because each worker can add more output","C":"Supply becomes zero","D":"Wages cannot change"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-labour"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-LAB-09","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-LABOUR","stem":"Why is demand for labour described as derived demand?","options":{"A":"It comes from demand for the goods and services workers help produce","B":"Customers stop responding to every price change in the market entirely","C":"It is unrelated to a firm's output","D":"It is always greater than labour supply"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-DERIVED-DEMAND","scaffold":"Firms demand labour because workers help produce output for sale. If demand for the final product rises, demand for the relevant labour may rise, depending on productivity and wage costs.","reforge":{"stem":"Online shopping becomes more popular and firms hire more delivery drivers. This shows that:","options":{"A":"Drivers are a form of capital","B":"Wages must fall when demand rises","C":"Labour demand can be linked to demand for the final service","D":"Firms have much less reason to improve the service they offer"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-labour"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-LAB-10","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-LABOUR","stem":"Gross monthly pay is £2,800 and deductions are £650. What is net pay?","options":{"A":"£3,450","B":"£2,150","C":"£2,800","D":"£650"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-PAY","scaffold":"Net pay is gross pay after deductions: £2,800 minus £650 equals £2,150. Gross pay is the amount before income tax, National Insurance, pension or other deductions.","reforge":{"stem":"Gross pay is £2,400 and deductions total £500. Net pay is:","options":{"A":"£500","B":"£2,900","C":"£2,400","D":"£1,900"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-labour"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-LAB-11","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-LABOUR","stem":"A teacher leaves a paid job to run a hotel. What is the relevant opportunity cost?","options":{"A":"Every possible future job","B":"The hotel's total revenue","C":"The teaching income given up","D":"The value of the hotel building"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-SCARCITY","scaffold":"The opportunity cost is the benefit of the next best alternative forgone. If the teacher leaves employment to run the hotel, the relevant forgone benefit includes the teaching salary.","reforge":{"stem":"A worker leaves a retail job to train as a chef. The opportunity cost includes:","options":{"A":"The wages from the retail job given up","B":"The restaurant's entire profit","C":"Every career the worker could ever choose","D":"The cost of all food in the restaurant"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-labour"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-LAB-12","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-LABOUR","stem":"Which change is most likely to increase the supply of qualified nurses?","options":{"A":"Fewer funded training places","B":"Lower working conditions","C":"Raises the wage employers must offer to attract new workers","D":"More training and qualification opportunities"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-LABOUR-SUPPLY","scaffold":"Training and qualifications can increase the number of people able to enter a skilled occupation. Labour supply also depends on wages, working conditions, participation and mobility.","reforge":{"stem":"Which change is most likely to reduce construction labour supply in a city?","options":{"A":"More local training","B":"A shortage of affordable nearby housing","C":"Improved transport links","D":"There is no demand for the final product"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-labour"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-LAB-13","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-LABOUR","stem":"Why might a surgeon earn more than a worker needing fewer qualifications?","options":{"A":"Training limits supply while demand for the skill is high","B":"All high wages are fixed by law","C":"Surgeons face no opportunity cost","D":"High wages always reflect low productivity among the workers involved"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-WAGE-DIFFERENTIALS","scaffold":"Wage differentials can reflect qualification costs, scarce skills, responsibility, demand, risk and working conditions. A restricted supply combined with strong demand can raise wages.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might night-shift workers receive a wage premium?","options":{"A":"Night work always requires no skill","B":"Demand for labour is zero at night","C":"Unsocial hours may reduce labour supply","D":"The supply of their labour is effectively unlimited"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-labour"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-LAB-14","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-LABOUR","stem":"A minimum wage is set above the equilibrium wage. What may result?","options":{"A":"A shortage of labour","B":"Excess supply of labour","C":"No change in the wage","D":"Every firm hires more workers"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-MIN-WAGE","scaffold":"A binding minimum wage above equilibrium raises the reward for working but may reduce labour demanded and increase labour supplied, creating a surplus of labour. A minimum below equilibrium is non-binding.","reforge":{"stem":"A legal minimum wage is below the market wage. It is likely to be:","options":{"A":"Forces firms to replace workers with machinery in every industry","B":"A cause of labour shortage","C":"A guarantee of higher employment","D":"Non-binding with little effect on the market wage"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-labour"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-LAB-15","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-LABOUR","stem":"A qualified worker cannot move to a city with vacancies because housing is unaffordable. This is:","options":{"A":"A worker learns a new skill","B":"A fixed cost","C":"Geographical immobility","D":"Derived demand"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-IMMOBILITY","scaffold":"Geographical mobility is the ability to move location for work. Housing costs, family commitments, transport and information can prevent movement between regions.","reforge":{"stem":"A worker cannot become an engineer because they lack the required qualifications. This is:","options":{"A":"Occupational immobility","B":"A market surplus","C":"A firm advertises a vacancy nationally","D":"Derived supply"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-labour"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-LAB-16","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-LABOUR","stem":"Which could increase labour demand for trained technicians?","options":{"A":"Becomes far more sensitive to even the smallest changes in its own price","B":"A fall in the firm's expected sales","C":"A ban on investment","D":"Higher productivity that raises the value of each worker's output"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-DERIVED-DEMAND","scaffold":"Labour demand is derived from demand for output and the productivity or value of workers. If trained technicians become more productive, firms may be willing to employ more of them.","reforge":{"stem":"A new qualification makes technicians more productive while their number stays fixed. What may happen?","options":{"A":"Wages cannot change","B":"Demand for their labour may rise","C":"It is set only by workers' leisure choices","D":"Firms stop valuing output"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-labour"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-LAB-17","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-LABOUR","stem":"What is a labour market?","options":{"A":"A market where workers sell labour and employers buy it","B":"Firms in competition always set exactly identical prices to each other in every market","C":"A market for imported currencies","D":"A government benefits office"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-LABOUR-SUPPLY","scaffold":"In the labour market, households supply labour and firms demand labour. Wages are the price paid for labour.","reforge":{"stem":"What does supply of labour refer to?","options":{"A":"The number of jobs advertised","B":"The wages paid after tax","C":"People willing and eligible to offer their labour","D":"Every market in the economy has exactly one seller operating within it at any time"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-labour"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-LAB-18","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-LABOUR","stem":"What is gross pay?","options":{"A":"Pay after income tax","B":"Earnings before deductions","C":"A pension payment","D":"An employer's national insurance bill"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-PAY","scaffold":"Gross pay is the employee's earnings before deductions such as income tax and national insurance.","reforge":{"stem":"What is net pay?","options":{"A":"Pay before any deductions","B":"The employer's total labour cost","C":"The value of future pension benefits","D":"Income left after deductions"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-labour"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-LAB-19","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-LABOUR","stem":"What is income tax?","options":{"A":"A payment for buying goods","B":"Labour demand never depends on product demand","C":"A tax levied directly on personal income","D":"A firm's total wage bill"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-PAY","scaffold":"Income tax is a direct tax on personal income, including earnings from work. It is deducted before an employee receives net pay.","reforge":{"stem":"What is national insurance?","options":{"A":"A contribution by workers and employers towards state benefits","B":"A tax on imported goods","C":"Consumers producing all of their own services at home themselves","D":"A firm's sales revenue"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-labour"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-LAB-20","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-LABOUR","stem":"What is a pension?","options":{"A":"A payment for a current shift","B":"Because saving always gives a higher return than every investment","C":"A firm's cost of recruitment","D":"A regular payment usually made to a retired person or dependant"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-PAY","scaffold":"A pension is a fixed amount paid at regular intervals, often to someone who has retired or to surviving dependants.","reforge":{"stem":"Which figure is measured before deductions?","options":{"A":"Disposable income","B":"Gross pay","C":"Net pay","D":"A pension payment"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-labour"}]},"GCSE-ECON-P1-MONEY":{"label":"Paper 1: Money & Financial Markets","color":"#bbf7d0","questions":[{"id":"GCSE-P1-MNY-01","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-MONEY","stem":"Which function of money allows people to compare the prices of different goods?","options":{"A":"Unit of account","B":"Medium of exchange","C":"Store of value only","D":"Method of taxation"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-MONEY-FUNC","scaffold":"A unit of account provides a common measure of value, making prices comparable. Money also acts as a medium of exchange, a store of value and a standard for deferred payment.","reforge":{"stem":"When a shopper uses pounds to pay for a meal, money is acting mainly as:","options":{"A":"A factor of production","B":"A barrier to trade","C":"A medium of exchange","D":"A production possibility frontier"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-money"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-MNY-02","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-MONEY","stem":"Why is money generally more convenient than barter?","options":{"A":"It has no value to anyone","B":"It avoids the need for a double coincidence of wants","C":"It cannot be stored","D":"They have no possible value to the people who grow them"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-MONEY-FUNC","scaffold":"Barter requires each person to want exactly what the other offers at the same time. Money acts as an accepted medium of exchange, making transactions easier and supporting specialisation.","reforge":{"stem":"Which feature is most important for money to work as a medium of exchange?","options":{"A":"It is impossible to divide","B":"They can be durable and portable in some conditions","C":"It can only be owned by banks","D":"It is widely accepted by people in the economy"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-money"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-MNY-03","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-MONEY","stem":"A saver deposits £3,000 for one year at 4% simple interest. How much interest is earned?","options":{"A":"£12","B":"£3,120","C":"£120","D":"£7,500"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-INTEREST","scaffold":"Interest is 4% of £3,000: 0.04 x £3,000 = £120. The final balance would be £3,120 if no withdrawals or fees apply.","reforge":{"stem":"A saver deposits £5,000 at 3% simple interest for one year. What is the final balance?","options":{"A":"£5,150","B":"£5,300","C":"£6,500","D":"£5,015"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-money"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-MNY-04","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-MONEY","stem":"What is a likely reason for borrowing money?","options":{"A":"Competition appears automatically whenever an industry is sold to private owners","B":"To remove every financial risk","C":"To make saving impossible","D":"To pay for a house or business investment before enough income has been saved"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-INTEREST","scaffold":"Borrowing allows households and firms to bring spending or investment forward, but interest and repayment create a cost. Whether borrowing is sensible depends on the purpose, affordability and risk.","reforge":{"stem":"Which is a potential disadvantage of borrowing?","options":{"A":"The value of money is guaranteed to rise faster than prices","B":"Interest and repayments reduce future disposable income","C":"Loans have no effect on financial risk","D":"Borrowing removes the need for a budget"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-money"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-MNY-05","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-MONEY","stem":"Why might a household save money?","options":{"A":"To build a fund for future spending or protect against unexpected costs","B":"Interest rate changes never affect the behaviour of consumers in any way","C":"To increase current spending immediately","D":"Because savings cannot earn interest"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-INTEREST","scaffold":"Saving postpones consumption and can provide security or fund a planned purchase. The reward may be interest, but inflation, tax and account conditions affect the real return.","reforge":{"stem":"If the interest rate on savings rises, what is one likely effect?","options":{"A":"All households must save the same amount","B":"Saving becomes impossible","C":"Saving may become more attractive relative to immediate consumption","D":"Structural unemployment, following a long-term decline in the industry"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-money"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-MNY-06","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-MONEY","stem":"What is the main purpose of insurance?","options":{"A":"To guarantee that accidents never happen","B":"To pool risk so an individual can receive compensation after a covered loss","C":"To make every investment profitable","D":"The total number of workers employed across a whole industry in a given year"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-FIN-INST","scaffold":"Insurance transfers and pools risk. Policyholders pay premiums, and the insurer pays valid claims under the policy terms. It reduces the financial impact of covered events but does not prevent them or cover every loss.","reforge":{"stem":"Why can insurance encourage a person to buy a car?","options":{"A":"Insurance makes fuel free","B":"The loan automatically becomes entirely free of any further charge","C":"Insurance removes the need to follow the law","D":"Cover for certain losses reduces the financial risk of ownership"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-money"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-MNY-07","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-MONEY","stem":"Which is a likely role of a bank in financial markets?","options":{"A":"Their income is guaranteed to rise enough to cover the shortfall","B":"Setting every worker's wage","C":"Taking deposits and lending some funds to borrowers","D":"Removing the need for money"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-FIN-INST","scaffold":"Commercial banks help channel funds from savers to borrowers, provide payment services and assess credit risk. Lending is not free and banks must manage the risk that loans are not repaid.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might a bank charge a higher interest rate to a borrower with a poor credit history?","options":{"A":"The bank may judge the loan more risky and require compensation for the greater chance of default","B":"Banks do not consider risk","C":"Guaranteed higher wages for all remaining workers because overseas production reduces domestic competition","D":"The borrower has no opportunity cost"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-money"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-MNY-08","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-MONEY","stem":"Which characteristic would make an item a poor form of money?","options":{"A":"It is durable and divisible","B":"Insurance guarantees the car will never be damaged","C":"It is widely accepted","D":"It spoils quickly and is difficult to divide"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-MONEY-FUNC","scaffold":"Good money should be durable, portable, divisible, recognisable, limited in supply and generally accepted. Perishable or indivisible items are inconvenient for transactions and poor stores of value.","reforge":{"stem":"Why are grains of rice an imperfect form of money?","options":{"A":"They can be durable and portable in some conditions","B":"They may spoil, be difficult to standardise and be too plentiful","C":"They are accepted by every bank","D":"A guaranteed payout made for every single event that is claimed for"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-money"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-MNY-09","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-MONEY","stem":"Which function of money lets a shopper compare the prices of different goods?","options":{"A":"Unit of account","B":"Medium of exchange","C":"Store of value","D":"Method of taxation"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-MONEY-FUNC","scaffold":"As a unit of account, money provides a common measure of value so prices can be compared. Money also acts as a medium of exchange, a store of value and a standard for deferred payment.","reforge":{"stem":"When pounds are used to pay for a meal, money is acting mainly as:","options":{"A":"A store of value","B":"A unit of account","C":"A medium of exchange","D":"A factor of production"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-money"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-MNY-10","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-MONEY","stem":"What is one reason money is more convenient than barter?","options":{"A":"It removes all scarcity","B":"It avoids the need for a double coincidence of wants","C":"It cannot be saved","D":"They have no possible value to the people who grow them"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-MONEY-FUNC","scaffold":"Barter requires each person to want what the other offers at the same time. Money is widely accepted as a medium of exchange, making transactions and specialisation easier.","reforge":{"stem":"Which feature helps money work as a medium of exchange?","options":{"A":"It cannot be divided","B":"It is owned only by banks","C":"Its value changes unpredictably","D":"It is widely accepted"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-money"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-MNY-11","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-MONEY","stem":"Why might a lender check a borrower's credit history?","options":{"A":"To remove the need for collateral","B":"To guarantee no interest is charged","C":"To assess the risk of non-repayment","D":"To make saving impossible"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-FIN-INST","scaffold":"A credit history gives information about past repayments and helps a lender judge default risk. A riskier borrower may face a higher interest rate or be refused credit.","reforge":{"stem":"What may happen if a borrower misses loan repayments?","options":{"A":"Their future access to credit may become harder","B":"The loan automatically becomes entirely free of any further charge","C":"The lender's risk disappears","D":"Their income is guaranteed to rise enough to cover the shortfall"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-money"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-MNY-12","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-MONEY","stem":"A saver deposits £5,000 at 3% simple interest for one year. What final balance results?","options":{"A":"£5,015","B":"£6,500","C":"£5,300","D":"£5,150"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-INTEREST","scaffold":"Interest is 3% of £5,000, which is £150. Adding the interest to the deposit gives a final balance of £5,150 after one year.","reforge":{"stem":"A £3,000 deposit earns 4% simple interest for one year. The interest is:","options":{"A":"£12","B":"£120","C":"£3,120","D":"£7,500"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-money"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-MNY-13","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-MONEY","stem":"What is an insurance premium?","options":{"A":"The payment made for insurance cover","B":"The value of every claim paid","C":"A tax on all saving","D":"To guarantee that accidents never happen"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-FIN-INST","scaffold":"A premium is the amount paid by a policyholder for insurance cover, usually at regular intervals or when the policy begins. The insurer then assesses valid claims against the policy terms.","reforge":{"stem":"What is an insurance excess?","options":{"A":"A guaranteed payout made for every single event that is claimed for","B":"The insurer's total annual profit across all of its policies","C":"The amount the policyholder pays towards a claim","D":"A bank's lending rate"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-money"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-MNY-14","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-MONEY","stem":"Which is a role of a commercial bank?","options":{"A":"Setting every wage","B":"Taking deposits and lending funds","C":"Producing all goods","D":"To make every investment profitable"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-FIN-INST","scaffold":"Commercial banks accept deposits, provide payment services and lend funds to households and firms. They assess risk and charge interest because loans may not be repaid.","reforge":{"stem":"Why may a bank charge a higher rate to a borrower with poor credit history?","options":{"A":"To remove all uncertainty from the economy","B":"Interest is unrelated to lending","C":"Banks never consider risk","D":"The loan may be more likely to default"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-money"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-MNY-15","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-MONEY","stem":"Which characteristic makes an item suitable as money?","options":{"A":"It spoils quickly","B":"It is difficult to divide","C":"It is widely accepted and durable","D":"Its value changes unpredictably every minute"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-MONEY-FUNC","scaffold":"Good money should be durable, portable, divisible, recognisable, limited in supply and widely accepted. Perishable or difficult-to-divide items are inconvenient as money.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is a perishable food item an imperfect form of money?","options":{"A":"It may spoil and be difficult to store","B":"It is perfectly durable","C":"It is always accepted by banks","D":"They can be durable and portable in some conditions"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-money"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-MNY-16","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-MONEY","stem":"What is a likely effect when interest rates rise on a variable-rate loan?","options":{"A":"Repayments become zero","B":"Saving becomes impossible","C":"Because saving always gives a higher return than every investment","D":"Repayments may rise, reducing disposable income"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-INTEREST","scaffold":"A higher interest rate can increase repayments on variable-rate borrowing, reducing disposable income. It may also make saving more attractive and discourage new borrowing.","reforge":{"stem":"A rise in interest rates is most likely to make new borrowing:","options":{"A":"Unrelated to repayment costs","B":"More expensive","C":"Free","D":"Impossible for every household"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-money"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-MNY-17","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-MONEY","stem":"What is money?","options":{"A":"Anything generally accepted as payment for goods and services","B":"The loan automatically becomes entirely free of any further charge","C":"A firm's annual profit","D":"A tax on spending"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-MONEY-FUNC","scaffold":"Money is anything generally accepted as a means of payment. It allows exchange without direct barter.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the medium of exchange function of money?","options":{"A":"The insurer's total annual profit across all of its policies","B":"It measures only national output","C":"It provides an accepted way to trade goods and services","D":"It guarantees a profit"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-money"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-MNY-18","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-MONEY","stem":"What is investment in economics?","options":{"A":"The value of money is guaranteed to rise faster than prices","B":"Purchasing capital goods used to produce future output","C":"Saving cash under a mattress","D":"Paying a tax bill"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-INTEREST","scaffold":"Investment includes buying capital goods that help produce future goods and services. It can also mean buying an asset to earn income or sell at a profit.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the rate of interest?","options":{"A":"Because saving always gives a higher return than every investment","B":"A firm's average cost","C":"The price of a house","D":"The cost of borrowing and reward for saving"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-money"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-MNY-19","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-MONEY","stem":"What does the financial sector include?","options":{"A":"Only government departments","B":"All retail shops","C":"Financial organisations, products and flows of capital","D":"Their income is guaranteed to rise enough to cover the shortfall"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-FIN-INST","scaffold":"The financial sector consists of financial organisations and their products. It helps capital flow between savers, borrowers and investors.","reforge":{"stem":"What is a building society?","options":{"A":"A mutual financial institution owned by its members","B":"A guaranteed payout made for every single event that is claimed for","C":"A government tax office","D":"An insurance premium"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-money"},{"id":"GCSE-P1-MNY-20","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P1-MONEY","stem":"What is a mortgage?","options":{"A":"A tax on house sales","B":"Insurance guarantees the car will never be damaged","C":"A return on savings","D":"An agreement to borrow money to buy property"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-FIN-INST","scaffold":"A mortgage is a loan agreement with a financial institution used to purchase property. The borrower normally repays it with interest.","reforge":{"stem":"What does an insurance company provide?","options":{"A":"A government budget surplus","B":"Compensation for specified losses in return for a premium","C":"The insurer's total annual profit across all of its policies","D":"A firm's total revenue"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p1-money"}]},"GCSE-ECON-UK":{"label":"Paper 2: The UK Economy Overview","color":"#166534","questions":[{"id":"GCSE-ECON-01","spec":"GCSE-ECON","stem":"Which of the following is NOT one of the UK government's four main macroeconomic objectives?","options":{"A":"Maximising the exchange rate at all times","B":"Low and stable inflation over the medium term","C":"Low unemployment across the economy","D":"Equilibrium on the balance of payments over time"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-OBJECTIVES","scaffold":"The four main objectives are: economic growth, low unemployment, low and stable inflation, and balance of payments equilibrium. The exchange rate isn't a fixed target the government tries to maximise — a very strong pound hurts exporters, a very weak pound raises import costs, so governments generally want stability, not a 'maximum' rate.","reforge":{"stem":"A government proudly announces the pound has reached its highest value against the dollar in a decade. Why might this NOT be entirely good news?","options":{"A":"Exchange rates have no effect at all on the balance of payments","B":"A stronger pound always benefits every UK business equally, whatever it happens to sell","C":"A stronger pound makes UK exports more expensive, which can hurt exporters and jobs.","D":"A strong pound automatically causes the rate of inflation to rise"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-uk"},{"id":"GCSE-ECON-02","spec":"GCSE-ECON","stem":"UK CPI inflation falls from 6% to 3%. A student says: 'Prices are now falling.' Why is this wrong?","options":{"A":"The student is correct — a lower inflation figure always means prices have fallen","B":"Prices are still rising, but more slowly.","C":"CPI only measures wages, not prices, so the statement doesn't apply","D":"Inflation falling to 3% means prices have fallen by exactly 3%"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-INFLATION-DEF","scaffold":"Inflation is the rate at which the price level is rising. As long as the inflation rate is positive, prices are still going up — 3% inflation means prices rose 3% over the year, not that they fell. Only 0% inflation (stable prices) or negative inflation (deflation) means prices themselves are falling.","reforge":{"stem":"Inflation falls from 3% to 0.5% but stays positive. What is happening to the UK price level?","options":{"A":"It is falling steadily each month","B":"It is completely unchanged from the level of the previous year","C":"It has fallen all the way to zero","D":"It continues to rise, just at a much slower rate than before"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-uk"},{"id":"GCSE-ECON-03","spec":"GCSE-ECON","stem":"A worker is made redundant when a UK car factory closes because demand has permanently shifted towards electric vehicles made abroad. What type of unemployment is this?","options":{"A":"Frictional unemployment — a short-term gap while moving between one job and the next","B":"Seasonal unemployment — tied to a particular time of year when demand for the work disappears","C":"Structural unemployment — caused by a long-term decline in an industry as demand permanently shifts away from it","D":"The worker isn't unemployed at all — they have chosen to become economically inactive and have left the labour force entirely"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-UNEMP-TYPES","scaffold":"Structural unemployment happens when an industry declines permanently, often due to changing demand or technology, leaving workers' skills mismatched with available jobs. Frictional unemployment is brief, between-jobs search; seasonal unemployment is tied to the calendar (e.g. ski instructors in summer) — neither fits a permanent industry-wide shift like this one.","reforge":{"stem":"A graduate spends three weeks applying for jobs before accepting a suitable offer. What type of unemployment does this best illustrate?","options":{"A":"Frictional unemployment — short-term search between entering the job market and finding a role","B":"Cyclical unemployment, caused by a recession reducing demand for labour across the whole economy","C":"Seasonal unemployment, tied to the time of year","D":"Structural unemployment, following a long-term decline in the industry"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-uk"},{"id":"GCSE-ECON-04","spec":"GCSE-ECON","stem":"The government cuts income tax and increases spending on the NHS during an economic slowdown. What type of fiscal policy is this, and what is its main aim?","options":{"A":"Contractionary fiscal policy — reducing aggregate demand in order to bring inflation back under control","B":"Supply-side policy — improving the economy's long-run productive capacity","C":"Monetary policy — using interest rates to influence spending","D":"Expansionary fiscal policy — increasing aggregate demand to boost growth and reduce unemployment"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-FISCAL","scaffold":"Cutting taxes and raising government spending both increase aggregate demand — this is expansionary fiscal policy, typically used to boost growth and employment during a slowdown. Contractionary fiscal policy does the opposite (raising taxes, cutting spending) to cool an overheating economy. Fiscal policy is spending/taxation, decided by the government — not interest rates, which is monetary policy set by the Bank of England.","reforge":{"stem":"To bring down high inflation, the government raises income tax and cuts public spending. What type of fiscal policy is this?","options":{"A":"Expansionary fiscal policy — raising aggregate demand to support growth and reduce unemployment","B":"Contractionary fiscal policy — reducing aggregate demand to help control inflation","C":"Monetary policy — changing interest rates to influence spending","D":"Trade policy — changing tariffs and trade barriers"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-uk"},{"id":"GCSE-ECON-05","spec":"GCSE-ECON","stem":"The Bank of England raises the base interest rate. A student says: 'This will encourage people to spend more.' Why is this wrong?","options":{"A":"Higher rates make borrowing dearer and saving more attractive — spending falls, not rises.","B":"The student is correct — higher interest rates always increase household spending in every case","C":"Interest rates only ever affect businesses, and never affect consumers","D":"Interest rates have no effect at all on borrowing or saving decisions"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-MONETARY","scaffold":"Raising interest rates makes loans, mortgages and credit card debt more expensive, while making saving more rewarding — both effects tend to reduce spending. This is exactly why the Bank of England raises rates when it wants to bring down inflation: less spending means less pressure on prices.","reforge":{"stem":"The Bank of England cuts the base interest rate to boost a slowing economy. What effect is this most likely to have on consumer spending?","options":{"A":"Interest rate changes never affect the behaviour of consumers in any way","B":"It is likely to decrease spending across the economy","C":"It is likely to increase spending, since borrowing becomes cheaper and saving less rewarding","D":"It will have no effect at all, because the commercial banks simply ignore the base rate entirely"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-uk"},{"id":"GCSE-ECON-06","spec":"GCSE-ECON","stem":"A news headline says: 'UK government budget deficit widens.' A student says: 'This means the UK is importing more than it is exporting.' Why is this wrong?","options":{"A":"The student is correct — the two terms mean exactly the same thing","B":"The budget deficit and balance of payments measure different things.","C":"A budget deficit can only be caused by a trade deficit","D":"The UK cannot have a budget deficit unless it also has a trade deficit"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-BUDGET","scaffold":"These are easily confused but distinct: the government budget deficit is the gap between what government spends and what it raises in tax; the balance of payments (current account) measures the value of a country's exports against its imports. A country can run either deficit independently of the other.","reforge":{"stem":"The UK's current account deficit widens because imports have risen faster than exports. Does this necessarily mean the government's budget deficit has also widened?","options":{"A":"Yes, the two always move together","B":"Yes, because imports are funded directly out of the government's own budget","C":"No such distinction exists between these two terms at all","D":"No: the current-account deficit and budget deficit are separate measures."},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-uk"},{"id":"GCSE-ECON-07","spec":"GCSE-ECON","stem":"The pound depreciates (falls in value) against the dollar. A student says: 'This is bad news for everyone in the UK.' Why is this an oversimplification?","options":{"A":"A weaker pound only ever benefits UK consumers, never firms","B":"Exchange rate changes have no effect on either exporters or importers at all","C":"A weaker pound benefits exporters but raises import costs for consumers.","D":"A weaker pound makes both exports and imports cheaper"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-EXCHANGE-RATE","scaffold":"Depreciation is a mixed picture: UK exporters benefit because their goods become cheaper for foreign buyers, but UK consumers and firms that rely on imported goods or raw materials face higher costs. Whether depreciation is 'good' or 'bad' for the UK overall depends on which groups you're asking about.","reforge":{"stem":"The pound appreciates (rises in value) against the euro. Which UK group is most likely to benefit?","options":{"A":"UK consumers, because imported goods become cheaper and their purchasing power over foreign goods rises.","B":"Everyone in the UK equally, because an appreciation changes the value of money in exactly the same way for all households.","C":"No one, since appreciation only ever causes harm","D":"UK exporters, since their goods become more competitive abroad"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-uk"},{"id":"GCSE-ECON-08","spec":"GCSE-ECON","stem":"The government imposes a tariff on imported steel to protect UK steel producers. A student says: 'This benefits everyone in the UK.' Why is this wrong?","options":{"A":"Tariffs benefit every group in the economy with no downside","B":"Tariffs automatically lower prices for UK consumers","C":"Tariffs only affect foreign countries, and never affect UK consumers or businesses at all","D":"Tariffs protect UK steel jobs but raise costs for other UK businesses and consumers."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-PROTECTIONISM","scaffold":"Protectionist measures like tariffs shield a specific domestic industry from foreign competition, which can save jobs in that industry — but the higher price of the protected good raises costs for UK businesses that use it as an input, and often for consumers too. It's a trade-off between groups, not a free win for everyone.","reforge":{"stem":"A government scraps tariffs on imported steel as part of a new free trade agreement. Which UK group is most likely to lose out in the short term?","options":{"A":"UK car manufacturers, who now face higher costs","B":"UK steel producers, who now face cheaper foreign competition","C":"UK consumers, who now face higher prices for steel-based goods","D":"No one loses out from removing tariffs"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-uk"},{"id":"GCSE-ECON-09","spec":"GCSE-ECON-UK","stem":"What does GDP per capita measure most directly?","options":{"A":"Average output per person","B":"The exact income of every person","C":"Whether a recession must be occurring","D":"The total population only"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-GDP","scaffold":"GDP per capita divides a country's GDP by its population. It is an average indicator, not a record of each person's income and not a complete measure of living standards.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the correct view of GDP per capita?","options":{"A":"It always equals median income","B":"It measures only exports","C":"It is GDP divided by population","D":"It cannot change when population changes"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-uk"},{"id":"GCSE-ECON-10","spec":"GCSE-ECON-UK","stem":"Which item would not normally be included in the CPI basket for UK households?","options":{"A":"Rent paid by UK households every month","B":"A machine exported to another country","C":"Food bought by UK households","D":"Bus journeys taken by UK households"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-INFLATION-DEF","scaffold":"CPI tracks prices of a representative basket of goods and services bought by households. A capital machine sold abroad is not a normal household consumption item in the UK basket.","reforge":{"stem":"Inflation falls from 5% to 2%, but remains positive. What happens to the general price level?","options":{"A":"It becomes impossible to measure","B":"It falls by 3%","C":"It is frozen","D":"It still rises, but more slowly"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-uk"},{"id":"GCSE-ECON-11","spec":"GCSE-ECON-UK","stem":"Which change is most likely to reduce the UK's potential economic growth?","options":{"A":"Improved transport links across the country","B":"More spending on training for workers","C":"Fewer available workers from overseas","D":"Higher investment in new machinery"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-GROWTH-CAUSES","scaffold":"Potential growth depends on productive capacity. A smaller labour force can reduce the amount an economy is able to produce, whereas training, infrastructure and capital investment can raise capacity.","reforge":{"stem":"What is a likely result of a free trade agreement?","options":{"A":"Trade barriers between the partners may fall","B":"Trade between the two countries must stop","C":"The range of goods that is available must shrink","D":"Every domestic firm becomes fully protected"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-uk"},{"id":"GCSE-ECON-12","spec":"GCSE-ECON-UK","stem":"Which transaction belongs outside the balance of payments current account?","options":{"A":"A UK tourist paying for a restaurant meal in Spain","B":"Interest received from a bank based overseas","C":"A UK firm exporting software abroad","D":"An overseas company buying UK industrial land"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-BOP","scaffold":"The current account records trade in goods and services, primary income and secondary income. A foreign purchase of UK land is a financial transaction recorded outside the current account.","reforge":{"stem":"Which item is recorded in the current account rather than as a capital investment?","options":{"A":"A foreign company buying up a UK factory","B":"A UK household paying for a hotel abroad","C":"A UK firm purchasing shares from overseas","D":"A bank lending money to a foreign government"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-uk"},{"id":"GCSE-ECON-13","spec":"GCSE-ECON-UK","stem":"A sustained current account surplus is most likely to help a country:","options":{"A":"Reduce its external borrowing","B":"Lower its exchange rate automatically","C":"Increase unemployment directly","D":"Eliminate all inflation"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-BOP","scaffold":"A current account surplus means receipts from trade and income exceed payments. This can reduce the need for external borrowing, although it does not guarantee lower inflation or unemployment.","reforge":{"stem":"What may happen when overseas buyers demand more of a country's exports?","options":{"A":"The supply of its currency must increase","B":"Its current account must move into deficit","C":"Demand for its currency may increase","D":"Domestic output must fall"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-uk"},{"id":"GCSE-ECON-14","spec":"GCSE-ECON-UK","stem":"What is meant by globalisation?","options":{"A":"A country producing everything that it needs entirely by itself","B":"Growing economic links and interdependence between countries","C":"The removal of all domestic markets from an economy","D":"A policy of banning imports from other countries"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-GLOBALISATION","scaffold":"Globalisation describes increasing connections between economies through trade, investment, migration, technology and supply chains. It does not mean that every barrier disappears.","reforge":{"stem":"Which development is evidence of greater globalisation?","options":{"A":"Every good being produced locally instead","B":"A fall in communication between different economies","C":"A country ending all of its international trade","D":"A firm using suppliers in several countries"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-uk"},{"id":"GCSE-ECON-15","spec":"GCSE-ECON-UK","stem":"Which outcome is most closely linked to social sustainability?","options":{"A":"A firm selling more imported fuel this year","B":"A higher exchange rate lasting for just one month","C":"A transport project improving long-term access for isolated communities","D":"A temporary rise in one private company's annual advertising budget this year"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-SUSTAINABILITY","scaffold":"Social sustainability considers whether present decisions support people's long-term wellbeing and access to opportunities. Better transport can connect communities to work, education and services.","reforge":{"stem":"Which policy most clearly supports sustainable development?","options":{"A":"Investing in reliable low-emission public transport","B":"Ignoring how resources will be used in future","C":"Using much more coal without any pollution controls at all","D":"Maximising output for one single year only"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-uk"},{"id":"GCSE-ECON-16","spec":"GCSE-ECON-UK","stem":"What is a possible benefit of international trade for UK producers?","options":{"A":"Fewer potential customers overall","B":"Higher unit costs in every single case","C":"A ban on all overseas competition","D":"Larger markets and economies of scale"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-TRADE-BENEFITS","scaffold":"Selling abroad can increase the market available to producers. Higher output may allow firms to spread fixed costs and gain economies of scale, although outcomes depend on competition and demand.","reforge":{"stem":"Which is a likely consumer benefit of international trade?","options":{"A":"A guarantee that every domestic price rises","B":"More choice of goods and services","C":"Less access to overseas products","D":"An end to all domestic production"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-uk"},{"id":"GCSE-ECON-DEF-01","spec":"GCSE-ECON-UK","stem":"What is economic growth?","options":{"A":"An increase in GDP over time","B":"A fall in the general price level","C":"A rise in the level of imports only","D":"A reduction in the population"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-GDP","scaffold":"Economic growth is growth in GDP, the value of output, over time. It is normally measured using a percentage change.","reforge":{"stem":"What does GDP measure?","options":{"A":"The total number of people who are in work in a country during a single year","B":"The average rate of tax paid","C":"The total value added of goods and services produced in a country in a year","D":"The exchange rate against the dollar"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-uk"},{"id":"GCSE-ECON-DEF-02","spec":"GCSE-ECON-UK","stem":"What does GDP per capita calculate?","options":{"A":"GDP multiplied by population","B":"GDP divided by population","C":"Population divided by exports","D":"Government spending per firm"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-GDP","scaffold":"GDP per capita is GDP divided by the population. It is an average measure and does not show how income is distributed.","reforge":{"stem":"Which description fits a boom?","options":{"A":"Zero production in the economy","B":"Falling GDP for two or more successive quarters","C":"A permanent deficit on trade","D":"High economic activity and high employment"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-uk"},{"id":"GCSE-ECON-DEF-03","spec":"GCSE-ECON-UK","stem":"What is a recession?","options":{"A":"Any month in which prices rise","B":"A period of unusually high employment","C":"A fall in GDP for two or more consecutive quarters","D":"A sustained rise in the level of GDP per capita over a year"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-CYCLE","scaffold":"A recession is commonly defined as GDP falling for two or more consecutive quarters. It represents a contraction in economic activity.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the most accurate description of the labour force?","options":{"A":"The number of people who work in a country","B":"The total number of children in a country","C":"Only the people receiving pensions","D":"All of the people outside the labour market"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-uk"},{"id":"GCSE-ECON-DEF-04","spec":"GCSE-ECON-UK","stem":"What is price stability?","options":{"A":"Every single product in the shops has exactly the same price as every other one","B":"Prices fall rapidly in every year that passes","C":"Wages never change from one year to the next","D":"The general price level stays constant or rises at an acceptably low rate"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-INFLATION-DEF","scaffold":"Price stability means the general level of prices is constant or increasing at a low, acceptable rate. It does not require every individual price to be unchanged.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the rate of inflation?","options":{"A":"The price of one single product","B":"The percentage rise in the general price level over time","C":"The total value of all imports","D":"The total number of firms that are operating within a market"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-uk"}]},"GCSE-ECON-P2-NATIONAL":{"label":"Paper 2: The National Economy","color":"#166534","questions":[{"id":"GCSE-P2-NAT-01","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-NATIONAL","stem":"Which is a macroeconomic objective of the UK government?","options":{"A":"Maintaining low and stable inflation","B":"Keeping every firm profitable","C":"Maximising imports","D":"Low and stable inflation over the medium term"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-OBJECTIVES","scaffold":"Macroeconomic objectives concern the performance of the whole economy, including growth, low unemployment, price stability and a sustainable balance of payments. They can conflict, so governments face trade-offs.","reforge":{"stem":"Which outcome would generally be considered a national economic policy aim?","options":{"A":"Equilibrium on the balance of payments over time","B":"Falling productivity","C":"Stable prices and sustainable economic growth","D":"A permanent trade deficit"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-national"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-NAT-02","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-NATIONAL","stem":"Why might the government remove VAT from a product such as a cycle helmet?","options":{"A":"It makes supply perfectly inelastic","B":"It may be a merit good with wider benefits when consumed","C":"It is always a luxury good","D":"It is impossible for the good to be produced privately at all"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-MERIT-GOODS","scaffold":"Merit goods create benefits for other people as well as the consumer. Reducing tax lowers the price and may encourage consumption, such as safer cycling or improved education, although the policy has an opportunity cost.","reforge":{"stem":"A government subsidises school vaccination programmes because:","options":{"A":"A good whose consumption creates negative externalities and no private benefit at all","B":"They are examples of demerit goods","C":"They are supplied only by overseas firms","D":"They create positive external benefits beyond the individual receiving the vaccine"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-national"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-NAT-03","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-NATIONAL","stem":"Which tax system takes a larger percentage of income from higher earners?","options":{"A":"A poll tax","B":"A proportional tax with one fixed amount","C":"Progressive taxation","D":"Regressive taxation"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-TAX-TYPES","scaffold":"Under a progressive system, the average tax rate rises as income rises. This can reduce post-tax inequality and raise revenue, but higher rates may affect incentives depending on the circumstances.","reforge":{"stem":"If a household on a low income pays 8% of its income in a tax while a high-income household pays 2%, the tax is best described as:","options":{"A":"Regressive","B":"Deflationary","C":"Proportional","D":"Progressive"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-national"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-NAT-04","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-NATIONAL","stem":"Which area is most likely to be included in government expenditure?","options":{"A":"A consumer's private saving","B":"Household pocket money","C":"A firm's retained profit","D":"Public healthcare"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-GOVT-SPENDING","scaffold":"Government spending includes public services such as health, education, transport, defence and social protection. Spending choices have opportunity costs because public funds are limited.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might a government increase spending on education?","options":{"A":"It reduces the number of people who are willing to look for paid work","B":"To improve human capital and potentially raise future productivity","C":"To reduce the size of the labour force","D":"To make all private firms state-owned"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-national"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-NAT-05","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-NATIONAL","stem":"A country has a labour force of 30 million and 1.5 million people are unemployed. What is the unemployment rate?","options":{"A":"5%","B":"0.05%","C":"20%","D":"31.5%"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-UNEMP-MEASURE","scaffold":"The unemployment rate is unemployed people divided by the labour force, multiplied by 100: 1.5 million / 30 million x 100 = 5%.","reforge":{"stem":"If 2 million people are unemployed in a labour force of 40 million, the unemployment rate is:","options":{"A":"2%","B":"42%","C":"5%","D":"20%"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-national"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-NAT-06","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-NATIONAL","stem":"What does a government budget deficit mean?","options":{"A":"Household saving is greater than borrowing","B":"Government spending is greater than tax revenue in a period","C":"The student is correct — the two terms mean exactly the same thing","D":"Exports are greater than imports"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-BUDGET","scaffold":"A budget deficit occurs when government expenditure exceeds its receipts, so the government may need to borrow. It is different from a current account deficit, which concerns international transactions.","reforge":{"stem":"A government collects £720 billion in taxes and spends £760 billion. Before interest and other adjustments, it has:","options":{"A":"A £1,480 billion trade deficit","B":"Balanced finances","C":"A £40 billion budget surplus","D":"A £40 billion budget deficit"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-national"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-NAT-07","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-NATIONAL","stem":"Why might an ageing population increase government spending?","options":{"A":"Older people cannot consume public services","B":"Reducing healthcare capacity across the whole of the public sector","C":"There may be more demand for pensions and healthcare","D":"The labour force automatically doubles"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-GOVT-SPENDING","scaffold":"An ageing population can increase expenditure on pensions, social care and healthcare. The government may also face a smaller proportion of working-age taxpayers, creating pressure on public finances.","reforge":{"stem":"Which demographic change is most likely to put pressure on social protection spending?","options":{"A":"A larger retired population relative to workers","B":"Tax revenue must always rise faster than spending","C":"A smaller need for healthcare","D":"A fall in life expectancy"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-national"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-NAT-08","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-NATIONAL","stem":"Why might governments support a large infrastructure project?","options":{"A":"It guarantees no opportunity cost","B":"A government department that decides and sets all of the prices charged anywhere in the economy","C":"It always reduces aggregate demand","D":"It can provide long-term social benefits that private investors may not fully capture"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-GOVT-SPENDING","scaffold":"Infrastructure such as rail links can create external benefits, connect regions and support productivity, but it may require very large upfront finance and careful cost-benefit evaluation.","reforge":{"stem":"A new rail line reduces journey times for many firms and workers. This is one reason public support may be justified because it:","options":{"A":"Makes all private investment unnecessary","B":"Creates wider benefits not captured in the ticket price alone","C":"Removes scarcity from the economy","D":"A fall in private investment because firms lose contracts to the state"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-national"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-NAT-09","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-NATIONAL","stem":"Which is a main macroeconomic objective of a government?","options":{"A":"Low and stable inflation","B":"Maximum imports in every year","C":"Zero public spending","D":"Permanent budget deficits"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-OBJECTIVES","scaffold":"Macroeconomic objectives include economic growth, low unemployment, low and stable inflation and a sustainable balance of payments. They can conflict, so governments face trade-offs.","reforge":{"stem":"Which outcome would normally be viewed as a macroeconomic policy success?","options":{"A":"A permanent fall in output","B":"Rapidly rising unemployment","C":"Stable prices alongside sustainable growth","D":"Exchange rates have no effect at all on the balance of payments"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-national"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-NAT-10","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-NATIONAL","stem":"Why might the government subsidise school vaccinations?","options":{"A":"They create costs for other people","B":"They create benefits beyond the person vaccinated","C":"They are always luxury goods","D":"It creates no private benefit for the person treated"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-MERIT-GOODS","scaffold":"Vaccination can create positive externalities because reducing infection risk benefits other people as well as the person receiving the vaccination. A subsidy may encourage consumption.","reforge":{"stem":"Which tax charges a higher percentage of income as income rises?","options":{"A":"A fixed poll charge","B":"A regressive tax","C":"A proportional tax","D":"A progressive tax"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-national"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-NAT-11","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-NATIONAL","stem":"How might an ageing population affect the tax base?","options":{"A":"It reduces the number of people who are willing to look for paid work","B":"Every person's tax payment must rise","C":"A smaller share of working-age people may pay income tax","D":"Pension spending disappears"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-GOVT-SPENDING","scaffold":"If the retired population grows relative to the working-age population, fewer people may be earning taxable income while demand for pensions and healthcare increases. The outcome depends on employment, migration and policy.","reforge":{"stem":"Which policy could reduce pressure caused by an ageing population?","options":{"A":"Encouraging later retirement or higher labour-force participation","B":"Reducing healthcare capacity across the whole of the public sector","C":"Banning pension saving","D":"Deliberately shrinking the size of the working-age population"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-national"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-NAT-12","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-NATIONAL","stem":"What does a government budget surplus mean?","options":{"A":"A budget deficit can only be caused by a trade deficit","B":"Imports exceed exports","C":"Household saving exceeds borrowing","D":"Tax revenue is greater than government spending"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-BUDGET","scaffold":"A budget surplus occurs when government revenue exceeds government spending in a period. It is different from a trade surplus, which concerns exports and imports.","reforge":{"stem":"A government collects £600 billion and spends £570 billion. It has:","options":{"A":"A £30 billion budget deficit","B":"A £30 billion budget surplus","C":"A £1,170 billion trade surplus","D":"Balanced finances"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-national"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-NAT-13","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-NATIONAL","stem":"If a government chooses to fund a hospital instead of a sports centre, what is the opportunity cost?","options":{"A":"The benefit of the sports centre forgone","B":"They are two names for exactly the same problem","C":"Every project in the country","D":"The value of all public assets"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-SCARCITY","scaffold":"Opportunity cost is the benefit of the next best alternative forgone. It is not the money spent on the chosen project or every option considered.","reforge":{"stem":"Which is a direct tax?","options":{"A":"Value added tax","B":"A tax on petrol purchases","C":"Income tax","D":"A customs duty"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-national"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-NAT-14","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-NATIONAL","stem":"Which change is most likely to raise average living standards?","options":{"A":"Lower real output per person","B":"Higher real GDP per capita with better public services","C":"A fall in employment and wages","D":"The countries have identical living standards in every respect"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-GDP-WELLBEING","scaffold":"Real GDP per capita is a useful average measure of material living standards, while public services such as health and education also matter. GDP alone does not show distribution or environmental quality.","reforge":{"stem":"Which indicator can add to GDP per capita when judging development?","options":{"A":"The number of banknotes in circulation","B":"The exchange rate on one day","C":"The value of one firm's exports","D":"Life expectancy"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-national"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-NAT-15","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-NATIONAL","stem":"A country produces increasingly poor-quality goods at high prices. Which problem may result?","options":{"A":"A rise in export competitiveness","B":"Deliberately shrinking the size of the working-age population","C":"A current account deficit as exports become harder to sell","D":"Automatic improvement in productivity"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-BOP","scaffold":"Poor quality and high prices can reduce foreign demand for exports. If imports exceed exports by more, the current account may move towards deficit.","reforge":{"stem":"Which policy could improve a country's long-run productive potential?","options":{"A":"Funding education and training","B":"Closing transport links","C":"Reducing investment in skills","D":"Restricting access to technology"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-national"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-NAT-16","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-NATIONAL","stem":"Why might the state provide education rather than leave all provision to private markets?","options":{"A":"Education has no opportunity cost","B":"It has no effect at all on the general level of public health","C":"State provision guarantees equal outcomes","D":"Education can create wider benefits and improve opportunity"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-MERIT-GOODS","scaffold":"Education can create positive externalities, raise human capital and improve social mobility. State provision still uses scarce resources and does not guarantee identical outcomes.","reforge":{"stem":"When the government spends on a school, what is the opportunity cost?","options":{"A":"The school building itself","B":"The next best use of the public funds","C":"All future tax revenue","D":"It always creates a private loss for the consumer"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-national"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-NAT-17","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-NATIONAL","stem":"What is government spending?","options":{"A":"The total money spent by government in a period","B":"Guarantees every household receives the same income","C":"The income earned by households","D":"The price of imports"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-GOVT-SPENDING","scaffold":"Government spending is the total amount of money spent by the government during a given period, for example on health, education or benefits.","reforge":{"stem":"What is government revenue?","options":{"A":"The total amount that households pay in taxes each year","B":"Household disposable income","C":"Finance available to fund government spending","D":"The value of exports"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-national"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-NAT-18","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-NATIONAL","stem":"What does income and wealth distribution describe?","options":{"A":"They guarantee higher profits for every firm operating in the economy","B":"Government action using mainly tax and benefits to reduce inequality","C":"A firm's production method","D":"The number of imports"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-REDISTRIBUTION","scaffold":"Income and wealth distribution refers to government action, mainly through taxation and benefits, to reduce inequalities in income and wealth.","reforge":{"stem":"What is a progressive tax?","options":{"A":"To make all workers earn exactly the same wage each week","B":"A tax on spending only","C":"A tax paid only by firms","D":"A tax taking a greater percentage as income rises"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-national"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-NAT-19","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-NATIONAL","stem":"Which is a direct tax?","options":{"A":"VAT on a purchase","B":"A tax on petrol","C":"Income or wealth tax","D":"A tariff on an import"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-TAX-TYPES","scaffold":"A direct tax is charged on income or wealth. VAT and duties on spending are examples of indirect taxes.","reforge":{"stem":"Which is an indirect tax?","options":{"A":"A tax on spending on goods and services","B":"A proportional tax with one fixed amount","C":"Inheritance tax","D":"Income tax"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-national"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-NAT-20","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-NATIONAL","stem":"When is the government budget balanced?","options":{"A":"Government spending is greater than tax revenue","B":"Exports equal imports","C":"Household saving equals investment","D":"Tax revenue equals government spending"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-BUDGET","scaffold":"A balanced government budget occurs when tax revenue is equal to government spending in the period.","reforge":{"stem":"What is a budget deficit?","options":{"A":"Income exceeds wealth","B":"Government spending is greater than tax revenue","C":"Exports exceed imports","D":"The UK cannot have a budget deficit unless it also has a trade deficit"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-national"}]},"GCSE-ECON-P2-GROWTH":{"label":"Paper 2: Economic Growth","color":"#15803d","questions":[{"id":"GCSE-P2-GRO-01","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-GROWTH","stem":"Real GDP rises from £500 billion to £515 billion. What is the approximate economic growth rate?","options":{"A":"3%","B":"0.3%","C":"15%","D":"103%"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-GDP","scaffold":"Growth rate is the change in real GDP divided by the original GDP, multiplied by 100: £15 billion / £500 billion x 100 = 3%.","reforge":{"stem":"Real GDP increases from £800 billion to £824 billion. The growth rate is:","options":{"A":"2%","B":"103%","C":"3%","D":"24%"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-growth"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-GRO-02","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-GROWTH","stem":"Why is real GDP per capita useful when comparing living standards?","options":{"A":"To make consumers automatically demand fewer strawberries in the following season","B":"It relates output to the size of the population and removes some price effects","C":"It measures only government spending","D":"It guarantees equal income distribution"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-GDP","scaffold":"Real GDP per capita divides inflation-adjusted output by population. It gives a rough average measure, but it does not show how income is distributed or capture every aspect of wellbeing.","reforge":{"stem":"A country’s real GDP rises but its population rises even faster. What may happen to real GDP per capita?","options":{"A":"It is unchanged by population","B":"It becomes a measure of inflation","C":"It must double","D":"It may fall"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-growth"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-GRO-03","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-GROWTH","stem":"Which description best fits a recession?","options":{"A":"A rise in the price level only","B":"A permanent trade surplus","C":"A sustained fall in real GDP, commonly defined as two consecutive quarters","D":"Firms sell their own labour in the product markets, and households buy resources there too"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-CYCLE","scaffold":"A recession is a period when economic activity contracts. Falling output can reduce business revenues, investment and employment, although definitions and severity can vary.","reforge":{"stem":"During an economic boom, which combination is most likely?","options":{"A":"Rising output and strong employment","B":"Zero investment and zero inflation","C":"A guaranteed current account surplus","D":"Falling output and falling demand"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-growth"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-GRO-04","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-GROWTH","stem":"Which change is most likely to increase long-run economic growth?","options":{"A":"A permanent fall in labour productivity","B":"Destroying transport infrastructure","C":"Less training for workers","D":"Investment in productive technology"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-GROWTH-CAUSES","scaffold":"Investment can increase the capital stock, capacity and productivity of an economy. Education, training, innovation and improved infrastructure can also shift productive potential outward.","reforge":{"stem":"A firm installs more efficient machinery and workers receive training. The most likely long-run effect is:","options":{"A":"Lower productive capacity","B":"Higher potential output through improved productivity","C":"Growth guarantees that no public spending is ever required at all","D":"No change to the economy’s supply"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-growth"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-GRO-05","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-GROWTH","stem":"Which is a possible benefit of economic growth?","options":{"A":"Higher average living standards and more employment","B":"Tax revenue is completely unrelated to the level of national output","C":"Guaranteed lower pollution","D":"The end of scarcity"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-GROWTH-CAUSES","scaffold":"Growth can create jobs, raise incomes and provide more resources for public services. Its benefits depend on distribution and whether growth is environmentally sustainable.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might economic growth reduce poverty?","options":{"A":"Growth makes resources unlimited","B":"It prevents every recession","C":"Higher output can create jobs and incomes, especially if the gains reach lower-income households","D":"Free markets always provide far too much public transport in every region of the country, at all times"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-growth"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-GRO-06","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-GROWTH","stem":"Why might rapid economic growth damage the environment?","options":{"A":"A higher GDP makes pollution impossible","B":"Higher production may increase resource use, emissions and waste","C":"Growth always reduces energy demand","D":"A temporary rise in one private company's annual advertising budget this year"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-SUSTAINABILITY","scaffold":"If growth relies on fossil fuels or intensive resource use, it may create pollution, congestion and climate damage. Sustainable growth seeks to raise living standards without exhausting environmental resources.","reforge":{"stem":"What does sustainable economic growth aim to do?","options":{"A":"Only current-year output, even if meeting today's targets exhausts resources","B":"Keep real GDP permanently unchanged","C":"Stop all economic activity","D":"Increase living standards while limiting long-term environmental damage"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-growth"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-GRO-07","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-GROWTH","stem":"A country’s nominal GDP rises by 6% while prices rise by 8%. What is likely to have happened to real GDP?","options":{"A":"It was unchanged because nominal GDP rose","B":"It cannot be affected by prices","C":"It fell by approximately 2%","D":"It rose by 14%"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-REAL-VS-NOMINAL","scaffold":"Nominal GDP is measured at current prices. If prices rise faster than nominal output, the volume of production may have fallen; approximately 6% - 8% = -2% before more precise adjustment.","reforge":{"stem":"Why do economists use real rather than nominal GDP to assess changes in output over time?","options":{"A":"Real GDP adjusts for price changes","B":"Real GDP measures only exports","C":"A permanent current account surplus","D":"Nominal GDP includes population"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-growth"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-GRO-08","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-GROWTH","stem":"Why can a rise in GDP be an imperfect measure of wellbeing?","options":{"A":"GDP measures only happiness","B":"The number of imports recorded, without considering health or education outcomes","C":"GDP automatically records unpaid care","D":"It may hide inequality and excludes some non-market and environmental effects"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-GDP-WELLBEING","scaffold":"GDP records the market value of output, not how income is shared, unpaid work, leisure, health or environmental quality. GDP per capita is useful but should be interpreted alongside other indicators.","reforge":{"stem":"Two countries have the same GDP per capita, but one has much greater inequality and pollution. What does this show?","options":{"A":"The countries have identical living standards in every respect","B":"GDP per capita alone cannot fully compare wellbeing","C":"GDP per capita measures pollution directly","D":"Inequality has no economic relevance"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-growth"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-GRO-09","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-GROWTH","stem":"Real GDP rises from £600 billion to £630 billion. What is the approximate growth rate?","options":{"A":"5%","B":"30%","C":"0.5%","D":"105%"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-GDP","scaffold":"Growth rate is the change divided by the original value, multiplied by 100: £30 billion / £600 billion x 100 = 5%.","reforge":{"stem":"Total real GDP rises by 4% while population rises by 3%. What is the approximate change in GDP per person?","options":{"A":"The exact income of every person","B":"A fall of 7%","C":"A rise of about 1%","D":"No change"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-growth"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-GRO-10","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-GROWTH","stem":"Why is real GDP preferred to nominal GDP when comparing output over time?","options":{"A":"It was unchanged because nominal GDP rose","B":"It removes the effect of price changes","C":"It measures the exchange rate","D":"It ignores changes in production"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-REAL-VS-NOMINAL","scaffold":"Real GDP is adjusted for inflation, so changes are more likely to reflect changes in the volume of output rather than only changing prices.","reforge":{"stem":"Which description best fits a recession?","options":{"A":"A period when all firms expand","B":"A period of rising prices only","C":"The countries have identical living standards in every respect","D":"A sustained contraction in real economic activity"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-growth"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-GRO-11","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-GROWTH","stem":"What does sustainable economic growth aim to achieve?","options":{"A":"No change in real GDP","B":"Increasing waste without controls while claiming that future clean-up will solve the problem","C":"Higher living standards without exhausting future resources","D":"An end to all economic choices"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-SUSTAINABILITY","scaffold":"Sustainable growth raises living standards while limiting long-term environmental damage and protecting the ability of future generations to meet their needs.","reforge":{"stem":"Which change is most likely to shift productive potential outward?","options":{"A":"Improved worker training","B":"Lower labour productivity","C":"Less capital investment","D":"A fall in consumer confidence"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-growth"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-GRO-12","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-GROWTH","stem":"How might economic growth increase government tax revenue?","options":{"A":"It raises current consumer spending rather than future capacity","B":"Growth removes all taxes","C":"Output falls whenever GDP rises","D":"Higher incomes and profits can widen the tax base"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-GROWTH-CAUSES","scaffold":"If growth raises employment, incomes and company profits, more taxable activity may increase government receipts. The size of the effect depends on tax rates, distribution and the cause of growth.","reforge":{"stem":"Why may growth give a government more scope to fund public services?","options":{"A":"Growth guarantees that no public spending is ever required at all","B":"Higher taxable incomes can raise receipts","C":"Economic activity ends","D":"Tax revenue is completely unrelated to the level of national output"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-growth"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-GRO-13","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-GROWTH","stem":"Why can GDP per capita be an imperfect measure of wellbeing?","options":{"A":"It can hide inequality and environmental damage","B":"It measures no economic output","C":"It includes every unpaid activity","D":"The current account balance, which measures trade and financial flows but not schooling"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-GDP-WELLBEING","scaffold":"GDP per capita is an average. It does not show how income is distributed and may omit unpaid work, leisure, health, security and environmental effects.","reforge":{"stem":"Factories are producing below their normal capacity and unemployment is rising. This suggests:","options":{"A":"A positive output gap","B":"No output gap","C":"A negative output gap","D":"An unavoidable boom"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-growth"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-GRO-14","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-GROWTH","stem":"Which investment is most likely to increase long-run economic growth?","options":{"A":"Advertising an existing product","B":"New productive machinery","C":"A temporary price cut","D":"Higher household borrowing"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-GROWTH-CAUSES","scaffold":"Investment in capital can increase productive capacity and labour productivity. A price cut or extra borrowing may affect demand without necessarily increasing potential output.","reforge":{"stem":"A tax cut increases household spending, but productive capacity is unchanged. This is mainly:","options":{"A":"Potential growth","B":"A fall in aggregate demand","C":"A supply shock","D":"Actual growth"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-growth"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-GRO-15","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-GROWTH","stem":"Nominal GDP rises by 3% while prices rise by 5%. What is likely to have happened to real output?","options":{"A":"It rose by 8%","B":"It cannot be affected by prices","C":"It fell by about 2%","D":"It rose by 5%"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-REAL-VS-NOMINAL","scaffold":"If prices rise faster than nominal GDP, the volume of output is likely to have fallen. A simple approximation is 3% - 5% = -2%.","reforge":{"stem":"Real GDP grows from £500 billion to £525 billion. What is the growth rate?","options":{"A":"5%","B":"4%","C":"2%","D":"10%"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-growth"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-GRO-16","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-GROWTH","stem":"Why can life expectancy help assess a country's development?","options":{"A":"It measures only export performance","B":"The number of imports recorded, without considering health or education outcomes","C":"It records the value of unpaid work","D":"It can indicate health and living conditions"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-GDP-WELLBEING","scaffold":"Development is broader than output. Life expectancy can provide information about health, nutrition, safety and access to medical care, although it is only one indicator.","reforge":{"stem":"Which measure gives information about educational development?","options":{"A":"The exchange rate","B":"Adult literacy or school enrolment","C":"The number of banknotes in circulation","D":"The inflation rate"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-growth"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-GRO-17","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-GROWTH","stem":"What does economic growth mean?","options":{"A":"Growth in GDP over time","B":"A fall in labour productivity","C":"A fall in all prices","D":"A permanent trade surplus"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-GDP","scaffold":"Economic growth is an increase in GDP, the value of output, over time. It is distinct from the level of GDP at one point.","reforge":{"stem":"What is GDP?","options":{"A":"The total number of people who are in work in a country during a single year","B":"The average household income","C":"The total value added of goods and services produced domestically in a year","D":"The exchange rate"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-growth"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-GRO-18","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-GROWTH","stem":"Which calculation gives GDP per capita?","options":{"A":"Population divided by GDP","B":"GDP divided by population","C":"GDP multiplied by population","D":"Exports divided by imports"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-CYCLE","scaffold":"GDP per capita divides GDP by the population. It gives an average output measure, not the income received by each individual.","reforge":{"stem":"Which description fits a boom?","options":{"A":"A period of falling GDP","B":"A period of zero employment","C":"A sustained rise in the level of GDP per capita over a year","D":"A period of high activity and high employment"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-growth"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-GRO-19","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-GROWTH","stem":"Which definition describes a recession?","options":{"A":"High GDP and high employment","B":"A sustained rise in the level of GDP per capita over a year","C":"A fall in GDP for at least two consecutive quarters","D":"A rise in output per worker"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-CYCLE","scaffold":"A recession is commonly defined as GDP falling for two or more consecutive quarters. It is a period of contracting economic activity.","reforge":{"stem":"What does the labour force measure in the supplied definitions?","options":{"A":"The number of people who work in a country","B":"The number of people receiving benefits","C":"All of the people outside the labour market","D":"The number of retired people"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-growth"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-GRO-20","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-GROWTH","stem":"What is the correct view of GDP per capita?","options":{"A":"It is always equal to median income","B":"It is total GDP multiplied by population","C":"It measures only exports","D":"It is GDP divided by population"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-GDP","scaffold":"GDP per capita is calculated by dividing GDP by population. It is a useful average but does not show inequality or every aspect of wellbeing.","reforge":{"stem":"Which outcome is characteristic of a boom?","options":{"A":"It cannot change when population changes","B":"High economic activity and employment","C":"GDP falling for two quarters","D":"A negative growth rate only"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-growth"}]},"GCSE-ECON-P2-UNEMP":{"label":"Paper 2: Low Unemployment","color":"#16a34a","questions":[{"id":"GCSE-P2-UNEMP-01","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-UNEMP","stem":"A labour force contains 28 million people, of whom 1.4 million are unemployed. What is the unemployment rate?","options":{"A":"5%","B":"0.5%","C":"20%","D":"29.4%"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-UNEMP-MEASURE","scaffold":"Unemployment rate = unemployed / labour force x 100. Therefore 1.4 / 28 x 100 = 5%.","reforge":{"stem":"If 900,000 people are unemployed in a labour force of 30 million, the unemployment rate is:","options":{"A":"0.3%","B":"33.3%","C":"3%","D":"30%"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-unemp"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-UNEMP-02","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-UNEMP","stem":"What is meant by full employment?","options":{"A":"Seasonal unemployment — tied to a particular time of year when demand for the work disappears","B":"Most willing and able workers can find jobs, although some frictional unemployment remains","C":"Every adult works for the government","D":"There is no unemployment of any kind"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-UNEMP-MEASURE","scaffold":"Full employment does not usually mean zero unemployment. People may be temporarily between jobs or entering the labour market, so some frictional unemployment can remain.","reforge":{"stem":"Why can an economy have full employment while its unemployment rate is above zero?","options":{"A":"Redistribution - income is transferred to every buyer in the ticket market","B":"Unemployment is counted as employment","C":"The unemployment rate excludes all workers","D":"Some people are changing jobs or searching for their first suitable job"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-unemp"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-UNEMP-03","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-UNEMP","stem":"A worker loses a job because demand permanently shifts away from the industry. What type of unemployment is most likely?","options":{"A":"Seasonal","B":"Voluntary retirement","C":"Structural","D":"Frictional"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-UNEMP-TYPES","scaffold":"Structural unemployment occurs when the pattern of demand or production changes and workers’ skills no longer match available jobs. Retraining and mobility can reduce it.","reforge":{"stem":"A ski instructor is unemployed during the summer but expects work next winter. This is:","options":{"A":"Seasonal unemployment","B":"Cyclical unemployment","C":"Technological unemployment only","D":"Structural unemployment"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-unemp"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-UNEMP-04","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-UNEMP","stem":"What is cyclical unemployment most closely associated with?","options":{"A":"Workers moving between jobs","B":"Frictional unemployment — a short-term gap while moving between one job and the next","C":"The school holiday calendar only","D":"A downturn reducing demand for goods and labour"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-UNEMP-TYPES","scaffold":"Cyclical unemployment rises during recessions when aggregate demand and firms’ derived demand for labour fall. It may reduce as the economy recovers.","reforge":{"stem":"A recession causes restaurants to reduce staff because customers are spending less. This is best described as:","options":{"A":"Frictional unemployment","B":"Cyclical unemployment","C":"Seasonal unemployment","D":"Occupational mobility"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-unemp"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-UNEMP-05","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-UNEMP","stem":"Which is a cost of unemployment to the government?","options":{"A":"More benefit spending and lower tax revenue","B":"Higher income tax receipts from the unemployed","C":"An automatic rise in exports","D":"Lower demand for welfare payments"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-UNEMP-EFFECTS","scaffold":"Unemployment can reduce income tax and national insurance receipts while increasing benefit payments. The government also faces the opportunity cost of using funds to support people out of work.","reforge":{"stem":"How can high unemployment affect public finances?","options":{"A":"Scarcity affects only households; poverty affects only firms","B":"It has no effect on government finances","C":"Tax receipts may fall while spending on benefits rises","D":"Benefit spending automatically falls to zero"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-unemp"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-UNEMP-06","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-UNEMP","stem":"Why can job losses damage a local economy?","options":{"A":"Local firms are unaffected by household income","B":"Lower household income can reduce spending at local businesses","C":"It guarantees the firm a profit whatever it chooses to produce and sell","D":"Job losses automatically attract investment"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-UNEMP-EFFECTS","scaffold":"Redundancies reduce incomes and spending, which can lower sales for other local firms. Persistent unemployment may also reduce skills, health and investment in the area.","reforge":{"stem":"A factory closes and nearby shops lose customers. This illustrates how unemployment can:","options":{"A":"Raise productivity immediately","B":"A market in which there is no economic cost to anyone involved at all","C":"Increase local disposable income","D":"Create a multiplier effect of lower spending in the local economy"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-unemp"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-UNEMP-07","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-UNEMP","stem":"Which policy could reduce structural unemployment?","options":{"A":"The worker isn't unemployed at all — they have chosen to become economically inactive and have left the labour force entirely","B":"Banning labour mobility","C":"Retraining workers for occupations with growing demand","D":"Reducing all education spending"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-UNEMP-TYPES","scaffold":"Retraining and better education can reduce skills mismatch. Relocation support, improved transport and information can also help workers move towards available vacancies.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might subsidised adult training reduce unemployment?","options":{"A":"It can give displaced workers skills that match vacancies in expanding sectors","B":"Cyclical unemployment, caused by a recession reducing demand for labour across the whole economy","C":"It increases skills mismatch","D":"It guarantees every firm makes a profit"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-unemp"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-UNEMP-08","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-UNEMP","stem":"Which person is economically inactive rather than unemployed?","options":{"A":"Someone temporarily between jobs and searching","B":"Someone actively seeking work and available to start","C":"Someone who has applied for three jobs this week","D":"Someone retired and not looking for paid work"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-UNEMP-MEASURE","scaffold":"Unemployed people are without work but available for work and actively seeking it. People who are not working and not seeking or available for work may be economically inactive, such as some retirees or full-time carers.","reforge":{"stem":"Which person should not normally be included in the unemployment rate?","options":{"A":"A laid-off worker applying for vacancies","B":"A student who is not seeking or available for a job","C":"A graduate attending interviews","D":"Poverty exists in rich countries but scarcity does not"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-unemp"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-UNEMP-09","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-UNEMP","stem":"A labour force has 24 million people and 1.2 million are unemployed. What is the unemployment rate?","options":{"A":"5%","B":"0.05%","C":"20%","D":"25.2%"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-UNEMP-MEASURE","scaffold":"Unemployment rate = unemployed people / labour force x 100. Therefore 1.2 / 24 x 100 = 5%.","reforge":{"stem":"A labour force of 20 million includes 800,000 unemployed people. What is the unemployment rate?","options":{"A":"25%","B":"0.4%","C":"4%","D":"20.8%"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-unemp"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-UNEMP-10","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-UNEMP","stem":"A worker loses a job because demand permanently moves away from their industry. What type of unemployment is this?","options":{"A":"Seasonal","B":"Structural","C":"Frictional","D":"Voluntary"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-UNEMP-TYPES","scaffold":"Structural unemployment arises when the pattern of demand or production changes and workers' skills no longer match available jobs.","reforge":{"stem":"A restaurant cuts staff during a recession because customers spend less. This is:","options":{"A":"Occupational mobility","B":"Structural unemployment","C":"Seasonal unemployment","D":"Cyclical unemployment"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-unemp"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-UNEMP-11","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-UNEMP","stem":"Which person is most likely to be economically inactive?","options":{"A":"Someone available to start work tomorrow","B":"Someone applying for jobs","C":"A retired person not seeking paid work","D":"Someone waiting to begin a confirmed job"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-UNEMP-MEASURE","scaffold":"Economically inactive people are not working and are not actively seeking or available for work. Some retirees, full-time carers and students may be economically inactive.","reforge":{"stem":"Why can full employment exist even when unemployment is not zero?","options":{"A":"Some frictional unemployment may remain","B":"Full employment means firms stop recruiting","C":"Unemployment is counted twice","D":"Firms stop recruiting"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-unemp"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-UNEMP-12","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-UNEMP","stem":"How can high unemployment affect the government budget?","options":{"A":"Someone actively seeking work and available to start","B":"Exports always increase","C":"Benefit spending falls to zero","D":"Tax receipts may fall while benefit spending rises"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-UNEMP-EFFECTS","scaffold":"Unemployment can reduce income tax and national insurance receipts while increasing spending on benefits. This can worsen the government's budget position.","reforge":{"stem":"A factory closure causes nearby shops to lose customers. What does this illustrate?","options":{"A":"Unemployment always raises local sales","B":"Lower incomes can reduce local demand","C":"A rise in labour productivity","D":"An increase in household wealth"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-unemp"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-UNEMP-13","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-UNEMP","stem":"Which policy is most likely to reduce structural unemployment?","options":{"A":"Retraining workers for expanding industries","B":"Seasonal unemployment, tied to the time of year","C":"Reducing careers information","D":"Closing colleges"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-UNEMP-TYPES","scaffold":"Retraining can reduce skills mismatch by helping workers gain qualifications needed in expanding occupations. Better transport and job information may also help.","reforge":{"stem":"A ski instructor is out of work during summer but expects a winter job. This is:","options":{"A":"Cyclical unemployment","B":"Structural unemployment","C":"Seasonal unemployment","D":"Technological unemployment"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-unemp"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-UNEMP-14","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-UNEMP","stem":"How can unemployment affect producers in a region?","options":{"A":"It guarantees higher sales","B":"Lower household incomes may reduce demand","C":"Higher income tax receipts from the unemployed","D":"It always raises investment"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-UNEMP-EFFECTS","scaffold":"Job losses reduce household incomes and spending. Local firms may therefore experience lower sales, although producers may also face lower wage costs when labour is plentiful.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might producers benefit from a larger pool of unemployed workers?","options":{"A":"Tax receipts must rise because fewer people work","B":"They receive automatic subsidies","C":"They face no competition","D":"They may be able to recruit at lower wages"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-unemp"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-UNEMP-15","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-UNEMP","stem":"What can cause regional differences in unemployment?","options":{"A":"Workers always move immediately","B":"Someone actively seeking work and available to start","C":"Workers may be unable to relocate to available jobs","D":"All firms pay the same wage"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-IMMOBILITY","scaffold":"Geographical immobility, housing costs, family responsibilities and transport limitations can prevent workers moving to areas with vacancies.","reforge":{"stem":"Automation permanently replaces some routine jobs. This is most likely to create:","options":{"A":"Structural unemployment","B":"Seasonal unemployment","C":"Frictional unemployment","D":"Voluntary unemployment"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-unemp"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-UNEMP-16","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-UNEMP","stem":"What is the correct view of the unemployment rate?","options":{"A":"Only a physical shop or store where goods are sold to buyers","B":"It includes every retired person","C":"It measures only long-term unemployment","D":"It is the number unemployed divided by the labour force"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-UNEMP-MEASURE","scaffold":"The unemployment rate is unemployed people divided by the labour force, multiplied by 100. The labour force includes people in work and those actively seeking and available for work.","reforge":{"stem":"Which person would normally be included in the unemployment rate?","options":{"A":"A student not seeking work","B":"A worker actively seeking and available for a job","C":"A retired person not available for work","D":"It is unemployed people divided by total population"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-unemp"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-UNEMP-17","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-UNEMP","stem":"What is the labour force?","options":{"A":"The people who work in a country","B":"Only people who are retired","C":"All children and students","D":"Better public transport into the city"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-LABOUR-SUPPLY","scaffold":"The supplied definitions describe the labour force or workforce as the number of people who work in a country. In wider labour-market analysis, people actively seeking work are also considered economically active.","reforge":{"stem":"Which group is part of the supply of labour?","options":{"A":"Every market in the economy has exactly one seller operating within it at any time","B":"Only company directors","C":"People willing and eligible to offer labour, including the unemployed","D":"People who cannot work"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-unemp"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-UNEMP-18","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-UNEMP","stem":"What does supply of labour refer to?","options":{"A":"The number of vacancies","B":"People willing and eligible to supply their labour","C":"The wages paid after tax","D":"It raises household spending power rather than the economy's capacity"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-LABOUR-SUPPLY","scaffold":"Supply of labour is the total number of people willing and eligible to offer their labour. It includes people who are unemployed but available and seeking work.","reforge":{"stem":"What is a labour market?","options":{"A":"A market for imported products","B":"Raises the wage employers must offer to attract new workers","C":"A market for government bonds","D":"A market where workers sell labour and employers buy it"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-unemp"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-UNEMP-19","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-UNEMP","stem":"Which person is most clearly part of the available labour force?","options":{"A":"A retired person not seeking work","B":"A child below working age","C":"A worker actively offering labour for employment","D":"It raises household spending power rather than the economy's capacity"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-LABOUR-SUPPLY","scaffold":"The labour market concerns people offering labour and employers demanding it. A person actively available for employment is part of the potential supply of labour.","reforge":{"stem":"What happens in a labour market?","options":{"A":"Workers sell labour while employers buy labour","B":"Only final goods are exchanged","C":"Raises the wage employers must offer to attract new workers","D":"Banks exchange currencies"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-unemp"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-UNEMP-20","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-UNEMP","stem":"A student is revising labour supply. Which explanation is correct?","options":{"A":"It is the income of retired people","B":"Firms in competition always set exactly identical prices to each other in every market","C":"It is the tax paid by employees","D":"It is the total number of people willing and eligible to supply labour"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-LABOUR-SUPPLY","scaffold":"Labour supply is the number of people willing and eligible to offer work, including the unemployed. Its size can be affected by wages, population and migration.","reforge":{"stem":"Which person is most likely to be outside the supply of labour?","options":{"A":"Someone ready to start work","B":"Someone unavailable for work and not seeking a job","C":"Someone applying for vacancies","D":"It raises household spending power rather than the economy's capacity"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-unemp"}]},"GCSE-ECON-P2-INCOME":{"label":"Paper 2: Fair Distribution of Income","color":"#22c55e","questions":[{"id":"GCSE-P2-INCOME-01","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-INCOME","stem":"What is the difference between income and wealth?","options":{"A":"Income is a flow over time; wealth is the stock of assets owned","B":"Income and wealth are exactly the same thing measured twice over","C":"Wealth is only the wages earned; income is only property owned","D":"Income is always a stock measured at one date"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-INCOME-VS-WEALTH","scaffold":"Income is money received over a period, such as wages or rent. Wealth is the value of accumulated assets minus liabilities, such as property, savings and shares.","reforge":{"stem":"Which is an example of wealth rather than income?","options":{"A":"The salary paid to a worker this month","B":"Interest received during this year","C":"A house owned by a household","D":"A weekly benefit payment received"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-income"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-INCOME-02","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-INCOME","stem":"What does the median income represent?","options":{"A":"The total income received by the government","B":"The middle value when incomes are ordered","C":"The highest income earned anywhere in the economy","D":"The total of all incomes added together"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-INEQUALITY","scaffold":"The median is the middle observation in an ordered list. It can represent a typical household better than the mean when a few very high incomes pull the average upwards.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might analysts prefer median household income to mean income when describing a typical household?","options":{"A":"The median is the highest value in the whole distribution","B":"It ignores every low income in the distribution","C":"The median always includes household wealth as well as income","D":"A few very high incomes have less influence on the median"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-income"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-INCOME-03","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-INCOME","stem":"Why might a professional sportsperson earn a very high wage?","options":{"A":"The supply of their labour is effectively unlimited","B":"Wages are entirely unrelated to the demand for labour","C":"Their scarce skills may face high demand from a large market","D":"High wages always reflect low productivity among the workers involved"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-WAGE-DIFFERENTIALS","scaffold":"Wage differentials can reflect skill, training, scarcity, demand for the output, responsibility and bargaining power. High earnings may arise where a worker’s talent is scarce and the market is large.","reforge":{"stem":"Which combination is most likely to raise a worker’s wage?","options":{"A":"High demand for the worker’s output and a scarce skill","B":"No training required and no responsibility held","C":"A complete absence of employers in the market","D":"Low demand for output and many identical workers available"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-income"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-INCOME-04","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-INCOME","stem":"Which is a possible consequence of a large gap between high and low incomes?","options":{"A":"Identical housing for every household in the country","B":"An automatic rise in social mobility across all groups","C":"No differences in health between groups","D":"Greater poverty and unequal access to opportunities"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-INEQUALITY","scaffold":"Large income differences may be associated with relative poverty, unequal housing, education and healthcare outcomes, and differences in life chances. The size of these effects depends on public services and redistribution.","reforge":{"stem":"How can income inequality affect opportunity?","options":{"A":"It guarantees that all children in the country start out with exactly the same resources","B":"Low-income families may have less access to education, housing or healthcare","C":"It removes all differences in living standards between households","D":"It makes poverty impossible to measure in any reliable way"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-income"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-INCOME-05","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-INCOME","stem":"What does a progressive income tax do?","options":{"A":"Raises the average tax rate as income rises","B":"Charges every household the same cash amount","C":"Takes a higher proportion from low earners","D":"Only taxes wealth, never income"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-TAX-TYPES","scaffold":"Progressive taxation can redistribute income because higher earners pay a larger proportion. Disposable income becomes more equal, although tax design can affect incentives and administrative costs.","reforge":{"stem":"Which policy is most directly redistributive?","options":{"A":"Charging exactly the same flat amount of tax to every household in the country each year","B":"Removing public education funding from schools","C":"Using higher tax rates on higher incomes to fund benefits for lower-income households","D":"Reducing all welfare payments to low-income households"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-income"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-INCOME-06","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-INCOME","stem":"If the richest 20% receive five times the income of the poorest 20%, what does this indicate?","options":{"A":"The economy has no poverty","B":"Income is unequally distributed","C":"The two groups have equal incomes","D":"Wealth is measured exactly"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-INEQUALITY","scaffold":"Comparing income shares between groups shows distribution and inequality. It does not by itself reveal the absolute living standards, wealth or public services available to each group.","reforge":{"stem":"What does an income-share comparison tell an economist?","options":{"A":"Only the rate of price inflation","B":"The number of firms operating in an industry","C":"The exact wealth held by every single household","D":"How total income is divided between groups"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-income"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-INCOME-07","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-INCOME","stem":"Why may wealth inequality be greater than income inequality?","options":{"A":"Income cannot be saved or turned into assets","B":"All households own an identical set of assets and always have done","C":"Assets can accumulate and generate further returns over time","D":"Wealth is paid out only once a week, like a wage"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-INCOME-VS-WEALTH","scaffold":"People who already own property, shares or businesses may receive capital gains and investment income, allowing wealth to compound. Inheritance can also pass assets between generations.","reforge":{"stem":"Which mechanism can widen wealth differences between households?","options":{"A":"House price rises benefiting existing owners more than non-owners","B":"A fall in the price of every asset held","C":"Removing the ability of families to inherit any assets","D":"All households receiving exactly identical wages every single month"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-income"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-INCOME-08","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-INCOME","stem":"Which is a likely aim of welfare benefits?","options":{"A":"To make all workers earn exactly the same wage each week","B":"To eliminate the need for taxation altogether","C":"To increase absolute poverty among households","D":"To support household income and reduce hardship"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-REDISTRIBUTION","scaffold":"Benefits can provide a minimum income, insure against events such as unemployment and reduce poverty. They have a fiscal cost and may affect incentives depending on their design.","reforge":{"stem":"A benefit paid to households below a minimum income is intended primarily to:","options":{"A":"Raise the general price level directly","B":"Reduce poverty and support basic living standards","C":"Increase the size of the trade deficit","D":"Reward only the highest-income households in the economy"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-income"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-INC-09","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-INCOME","stem":"What does a rise in the income share of the richest fifth suggest?","options":{"A":"Income distribution has become less equal","B":"Every household has gained equally from growth","C":"Wealth has disappeared from the economy","D":"The bottom fifth must have a higher income"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-INEQUALITY","scaffold":"If the richest group receives a larger share of total income, the distribution has become more unequal unless other changes offset it.","reforge":{"stem":"Which change would show a more equal income distribution?","options":{"A":"Only the owners of assets gain any income","B":"The top fifth receives an even larger share of total income","C":"The lowest groups receive a greater share of total income","D":"Average income falls for every group in the distribution"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-income"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-INC-10","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-INCOME","stem":"What is the defining feature of a progressive income tax?","options":{"A":"Everyone pays exactly the same cash amount","B":"Higher earners pay a higher percentage of income","C":"Lower earners pay a higher percentage of their income","D":"Only firms pay the tax, not households"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-TAX-TYPES","scaffold":"A progressive tax takes a rising proportion of income as income rises. Revenue can fund public services or benefits that may redistribute income.","reforge":{"stem":"Which is an example of a direct tax?","options":{"A":"A tariff charged on imported goods","B":"VAT on everyday household purchases","C":"A duty charged on petrol","D":"Income tax deducted from earnings"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-income"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-INC-11","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-INCOME","stem":"Why can wealth be distributed less equally than income?","options":{"A":"Wealth cannot be saved or passed on","B":"Income is never paid to a household in cash","C":"Assets can generate interest and capital gains","D":"All households own an identical mix of assets already"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-INCOME-VS-WEALTH","scaffold":"People who own property, shares or savings may receive interest, rent or capital gains. Existing asset ownership can therefore reinforce differences in wealth and income.","reforge":{"stem":"How can tax revenue help reduce inequality?","options":{"A":"By funding services and benefits for lower-income households","B":"By ending all private saving by households","C":"By making every wage in the economy identical to all the others","D":"By preventing firms from trading with one another"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-income"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-INC-12","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-INCOME","stem":"What is a likely effect of reducing direct taxes?","options":{"A":"Every household's income must fall as a result","B":"Government revenue must increase overall","C":"The supply of labour must disappear entirely","D":"Households may have more disposable income"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-REDISTRIBUTION","scaffold":"Lower direct taxes can increase household disposable income and firms' retained profits. This may raise consumption or investment, but it can also reduce government revenue.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might higher taxes on high earners reduce inequality?","options":{"A":"They guarantee higher profits for every firm operating in the economy","B":"They can transfer resources through public spending and benefits","C":"They remove the need for public education spending","D":"They make low earners pay more than high earners do"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-income"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-INC-13","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-INCOME","stem":"Which pair contains two direct taxes?","options":{"A":"Income tax and corporation tax","B":"VAT and fuel duty on spending","C":"VAT and income tax combined","D":"Tariffs and purchase taxes on goods"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-TAX-TYPES","scaffold":"Direct taxes are charged on income, profits or wealth, such as income tax and corporation tax. VAT and duties are indirect taxes on spending or transactions.","reforge":{"stem":"Which tax is normally indirect?","options":{"A":"Inheritance tax","B":"Corporation tax","C":"VAT","D":"Income tax"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-income"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-INC-14","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-INCOME","stem":"Which policy is most likely to redistribute income?","options":{"A":"A higher tax-free allowance for all firms and workers","B":"Progressive taxation funding targeted benefits","C":"A ban on providing public services","D":"A flat charge levied on every household"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-REDISTRIBUTION","scaffold":"Progressive taxes collect a larger proportion from higher incomes, while targeted benefits and public services can support lower-income households.","reforge":{"stem":"What is one possible drawback of raising income tax on high earners?","options":{"A":"It must eliminate all inequality","B":"It prevents government from funding services","C":"It always increases disposable income","D":"It may weaken incentives to earn or invest"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-income"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-INC-15","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-INCOME","stem":"How might redistribution benefit lower-paid households?","options":{"A":"It guarantees that their private wages will double each year","B":"It removes all scarcity from the economy","C":"It can improve access to education and healthcare","D":"It prevents unemployment of every kind"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-REDISTRIBUTION","scaffold":"Revenue from taxation can fund benefits and public services. Better access to education and healthcare may raise living standards and future earning opportunities.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might a high marginal tax rate affect labour supply?","options":{"A":"Workers may have less incentive to work extra hours","B":"It makes all jobs unpaid for the worker","C":"It guarantees more working hours for every single worker","D":"It removes the need to pay any wages"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-income"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-INC-16","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-INCOME","stem":"What distinguishes income from wealth?","options":{"A":"Income and wealth are two identical measures of the same thing","B":"Wealth can be earned only from wages","C":"Income can never be used to buy any assets","D":"Income is a flow; wealth is a stock of assets"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-INCOME-VS-WEALTH","scaffold":"Income is received over a period, while wealth is the value of assets owned at a point in time. Wealth can generate income through rent, interest and capital gains.","reforge":{"stem":"Which development could improve equality of opportunity?","options":{"A":"Reducing access to schools for poorer pupils","B":"Providing high-quality education to disadvantaged pupils","C":"Increasing the barriers to healthcare for poorer households","D":"Removing training opportunities from public services"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-income"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-INC-17","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-INCOME","stem":"What does income and wealth distribution refer to?","options":{"A":"Government action, mainly through taxes and benefits, to reduce inequality","B":"The price of a typical basket of consumer goods","C":"The total value of one firm's annual sales","D":"The total number of workers employed across a whole industry in a given year"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-TAX-TYPES","scaffold":"Income and wealth distribution concerns government action, mainly taxation and benefits, to reduce inequalities in income and wealth.","reforge":{"stem":"What is a progressive tax?","options":{"A":"A tax taking the same cash amount from every person","B":"A tax on spending only","C":"A tax taking a greater percentage as income rises","D":"A tax paid by firms only"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-income"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-INC-18","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-INCOME","stem":"Which is a direct tax?","options":{"A":"VAT charged on a purchase","B":"A tax on income or wealth","C":"A duty on petrol sales","D":"A tariff charged on imports"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-TAX-TYPES","scaffold":"Direct taxes are charged on income or wealth. Taxes on spending, goods and services are indirect taxes.","reforge":{"stem":"Which is an indirect tax?","options":{"A":"Income tax on earnings","B":"Corporation tax on profits","C":"Inheritance tax on estates that are passed on","D":"A tax on spending on goods or services"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-income"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-INC-19","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-INCOME","stem":"What is a progressive tax?","options":{"A":"A tax charged as one fixed cash payment","B":"A tax charged on imported goods only","C":"A tax taking a greater percentage as income increases","D":"A tax that is paid only by consumers and never by firms"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-TAX-TYPES","scaffold":"A progressive tax takes a greater percentage of income as income rises. It can help redistribute income when combined with public spending and benefits.","reforge":{"stem":"How can government action reduce inequality of income and wealth?","options":{"A":"By using taxation and benefits","B":"By setting every wage identically","C":"By banning private saving","D":"By removing public services"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-income"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-INC-20","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-INCOME","stem":"Which policy is most directly linked to income and wealth distribution?","options":{"A":"Reducing the volume of imports only","B":"Changing the country's exchange rate","C":"Setting the price of a single product in one market","D":"Using taxes and benefits to reduce inequality"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-REDISTRIBUTION","scaffold":"Governments can influence distribution mainly through tax systems, benefits and public services. These policies affect the resources available to different groups.","reforge":{"stem":"Which item is a direct tax?","options":{"A":"A duty on fuel","B":"Income tax on wages","C":"VAT on a meal","D":"A tariff on imports"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-income"}]},"GCSE-ECON-P2-PRICE":{"label":"Paper 2: Price Stability","color":"#16a34a","questions":[{"id":"GCSE-P2-PRICE-01","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-PRICE","stem":"What does a positive inflation rate of 3% mean?","options":{"A":"The average price level is rising by about 3% over the period","B":"The student is correct — a lower inflation figure always means prices have fallen","C":"Prices are falling by 3%","D":"Real output is rising by 3%"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-INFLATION-DEF","scaffold":"Inflation is the rate of increase in the general price level. A positive rate means prices are still rising, even if some individual prices fall.","reforge":{"stem":"Inflation falls from 6% to 2%, but remains positive. What happens to the general price level?","options":{"A":"CPI only measures wages, not prices, so the statement doesn't apply","B":"It falls to zero","C":"It continues to rise, but more slowly","D":"It is unchanged"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-price"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-PRICE-02","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-PRICE","stem":"A basket costs £50 in the base year and £60 in the next year. What is the CPI in the next year?","options":{"A":"100","B":"120","C":"83","D":"110"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-CPI","scaffold":"CPI = cost of basket in current period divided by cost in base period x 100: £60 / £50 x 100 = 120. This indicates prices are 20% higher than in the base year.","reforge":{"stem":"A representative basket costs £80 in the base year and £88 later. Its CPI later is:","options":{"A":"100","B":"180","C":"90","D":"110"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-price"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-PRICE-03","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-PRICE","stem":"Why are goods given different weights when calculating the CPI?","options":{"A":"Weights measure unemployment","B":"Only luxury goods are included","C":"Households spend different proportions of their budgets on them","D":"Increase the demand for driving into the city at every possible price"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-CPI","scaffold":"The CPI uses a representative basket and weights items according to their share of household spending. A change in the price of a frequently purchased necessity can have a larger effect on household budgets.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might food receive a larger CPI weight than a rare leisure purchase?","options":{"A":"Food usually represents a larger and more regular share of household spending","B":"Leisure goods are excluded from inflation","C":"Government orders that fix the level of output and the price charged for every single good","D":"Food prices never change"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-price"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-PRICE-04","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-PRICE","stem":"Who is most likely to lose from unexpectedly high inflation?","options":{"A":"A business that faces no competitors at all in its industry","B":"A worker whose wages rise faster than prices","C":"A firm that raises its prices with its costs","D":"A person whose cash savings earn interest below inflation"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-INFLATION-EFFECTS","scaffold":"If inflation exceeds the interest rate on savings, the real value and purchasing power of those savings fall. Borrowers may gain if nominal debts become easier to repay in real terms, though lenders lose.","reforge":{"stem":"If savings earn 2% interest while inflation is 5%, the real return is approximately:","options":{"A":"+3%","B":"-3%","C":"+7%","D":"-7%"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-price"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-PRICE-05","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-PRICE","stem":"What is demand-pull inflation?","options":{"A":"Prices rising because aggregate demand grows faster than productive capacity","B":"Prices falling because demand collapses","C":"Economies of scale increase every new entrant's costs by exactly the same amount each year","D":"A reduction in the money supply"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-INFLATION-CAUSES","scaffold":"Demand-pull inflation occurs when spending pressure exceeds the economy’s ability to increase output, especially near capacity. Cost-push inflation instead originates from rising production costs.","reforge":{"stem":"Households increase spending rapidly while factories are already near full capacity. This may cause:","options":{"A":"Deflation caused by excess supply","B":"A fall in aggregate demand","C":"Demand-pull inflation","D":"Structural unemployment only"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-price"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-PRICE-06","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-PRICE","stem":"What is deflation?","options":{"A":"An increase in real GDP","B":"A sustained fall in the general price level","C":"Inflation falling to 3% means prices have fallen by exactly 3%","D":"A rise in the price level"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-INFLATION-DEF","scaffold":"Deflation means the general price level falls. It can encourage consumers to delay purchases and increase the real burden of debt, although small price falls for particular goods are not necessarily deflation.","reforge":{"stem":"Prices fall across the economy for a sustained period. This is known as:","options":{"A":"Demand-pull inflation","B":"Hyper-employment","C":"Disinflation","D":"Deflation"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-price"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-PRICE-07","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-PRICE","stem":"Which group may benefit from unanticipated inflation?","options":{"A":"A lender receiving fixed repayments","B":"A worker whose wage is fixed forever","C":"A borrower with a fixed-rate loan","D":"A saver with a fixed nominal return"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-INFLATION-EFFECTS","scaffold":"Unexpected inflation reduces the real value of fixed nominal repayments, which can benefit borrowers and disadvantage lenders. The effect depends on contracts and whether incomes adjust.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might inflation help a household with a fixed-rate mortgage?","options":{"A":"The real value of its outstanding debt can fall as prices and incomes rise","B":"The lender receives more purchasing power","C":"Inflation guarantees a lower interest rate","D":"Consumers will always receive far too much choice in the market as a result"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-price"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-PRICE-08","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-PRICE","stem":"Why is price stability an economic policy objective?","options":{"A":"It guarantees high growth every year","B":"Its nominal repayments automatically become zero","C":"It removes all unemployment","D":"It makes planning, saving and investment easier"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-INFLATION-EFFECTS","scaffold":"Stable prices reduce uncertainty for households and firms. Very high or volatile inflation can distort decisions, redistribute income unpredictably and damage international competitiveness.","reforge":{"stem":"Why can volatile inflation make business planning more difficult?","options":{"A":"It fixes every input cost","B":"Firms cannot predict future costs, prices and returns as confidently","C":"It makes demand perfectly predictable","D":"Every firm in the market charging exactly the same price as the others"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-price"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-PRI-09","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-PRICE","stem":"What does CPI inflation measure?","options":{"A":"The percentage change in a representative basket of consumer prices","B":"Small firms automatically receive bigger discounts from their suppliers","C":"The level of exports only","D":"The exchange rate of the pound"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-CPI","scaffold":"CPI measures the change in the price of a representative basket of goods and services bought by households. It is used to estimate the rate of inflation.","reforge":{"stem":"Which option describes positive inflation?","options":{"A":"Every price is unchanged","B":"The average price level is falling","C":"The average price level is rising","D":"Only wages are rising"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-price"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-PRI-10","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-PRICE","stem":"Which is a likely cause of cost-push inflation?","options":{"A":"Prices falling because demand collapses","B":"Higher energy and raw-material costs","C":"A rise in spare capacity","D":"Lower import prices"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-INFLATION-CAUSES","scaffold":"Cost-push inflation occurs when firms face higher costs and raise prices, for example after increases in energy, wages or imported raw materials.","reforge":{"stem":"Which change is most likely to cause demand-pull inflation?","options":{"A":"Prices rising only because imported materials cost more","B":"A fall in aggregate demand","C":"Lower consumer confidence","D":"Rapid growth in spending beyond productive capacity"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-price"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-PRI-11","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-PRICE","stem":"What is one effect of unexpectedly high inflation?","options":{"A":"It is completely unchanged from the level of the previous year","B":"Every saver gains","C":"The purchasing power of money may fall","D":"Real wages must rise"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-INFLATION-DEF","scaffold":"When prices rise faster than expected, a given amount of money buys fewer goods and services. The effect on savers, borrowers and workers depends on contracts and whether incomes keep pace.","reforge":{"stem":"What happens when the inflation rate falls from 6% to 3% but remains positive?","options":{"A":"Prices rise more slowly","B":"Prices fall by 3%","C":"It is falling steadily each month","D":"Deflation occurs"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-price"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-PRI-12","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-PRICE","stem":"Why might deflation reduce consumer spending?","options":{"A":"The student is correct — higher interest rates always increase household spending in every case","B":"Borrowing becomes impossible in every case","C":"Firms face no uncertainty","D":"Consumers may delay purchases expecting lower prices"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-MONETARY","scaffold":"If people expect prices to fall, they may postpone purchases. Lower spending can reduce firms' revenues and output, although the effects depend on the cause and duration of deflation.","reforge":{"stem":"Which policy is commonly used to reduce demand-pull inflation?","options":{"A":"Lowering interest rates","B":"Raising interest rates","C":"Cutting taxes and raising spending","D":"Increasing consumer credit"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-price"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-PRI-13","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-PRICE","stem":"A 10% wage rise follows a 6% productivity rise. What pressure may result?","options":{"A":"Cost-push inflation","B":"A guaranteed fall in prices","C":"Lower unit labour costs","D":"No possible change in costs"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-INFLATION-CAUSES","scaffold":"If wages rise faster than output per worker, unit labour costs may rise. Firms may respond by raising prices, creating cost-push inflationary pressure.","reforge":{"stem":"How can an indirect tax on a product reduce its negative externality?","options":{"A":"Prices rising only because imported materials cost more","B":"It makes production costless","C":"It shifts supply left and raises the market price","D":"It guarantees consumption rises"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-price"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-PRI-14","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-PRICE","stem":"Which policy can reduce inflation by lowering aggregate demand?","options":{"A":"Lower income tax","B":"Higher interest rates","C":"Higher government spending","D":"A larger budget deficit"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-SUPPLYSIDE","scaffold":"Higher interest rates can reduce borrowing and consumption while encouraging saving. Lower aggregate demand may reduce demand-pull inflation.","reforge":{"stem":"Which supply-side measure may reduce cost pressures over time?","options":{"A":"Lower worker productivity","B":"Education always reduces potential output","C":"Less competition between firms","D":"Investment in skills and infrastructure"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-price"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-PRI-15","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-PRICE","stem":"If a country's inflation rate is -0.5%, what has happened to its general price level?","options":{"A":"It has risen by 0.5%","B":"It is unchanged","C":"It has fallen on average","D":"It has fallen all the way to zero"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-INFLATION-DEF","scaffold":"A negative inflation rate indicates deflation: the average price level has fallen over the period, although individual prices may still rise.","reforge":{"stem":"The inflation rate falls from 8% to 3%. What is the absolute change in the rate?","options":{"A":"5 percentage points","B":"3 percentage points","C":"11 percentage points","D":"0.5 percentage points"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-price"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-PRI-16","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-PRICE","stem":"Which group may lose from unexpected inflation?","options":{"A":"Borrowers with fixed-rate debt","B":"Its nominal repayments automatically become zero","C":"Firms able to raise prices fully","D":"Savers whose interest income does not keep pace"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-INFLATION-EFFECTS","scaffold":"Unexpected inflation reduces the real value of fixed nominal payments. Savers may lose purchasing power if their interest income rises more slowly than prices.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the most accurate view of price stability?","options":{"A":"It means every price is identical","B":"It supports planning by reducing uncertainty about purchasing power","C":"They prevent consumers from comparing prices between the rival stores","D":"It prevents all changes in relative prices"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-price"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-PRI-17","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-PRICE","stem":"What does cost of living refer to?","options":{"A":"The price level of goods and services bought by an average family","B":"The total income of a firm","C":"The price of exports only","D":"Every single product in the shops has exactly the same price as every other one"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-INFLATION-DEF","scaffold":"Cost of living describes the price level of goods and services bought by an average family. It is affected by changes in prices and household spending patterns.","reforge":{"stem":"What is inflation?","options":{"A":"Prices fall rapidly in every year that passes","B":"A rise in one product's price","C":"A sustained rise in the general price level","D":"A fall in household income"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-price"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-PRI-18","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-PRICE","stem":"What is price stability?","options":{"A":"The student is correct — a lower inflation figure always means prices have fallen","B":"The general price level stays constant or rises at a low acceptable rate","C":"Prices fall rapidly","D":"Wages never change"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-INFLATION-DEF","scaffold":"Price stability means the general level of prices is constant or rising at an acceptably low rate. Individual prices can still change.","reforge":{"stem":"What does the rate of inflation measure?","options":{"A":"The total number of firms that are operating within a market","B":"The price of one good","C":"The number of households","D":"The percentage rise in the general price level over time"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-price"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-PRI-19","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-PRICE","stem":"What is the consumer price index used for?","options":{"A":"Calculating total national output","B":"Measuring a firm's productivity","C":"Calculating the rate of inflation","D":"Finding the exchange rate"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-CPI","scaffold":"The consumer price index is a method used to calculate the rate of inflation by tracking changes in a representative basket of consumer prices.","reforge":{"stem":"Which phrase best describes the cost of living?","options":{"A":"The price level of goods and services bought by an average family","B":"An automatic increase in the incomes of all consumers in the market","C":"The value of a country's exports","D":"The rate of interest"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-price"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-PRI-20","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-PRICE","stem":"Which option defines inflation?","options":{"A":"A rise in employment","B":"Wages never change from one year to the next","C":"A fall in GDP","D":"A sustained rise in the general price level"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-INFLATION-DEF","scaffold":"Inflation is a sustained rise in the general price level over time. It is not the same as a single price increase.","reforge":{"stem":"What does price stability allow households and firms to experience?","options":{"A":"CPI only measures wages, not prices, so the statement doesn't apply","B":"A relatively constant or slowly rising general price level","C":"Falling output every year","D":"Zero changes in wages"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-price"}]},"GCSE-ECON-P2-FISCAL":{"label":"Paper 2: Fiscal Policy","color":"#15803d","questions":[{"id":"GCSE-P2-FISC-01","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-FISCAL","stem":"Which pair of actions is expansionary fiscal policy?","options":{"A":"Lower taxes and higher government spending","B":"Higher taxes and lower government spending","C":"Higher interest rates and lower money supply","D":"A tariff and an import quota"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-FISCAL","scaffold":"Expansionary fiscal policy increases aggregate demand through lower taxation, higher spending or both. It may support growth and employment during a downturn but can increase inflation or borrowing.","reforge":{"stem":"To stimulate demand during a recession, the government could:","options":{"A":"Reduce government borrowing to balance the budget quickly","B":"Raise the central bank interest rate","C":"Increase infrastructure spending or reduce income tax","D":"Raise taxes and reduce benefits"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-fiscal"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-FISC-02","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-FISCAL","stem":"Which is an example of an automatic stabiliser?","options":{"A":"A rise in income tax rates announced by the Chancellor in March","B":"Income tax receipts falling when incomes fall in a recession","C":"A one-off decision to build a new airport","D":"The central bank changing interest rates"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-AUTO-STABILISERS","scaffold":"Automatic stabilisers change the government’s budget without a new policy decision. In a downturn, tax receipts tend to fall and benefit payments rise, supporting aggregate demand.","reforge":{"stem":"During a recession, unemployment benefits rise automatically. This can:","options":{"A":"Make fiscal policy contractionary","B":"Increase the budget surplus because more benefits are paid out","C":"Reduce all government spending","D":"Support household incomes and partially stabilise demand"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-fiscal"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-FISC-03","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-FISCAL","stem":"What is the likely effect of contractionary fiscal policy?","options":{"A":"A rise in productive capacity through lower government borrowing","B":"An automatic increase in productive capacity","C":"Lower aggregate demand and reduced inflationary pressure","D":"Higher aggregate demand and more inflation"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-FISCAL","scaffold":"Higher taxes or lower government spending can reduce disposable income and spending, lowering aggregate demand. This may help control inflation but can weaken growth and employment.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might a government cut spending when inflation is persistently high?","options":{"A":"To reduce demand pressure on scarce resources","B":"To increase household disposable income","C":"To increase disposable income and encourage more spending","D":"To guarantee faster economic growth"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-fiscal"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-FISC-04","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-FISCAL","stem":"What is public debt?","options":{"A":"The value of all household mortgages","B":"The total borrowing of households and firms combined","C":"A single year’s trade deficit","D":"The accumulated borrowing of the government"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-BUDGET","scaffold":"Public debt is the stock of government borrowing built up over time. A budget deficit is a flow measured over a period; deficits can add to the debt, while surpluses can reduce it.","reforge":{"stem":"What distinguishes a deficit from debt?","options":{"A":"Debt is a yearly flow and deficit is an accumulated stock","B":"A deficit is a yearly shortfall; debt is the accumulated stock of borrowing","C":"A deficit is measured across ten years while debt is measured within a single year","D":"Neither relates to government finances"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-fiscal"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-FISC-05","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-FISCAL","stem":"How can government spending reduce unemployment?","options":{"A":"It can directly employ workers and raise demand for firms’ output","B":"It always reduces the labour force","C":"It makes all vacancies disappear","D":"It reduces the number of people who are willing to look for paid work"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-GOVT-SPENDING","scaffold":"Public-sector employment and spending on goods and services can raise demand, encouraging private firms to produce more and hire workers. The final effect depends on spare capacity and crowding out.","reforge":{"stem":"A government funds a new hospital and buys equipment from local firms. This may reduce unemployment through:","options":{"A":"A fall in private investment because firms lose contracts to the state","B":"Lower investment by every firm","C":"Direct jobs and higher derived demand for workers in supplying firms","D":"A reduction in the labour force"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-fiscal"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-FISC-06","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-FISCAL","stem":"What is crowding out?","options":{"A":"A fall in the price level from stronger competition","B":"Government borrowing contributing to higher interest rates and less private investment","C":"Government spending on public services replacing imported goods with domestically produced ones","D":"Firms hiring more workers after a tax cut"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-BUDGET","scaffold":"If government borrowing raises demand for loanable funds, interest rates may rise and private investment may be discouraged. Crowding out is less likely when the economy has spare capacity and interest rates are low.","reforge":{"stem":"Why could heavy government borrowing reduce private investment?","options":{"A":"It raises taxes on firms immediately, leaving them less retained profit","B":"It removes the need for finance","C":"It guarantees that firms receive more profits","D":"It may push up interest rates, making business loans more expensive"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-fiscal"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-FISC-07","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-FISCAL","stem":"Why may fiscal policy take time to affect the economy?","options":{"A":"Government departments are required to wait a full decade before changing any spending","B":"Fiscal policy affects only foreign economies","C":"Budgets must be designed, approved and implemented before spending changes","D":"Taxes change every minute automatically"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-POLICY-LAGS","scaffold":"Recognition, decision and implementation lags mean policy may affect demand after economic conditions have changed. This can make timing difficult.","reforge":{"stem":"A government announces a school-building programme, but construction starts a year later. This illustrates:","options":{"A":"A time lag between fiscal decision and its economic effect","B":"A fall in productive capacity caused by tax","C":"An automatic stabiliser working without any government decision","D":"Perfectly instant policy transmission"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-fiscal"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-FISC-08","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-FISCAL","stem":"Why might a government raise spending on education even if it creates a short-run deficit?","options":{"A":"Education always reduces potential output","B":"Borrowing for current spending always raises future productivity","C":"A deficit can never finance investment","D":"Human capital may raise future productivity and growth"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-SUPPLYSIDE","scaffold":"Spending on education can be investment in human capital. Better skills may increase productivity, employment and tax receipts over time, although the benefits are uncertain and the spending has an opportunity cost.","reforge":{"stem":"Which is an example of fiscal spending intended to improve long-run supply?","options":{"A":"Reducing all maintenance of public infrastructure","B":"Funding vocational training and skills development","C":"Increasing benefit payments to raise household spending now","D":"Cutting school access during a skills shortage"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-fiscal"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-FIS-09","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-FISCAL","stem":"Which policy is expansionary fiscal policy?","options":{"A":"Cutting income tax and increasing government spending","B":"Raising taxes and reducing spending","C":"Increasing taxes in order to fund higher government spending","D":"Reducing the money supply"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-FISCAL","scaffold":"Expansionary fiscal policy increases aggregate demand through lower taxation, higher government spending or both. It may be used to support growth during a slowdown.","reforge":{"stem":"Which policy is contractionary fiscal policy?","options":{"A":"Lower interest rates","B":"Lower taxes and more public spending","C":"Higher taxes and less public spending","D":"More government borrowing for consumption"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-fiscal"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-FIS-10","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-FISCAL","stem":"What can happen to the budget when unemployment rises?","options":{"A":"Government borrowing falls automatically as incomes decline","B":"Tax revenue may fall and benefit spending may rise","C":"Benefit spending must fall","D":"The budget automatically balances"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-AUTO-STABILISERS","scaffold":"Higher unemployment can reduce income tax and national insurance receipts while increasing benefit payments. This can widen a budget deficit.","reforge":{"stem":"Which policy is an automatic stabiliser during a downturn?","options":{"A":"A fall in all public services","B":"A fixed exchange-rate target","C":"A one-off decision to build a new hospital wing","D":"Benefits rising as more people lose work"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-fiscal"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-FIS-11","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-FISCAL","stem":"How can a rise in government spending increase short-run growth?","options":{"A":"It always lowers employment","B":"It reduces aggregate demand","C":"It may increase aggregate demand and output","D":"It raises the economy's productive capacity immediately"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-FISCAL","scaffold":"Government spending is a component of aggregate demand. If spare capacity exists, higher spending can increase sales, output and employment.","reforge":{"stem":"What is a possible drawback of expansionary fiscal policy near full capacity?","options":{"A":"It may add to inflationary pressure","B":"It must lower aggregate demand","C":"It guarantees deflation","D":"It automatically increases the economy's spare capacity"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-fiscal"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-FIS-12","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-FISCAL","stem":"Why can government spending on education support long-run growth?","options":{"A":"It reduces human capital","B":"It lowers the labour force","C":"It raises aggregate demand without affecting capacity","D":"It can raise worker productivity"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-BUDGET","scaffold":"Education can improve human capital and labour productivity. This may increase the economy's productive capacity as well as supporting employment and incomes.","reforge":{"stem":"Which action could increase a government's budget deficit?","options":{"A":"Lower spending with unchanged revenue","B":"Higher spending without matching extra revenue","C":"Higher tax revenue with unchanged spending","D":"Higher tax revenue combined with reduced public spending"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-fiscal"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-FIS-13","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-FISCAL","stem":"Which option describes a budget surplus?","options":{"A":"Tax revenue is greater than government spending","B":"Government spending is greater than tax revenue","C":"Exports exceed imports","D":"Households save more than firms"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-SCARCITY","scaffold":"A budget surplus occurs when government revenue exceeds government spending in a period. It is separate from the balance of payments.","reforge":{"stem":"What is an opportunity cost of increasing health spending?","options":{"A":"The health service receives resources","B":"The money value of the extra health spending itself","C":"The next best public use of those resources","D":"Every future tax receipt"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-fiscal"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-FIS-14","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-FISCAL","stem":"How does a progressive income tax redistribute income?","options":{"A":"It charges everyone an identical amount","B":"It takes a higher percentage from higher incomes","C":"It charges the same percentage of income at every level","D":"It pays benefits only to firms"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-TAX-TYPES","scaffold":"A progressive tax takes a greater proportion of income from higher earners. Revenue can then fund public services and benefits that support lower-income households.","reforge":{"stem":"Which is an indirect tax?","options":{"A":"Income tax charged on wages","B":"Inheritance tax paid on an estate","C":"Corporation tax on company profits","D":"VAT on a restaurant meal"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-fiscal"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-FIS-15","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-FISCAL","stem":"Which fiscal measure is most likely to support demand during a recession?","options":{"A":"Higher taxes on household income","B":"Raising interest rates to encourage more saving","C":"Temporary tax cuts or extra public spending","D":"A reduction in benefit payments"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-FISCAL","scaffold":"During a recession, expansionary fiscal policy can raise aggregate demand. The effect depends on the size, timing and targeting of the policy.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might a government hesitate to run a large deficit?","options":{"A":"Borrowing may raise future interest and repayment costs","B":"Borrowing removes the need for the government to collect tax revenue","C":"It always reduces current demand","D":"It eliminates public investment"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-fiscal"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-FIS-16","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-FISCAL","stem":"A rise in income tax is most likely to reduce household:","options":{"A":"The supply of money","B":"The number of imports automatically","C":"The size of the labour force to zero","D":"Disposable income"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-SUPPLYSIDE","scaffold":"Income tax reduces the income households have available after tax. This can reduce consumption, although the effect depends on confidence and the marginal propensity to consume.","reforge":{"stem":"Which government spending decision is most likely to be supply-side as well as fiscal?","options":{"A":"A short-term rise in benefit payments to households","B":"Long-term spending on transport infrastructure","C":"A temporary reduction in VAT only","D":"Paying interest on existing debt"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-fiscal"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-FIS-17","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-FISCAL","stem":"What is government spending?","options":{"A":"The total money spent by government during a period","B":"The money earned by all households","C":"The total amount that households pay in taxes each year","D":"The income of one public employee"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-GOVT-SPENDING","scaffold":"Government spending is the total amount of money spent by the government in a given period, such as on public services, benefits and investment.","reforge":{"stem":"What is government revenue?","options":{"A":"The cost of government borrowing","B":"The total money the government spends in a year","C":"A source of finance for government spending","D":"The price of public services"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-fiscal"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-FIS-18","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-FISCAL","stem":"What is a direct tax?","options":{"A":"A tax on spending","B":"A tax on income or wealth","C":"A charge on imported goods only","D":"A tax on one firm's output"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-TAX-TYPES","scaffold":"A direct tax is levied on income or wealth. An indirect tax is charged on spending, goods or services.","reforge":{"stem":"What is an indirect tax?","options":{"A":"A tax paid directly out of a person's earnings","B":"A tax on wealth","C":"Corporation tax","D":"A tax on spending on goods or services"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-fiscal"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-FIS-19","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-FISCAL","stem":"When is a government budget balanced?","options":{"A":"Spending is above revenue","B":"Revenue is above spending","C":"Tax revenue equals government spending","D":"Government borrowing equals household saving"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-BUDGET","scaffold":"A balanced government budget occurs when tax revenue is equal to government spending during the period.","reforge":{"stem":"What is a budget deficit?","options":{"A":"Government spending is greater than tax revenue","B":"Government borrowing is greater than household saving","C":"Household income falls","D":"Tax revenue is greater than spending"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-fiscal"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-FIS-20","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-FISCAL","stem":"What is fiscal policy?","options":{"A":"A policy using interest rates to control inflation","B":"A policy controlling imports only","C":"A policy that uses exchange-rate changes to control the level of prices","D":"A policy using government spending and taxation to affect the economy"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-BUDGET","scaffold":"Fiscal policy uses government spending and taxation to influence the economy as a whole. It differs from monetary policy, which uses the money supply and interest rates.","reforge":{"stem":"When is there a budget surplus?","options":{"A":"Government borrowing rises above the level of tax revenue","B":"Tax revenue is greater than government spending","C":"Spending is greater than tax revenue","D":"Government revenue is zero"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-fiscal"}]},"GCSE-ECON-P2-MONETARY":{"label":"Paper 2: Monetary Policy","color":"#16a34a","questions":[{"id":"GCSE-P2-MON-01","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-MONETARY","stem":"Which institution sets the UK base interest rate?","options":{"A":"The Bank of England’s Monetary Policy Committee","B":"Every commercial bank acting separately","C":"The Office for National Statistics, based in London","D":"The World Trade Organization in Geneva"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-BOE","scaffold":"The Monetary Policy Committee sets Bank Rate to help achieve the inflation target. Commercial banks then decide their own rates, influenced by the base rate and risk.","reforge":{"stem":"The UK base rate is changed by the:","options":{"A":"The Department for Transport","B":"The International Monetary Fund only","C":"Bank of England Monetary Policy Committee","D":"The Competition and Markets Authority only"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-monetary"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-MON-02","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-MONETARY","stem":"What is the likely effect of a rise in interest rates on household borrowing?","options":{"A":"Mortgage repayments must fall for every household with a loan","B":"Borrowing becomes more expensive and spending may fall","C":"Borrowing becomes free of any interest charge","D":"Saving becomes much less attractive to households"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-MONETARY","scaffold":"Higher interest rates raise the cost of new and variable-rate borrowing. Households may cut consumption or delay purchases, reducing aggregate demand.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might a higher interest rate reduce demand for new cars bought on credit?","options":{"A":"Households receive more disposable income each month","B":"Credit becomes unlimited for buyers","C":"The car becomes a merit good instead","D":"The finance repayments become more expensive"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-monetary"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-MON-03","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-MONETARY","stem":"Why might a rise in interest rates reduce inflation?","options":{"A":"It makes all imported goods more expensive to buy","B":"It guarantees a fall in wages across the economy","C":"It can reduce borrowing and spending, lowering demand pressure on prices","D":"It directly increases the productive capacity of every firm in the economy"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-MONETARY","scaffold":"The interest-rate transmission mechanism works through borrowing, saving, asset prices and exchange rates. Lower aggregate demand can reduce inflation, although supply shocks may remain.","reforge":{"stem":"If higher rates encourage saving and discourage consumption, the immediate effect on aggregate demand is likely to be:","options":{"A":"A fall","B":"No possible change","C":"A shift in long-run supply only","D":"A rise"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-monetary"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-MON-04","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-MONETARY","stem":"Which group may lose when interest rates rise?","options":{"A":"A saver receiving a higher return on deposits","B":"A person with no borrowing and no saving at all","C":"A lender making new loans to customers","D":"A household with a variable-rate mortgage"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-MONETARY","scaffold":"Higher rates increase repayments for variable-rate borrowers, reducing disposable income. Savers may benefit from higher returns, while the effect on firms depends on their borrowing.","reforge":{"stem":"A rise in mortgage rates is most likely to reduce the disposable income of:","options":{"A":"A household with no debt and no savings","B":"A household with a variable-rate mortgage","C":"A bank receiving interest","D":"A saver whose rate rises by more than inflation"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-monetary"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-MON-05","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-MONETARY","stem":"How might lower interest rates affect house prices?","options":{"A":"Cheaper mortgages can increase demand for houses","B":"They always reduce the demand for property everywhere","C":"They make borrowing impossible for households","D":"They affect only the level of exports"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-MONETARY","scaffold":"Lower rates can reduce mortgage costs and make borrowing more attractive, increasing housing demand. The effect on prices also depends on housing supply, incomes and expectations.","reforge":{"stem":"If mortgage rates fall while the supply of homes is fixed in the short run, house prices may:","options":{"A":"Fall to zero","B":"Become a measure of GDP","C":"Rise as demand increases","D":"Be unaffected in every case"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-monetary"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-MON-06","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-MONETARY","stem":"Why might a rise in UK interest rates strengthen the pound?","options":{"A":"It prevents all imports from entering the UK","B":"UK financial assets may become more attractive to overseas investors","C":"It makes UK exports automatically cheaper for every overseas buyer of them","D":"It reduces the demand for UK financial assets"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-RATES-AND-CURRENCY","scaffold":"Higher UK interest rates may increase demand for sterling-denominated savings and investments, raising demand for the pound. The exchange rate also depends on expectations and rates elsewhere.","reforge":{"stem":"A stronger pound following higher UK interest rates may make:","options":{"A":"Foreign holidays more expensive for UK residents","B":"All UK wages fall immediately","C":"UK exports cheaper for foreign buyers","D":"Imports cheaper for UK buyers"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-monetary"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-MON-07","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-MONETARY","stem":"Why can a rise in interest rates create a trade-off for the economy?","options":{"A":"It raises demand while also reducing borrowing costs","B":"It affects only the financial sector of the economy","C":"It may reduce inflation but also weaken growth and employment","D":"It always increases growth and lowers unemployment at the same time"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-MONETARY","scaffold":"Monetary policy can stabilise prices but weaker spending may reduce output and jobs. The size of the trade-off depends on the economy’s starting position and the cause of inflation.","reforge":{"stem":"If inflation is high but economic growth is already weak, a rate rise could:","options":{"A":"Lower price pressure while adding to the risk of recession","B":"Raise the level of household borrowing sharply in the short run","C":"Have no effect at all on aggregate demand","D":"Guarantee strong economic growth in future"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-monetary"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-MON-08","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-MONETARY","stem":"What is the main purpose of an inflation target?","options":{"A":"To guarantee that every individual price charged in the economy stays fixed","B":"To eliminate unemployment of every kind","C":"To set the sterling exchange rate permanently","D":"To provide a clear measure that guides monetary policy and expectations"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-BOE","scaffold":"An inflation target helps anchor expectations and gives the central bank a framework for deciding interest rates. It does not mean every price changes at the target rate.","reforge":{"stem":"Why can a credible inflation target help households and firms?","options":{"A":"It guarantees that prices in the shops never change from year to year","B":"It gives them a clearer basis for planning future prices and wages","C":"It fixes every mortgage rate in the country","D":"It removes supply shocks from the economy"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-monetary"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-MON-09","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-MONETARY","stem":"What is a likely effect of an increase in the central bank interest rate?","options":{"A":"Borrowing becomes dearer and saving more attractive","B":"Consumer spending must rise as a result","C":"The cost of mortgages falls for borrowers","D":"Inflation is guaranteed to increase almost straight away"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-MONETARY","scaffold":"Higher interest rates can reduce borrowing and consumption while encouraging saving. This may lower aggregate demand and inflationary pressure.","reforge":{"stem":"What is a likely effect of a cut in interest rates?","options":{"A":"The exchange rate must appreciate immediately","B":"Saving becomes more rewarding for households","C":"Borrowing may become cheaper and spending may rise","D":"Government spending automatically falls at the same time"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-monetary"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-MON-10","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-MONETARY","stem":"Which is a responsibility of a central bank?","options":{"A":"Setting the wage paid by every firm","B":"Using monetary policy to influence economic conditions","C":"Choosing how much each household spends every single week","D":"Producing all of the country's public services"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-BOE","scaffold":"A central bank can set the policy interest rate and support financial stability. It does not set every wage or decide how households spend.","reforge":{"stem":"Which action is an example of monetary policy?","options":{"A":"Building a new public hospital in a city","B":"Increasing spending on state schools","C":"Changing the rate of VAT","D":"Changing the official interest rate"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-monetary"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-MON-11","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-MONETARY","stem":"How can higher interest rates reduce demand-pull inflation?","options":{"A":"They reduce the incentive to save money","B":"They increase borrowing by households","C":"They can reduce consumption and investment","D":"They make government spending rise automatically"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-RATES-AND-CURRENCY","scaffold":"Higher rates make borrowing more expensive and saving more attractive. Lower consumption and investment can reduce aggregate demand and pressure on prices.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might higher UK interest rates increase demand for pounds?","options":{"A":"International investors may seek higher UK returns","B":"UK saving becomes less attractive to investors","C":"Imports automatically stop coming in","D":"The supply of pounds must fall to zero on the market"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-monetary"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-MON-12","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-MONETARY","stem":"A cut in UK interest rates is most likely to place what pressure on the pound?","options":{"A":"Upward pressure, because UK assets would now yield more than any others","B":"A guaranteed fixed value for sterling","C":"No pressure on the currency in any circumstance","D":"Downward pressure, as some investors seek better returns elsewhere"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-RATES-AND-CURRENCY","scaffold":"Lower interest rates can reduce the attractiveness of UK financial assets. Lower capital inflows may reduce demand for pounds, although the exchange rate depends on many factors.","reforge":{"stem":"How may higher interest rates affect inflation?","options":{"A":"They always raise wage costs for firms","B":"They may reduce spending and inflationary pressure","C":"They make prices fall towards zero","D":"They increase aggregate demand automatically in the economy"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-monetary"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-MON-13","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-MONETARY","stem":"Why might a central bank aim for low and stable inflation?","options":{"A":"It reduces uncertainty for households and firms","B":"It makes every product free of charge","C":"It guarantees a government budget surplus","D":"It prevents all relative-price changes in the economy"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-BOE","scaffold":"Stable prices make it easier for households and firms to plan spending, saving and investment. The objective is not zero change in every individual price.","reforge":{"stem":"Which institution normally sets the UK's base interest rate?","options":{"A":"The Office for National Statistics","B":"The Department for Transport","C":"The Bank of England","D":"The Competition and Markets Authority"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-monetary"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-MON-14","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-MONETARY","stem":"Who is most likely to lose from a rise in interest rates?","options":{"A":"A saver receiving more interest on deposits","B":"A borrower with a variable-rate mortgage","C":"A firm that carries no debt at all","D":"A household holding only cash savings"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-MONETARY","scaffold":"Higher rates raise interest payments on variable-rate borrowing. Savers may gain, while the effects on firms and households depend on their debts and savings.","reforge":{"stem":"Which household is most likely to benefit from higher savings rates?","options":{"A":"A household holding a variable-rate loan today","B":"A consumer relying on an overdraft","C":"A firm that needs new borrowing","D":"A household with a large deposit account"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-monetary"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-MON-15","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-MONETARY","stem":"How can monetary policy affect economic growth?","options":{"A":"Interest rates never affect the level of demand","B":"It changes only the wages that are paid in the public sector","C":"Lower rates may raise borrowing, spending and output","D":"It removes all supply constraints from the economy"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-MONETARY","scaffold":"Lower rates can encourage borrowing and reduce the reward for saving, raising consumption and investment. If spare capacity exists, output may increase.","reforge":{"stem":"What is a possible cost of keeping interest rates high for a long time?","options":{"A":"Weaker consumption and investment","B":"Lower debt-service costs","C":"More borrowing and stronger housebuilding","D":"Reduced saving"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-monetary"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-MON-16","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-MONETARY","stem":"Which is not a likely direct effect of higher interest rates?","options":{"A":"A greater incentive for households to save","B":"More expensive borrowing for households","C":"Lower consumer spending overall","D":"Cheaper repayments on variable-rate loans"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-MONETARY","scaffold":"Higher rates tend to reward saving and increase borrowing costs. Variable-rate borrowers usually face higher, not cheaper, repayments.","reforge":{"stem":"Which channel links interest rates to household demand?","options":{"A":"Higher rates make every household in the country richer","B":"Higher rates can reduce spending on credit-financed purchases","C":"Higher rates remove the need for any saving at all","D":"Higher rates guarantee faster wage growth for everyone who is in work"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-monetary"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-MON-17","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-MONETARY","stem":"What is monetary policy?","options":{"A":"A policy controlling the money supply to help achieve economic objectives","B":"A policy setting every wage paid in the economy","C":"A policy that controls only the level of imports coming into the country each year","D":"A policy for paying out public benefits"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-BOE","scaffold":"Monetary policy aims to control the total supply of money in the economy to help achieve government objectives, especially price stability.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the Bank of England's bank rate?","options":{"A":"The rate of interest that every shop charges its customers on its credit deals","B":"The rate of tax charged on savings income","C":"The rate set by the Bank of England on which commercial rates are based","D":"The exchange rate of sterling against the dollar"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-monetary"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-MON-18","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-MONETARY","stem":"What is the bank rate?","options":{"A":"The rate of pay that a firm gives to each of its own workers for every hour worked","B":"The rate set by the Bank of England that influences commercial interest rates","C":"The rate of inflation in the economy","D":"The average price of a house"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-BOE","scaffold":"The bank rate, or base rate, is set by the Bank of England. Commercial interest rates are based partly on it.","reforge":{"stem":"What does quantitative easing mean?","options":{"A":"A contraction in government spending","B":"A rise in income tax","C":"A fall in exports","D":"An expansion in the money supply"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-monetary"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-MON-19","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-MONETARY","stem":"What does quantitative easing involve?","options":{"A":"Reducing the total money supply","B":"Increasing direct taxes on income","C":"Expanding the money supply","D":"Fixing the exchange rate permanently"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-BOE","scaffold":"Quantitative easing is an expansion in the money supply. It is a monetary policy tool intended to support spending and economic activity when conventional rates are limited.","reforge":{"stem":"Which policy is primarily concerned with controlling money supply and price stability?","options":{"A":"Monetary policy","B":"Fiscal policy","C":"Trade policy","D":"Industrial policy"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-monetary"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-MON-20","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-MONETARY","stem":"Which change is an expansion of the money supply?","options":{"A":"A tariff on imports","B":"A rise in income tax","C":"A fall in government spending","D":"Quantitative easing"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-BOE","scaffold":"Quantitative easing expands the money supply. It is distinct from fiscal measures such as taxation and government spending.","reforge":{"stem":"Which institution sets the UK's bank rate?","options":{"A":"The Office for National Statistics","B":"The Bank of England","C":"The Treasury alone","D":"A commercial supermarket"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-monetary"}]},"GCSE-ECON-P2-SUPPLY":{"label":"Paper 2: Supply-side Policies","color":"#15803d","questions":[{"id":"GCSE-P2-SUP-01","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-SUPPLY","stem":"What is the main aim of supply-side policy?","options":{"A":"To increase productive capacity and productivity","B":"To reduce the economy’s ability to produce","C":"To set one price for every product","D":"To raise total spending in the economy in the short run"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-SUPPLYSIDE","scaffold":"Supply-side policies aim to improve the quantity or quality of factors of production and the efficiency of markets. They can shift long-run aggregate supply to the right.","reforge":{"stem":"A successful supply-side policy should generally:","options":{"A":"Reduce skills and investment","B":"Prevent firms entering markets","C":"Allow the economy to produce more at a given price level","D":"Raise the price level while leaving real output unchanged"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-supply"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-SUP-02","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-SUPPLY","stem":"How can education and training improve supply?","options":{"A":"They always reduce employment","B":"They can raise labour productivity and human capital","C":"They reduce workers’ skills","D":"They raise demand for goods rather than the ability to produce"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-SUPPLYSIDE","scaffold":"Education and training can improve skills, productivity and occupational mobility. This can raise productive capacity, although benefits may take time and public funding has an opportunity cost.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might vocational training reduce structural unemployment as well as raise supply?","options":{"A":"It reduces the quality of the workforce","B":"It makes firms less productive","C":"It cuts the number of people actively looking for work in each region","D":"It gives workers skills that match vacancies in expanding industries"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-supply"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-SUP-03","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-SUPPLY","stem":"Why might lower income tax encourage labour supply?","options":{"A":"It reduces the reward from working","B":"It always lowers employment","C":"People may keep more of each extra pound earned","D":"It raises government spending and so increases demand for labour"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-SUPPLYSIDE","scaffold":"Lower marginal tax rates may strengthen incentives to work more hours, gain skills or enter employment. The final effect depends on income effects, substitution effects and how tax revenue is used.","reforge":{"stem":"A supply-side argument for reducing corporation tax is that it may:","options":{"A":"Attract investment and increase firms’ productive capacity","B":"Make exports impossible","C":"Reduce the capital stock automatically","D":"Increase household spending directly by raising disposable incomes"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-supply"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-SUP-04","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-SUPPLY","stem":"How can improved broadband infrastructure increase productive capacity?","options":{"A":"It prevents firms trading online","B":"It mainly raises consumer demand for online shopping and entertainment","C":"It reduces access to information","D":"It can improve communication, remote working and business efficiency"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-SUPPLYSIDE","scaffold":"Infrastructure can reduce business costs, improve connectivity and allow new firms to enter markets. This can raise productivity and competition, although installation is expensive.","reforge":{"stem":"A reliable high-speed network allows firms to coordinate production more cheaply. This is likely to:","options":{"A":"Increase aggregate demand by encouraging extra household spending","B":"Raise productivity and shift productive capacity right","C":"Lower access to markets","D":"Make technology irrelevant"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-supply"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-SUP-05","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-SUPPLY","stem":"Why might privatisation be considered a supply-side policy?","options":{"A":"Private ownership may increase competition and efficiency in some industries","B":"It always makes markets less competitive","C":"It removes the profit motive","D":"Public ownership always delivers lower costs and better service than private firms"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-SUPPLYSIDE","scaffold":"Privatisation can introduce competition, stronger incentives and private investment, but outcomes depend on regulation and market structure. A natural monopoly may still require controls.","reforge":{"stem":"A possible risk of privatisation in a natural monopoly is that:","options":{"A":"Competition is guaranteed to be perfect","B":"Investment becomes impossible","C":"A private firm may exploit market power without effective regulation","D":"Competition appears automatically whenever an industry is sold to private owners"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-supply"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-SUP-06","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-SUPPLY","stem":"How could state-funded childcare increase potential output?","options":{"A":"It raises household spending power rather than the economy's capacity","B":"It may allow more parents to enter or increase hours in paid work","C":"It reduces labour force participation","D":"It makes caring costs higher for every family"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-LABOUR-SUPPLY","scaffold":"Affordable childcare can reduce a barrier to labour market participation, increasing the available workforce and potential output. It may also raise tax receipts and reduce benefit spending.","reforge":{"stem":"A childcare subsidy may increase labour supply because it:","options":{"A":"Reduces the number of working-age people","B":"Raises the wage employers must offer to attract new workers","C":"Makes employment less accessible","D":"Lowers the cost of taking a job for some parents"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-supply"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-SUP-07","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-SUPPLY","stem":"Why could a higher minimum wage have both positive and negative effects on supply?","options":{"A":"It raises workers' incomes and therefore always lowers firms' unit costs","B":"It prevents productivity improvements","C":"It may improve work incentives but raise firms’ labour costs","D":"It always reduces costs and raises unemployment"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-MIN-WAGE","scaffold":"Higher wages can attract workers, reduce turnover and encourage productivity. If set above the equilibrium wage, they can also raise costs and reduce employment or output in some firms.","reforge":{"stem":"A higher minimum wage may improve productivity when it:","options":{"A":"Motivates workers and reduces costly staff turnover","B":"Reduces the reward from working","C":"Forces firms to replace workers with machinery in every industry","D":"Makes every firm close"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-supply"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-SUP-08","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-SUPPLY","stem":"Why may supply-side policy take a long time to work?","options":{"A":"All policies change capacity instantly","B":"Capital can be created without investment","C":"Firms respond to every policy announcement by raising their output immediately","D":"Education, infrastructure and investment need time before productivity rises"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-POLICY-LAGS","scaffold":"Long-run policies often require planning, construction, training and changes in behaviour. Their benefits may be substantial but are uncertain and delayed.","reforge":{"stem":"Which policy is most likely to have a delayed effect on productive capacity?","options":{"A":"Changing a shop’s opening sign for one day","B":"Building a new rail network and training engineers","C":"Reducing a single consumer’s spending","D":"Cutting VAT for a single weekend of retail promotions"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-supply"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-SUP-09","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-SUPPLY","stem":"How can education increase an economy's productive potential?","options":{"A":"It can raise human capital and productivity","B":"It raises consumer demand for education services","C":"It reduces worker skills","D":"It guarantees lower demand"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-SUPPLYSIDE","scaffold":"Education and training improve human capital. More skilled workers may produce more output per hour, shifting productive capacity outward.","reforge":{"stem":"Which is a supply-side benefit of better vocational training?","options":{"A":"An immediate rise in spending by trainees and families","B":"Lower output per worker","C":"Skills that match vacancies more closely","D":"A permanent fall in investment"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-supply"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-SUP-10","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-SUPPLY","stem":"Why can improved transport infrastructure support long-run growth?","options":{"A":"It mainly boosts demand for construction in the short run","B":"It can lower travel costs and raise productivity","C":"It prevents all congestion immediately","D":"It guarantees a current account surplus"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-SUPPLYSIDE","scaffold":"Better roads, railways and broadband can connect workers and firms, reduce delays and make production more efficient. Benefits may take time and require large investment.","reforge":{"stem":"Which is a possible supply-side effect of reducing unnecessary regulation?","options":{"A":"The labour force must shrink","B":"Every firm must become state-owned","C":"Consumer protection improves automatically in every market","D":"Firms may face lower costs and more competition"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-supply"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-SUP-11","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-SUPPLY","stem":"Which policy may increase competition and productive efficiency?","options":{"A":"Setting a maximum price for every product sold","B":"Restricting new firms from entering","C":"Reducing unnecessary barriers to entry","D":"Giving one firm a permanent monopoly"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-SUPPLYSIDE","scaffold":"Lower barriers to entry can allow new firms to compete. Greater competition may encourage lower costs, innovation and better service, although some regulation protects consumers.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might privatisation increase efficiency in some cases?","options":{"A":"It can strengthen incentives to control costs","B":"Private firms never face competition","C":"It removes the need for any government regulation of the industry","D":"It guarantees higher output in every market"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-supply"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-SUP-12","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-SUPPLY","stem":"Which change is most likely to improve labour productivity?","options":{"A":"Fewer skills and less training","B":"A rise in household spending on finished goods","C":"More time spent waiting for inputs","D":"Investment in training and efficient capital"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-PRODUCTIVITY","scaffold":"Productivity is output per worker or per hour. Training and better machinery can raise output from a given amount of labour.","reforge":{"stem":"Which change is most likely to reduce productive capacity?","options":{"A":"A rise in worker skills","B":"The loss of skilled workers","C":"More reliable infrastructure","D":"New productive technology"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-supply"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-SUP-13","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-SUPPLY","stem":"How can research and development support economic growth?","options":{"A":"It may create new technology and products","B":"It raises current consumer spending rather than future capacity","C":"It lowers potential output by definition","D":"It affects only current tax receipts"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-GROWTH-CAUSES","scaffold":"Research and development can lead to innovation, more efficient production and new goods. These effects may raise productivity and productive capacity over time.","reforge":{"stem":"Which policy may improve geographical labour mobility?","options":{"A":"Restricting public transport","B":"Raising unemployment benefits for workers who stay in one region","C":"Providing relocation support and better housing access","D":"Closing regional colleges"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-supply"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-SUP-14","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-SUPPLY","stem":"Which is a likely long-run effect of higher labour productivity?","options":{"A":"Lower maximum output","B":"Higher productive capacity","C":"Less output from every worker","D":"An end to economic choices"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-PRODUCTIVITY","scaffold":"If workers produce more per hour, the economy can produce more from its available resources. This can raise potential output, although employment effects depend on demand and technology.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might better job information reduce structural unemployment?","options":{"A":"It removes all skills mismatch instantly","B":"It prevents workers changing occupation","C":"It guarantees every unemployed worker is matched within one month","D":"It can help workers find vacancies matching their skills"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-supply"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-SUP-15","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-SUPPLY","stem":"Which measure is most likely to raise the quantity and quality of labour?","options":{"A":"Reducing access to education","B":"Raising the school leaving age without extra teaching funding","C":"Funding training and lifelong learning","D":"Reducing workplace safety"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-SUPPLYSIDE","scaffold":"Training and lifelong learning can increase skills, employability and labour productivity. The effect depends on the quality and relevance of the provision.","reforge":{"stem":"Which is a possible drawback of supply-side reform?","options":{"A":"Some reforms have high upfront costs and uncertain results","B":"Reforms raise demand immediately but leave capacity unchanged","C":"Every reform lowers productivity","D":"Supply-side policy cannot affect capacity"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-supply"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-SUP-16","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-SUPPLY","stem":"Supply-side policies mainly aim to:","options":{"A":"Reduce the economy's productive capacity","B":"Set every market price","C":"Remove the role of firms","D":"Increase potential output and efficiency"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-SUPPLYSIDE","scaffold":"Supply-side policies seek to improve the quantity or quality of factors of production and the way resources are used. They can shift productive potential outward.","reforge":{"stem":"Which example is a supply-side policy?","options":{"A":"A permanent ban on investment","B":"A programme improving worker skills","C":"A one-day rise in consumer spending","D":"A temporary change in the exchange rate"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-supply"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-SUP-17","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-SUPPLY","stem":"How is productivity measured in the supplied definitions?","options":{"A":"Output divided by input","B":"Price divided by revenue","C":"Tax divided by wages","D":"Population divided by GDP"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-PRODUCTIVITY","scaffold":"Productivity measures the efficiency of factor use. A common calculation is output divided by input, such as output per worker.","reforge":{"stem":"What are economies of scale?","options":{"A":"All costs paid by a firm","B":"A fall in total output","C":"Cost advantages from increasing production scale that lower average cost","D":"The fall in average cost that happens purely by producing fewer units each year"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-supply"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-SUP-18","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-SUPPLY","stem":"What is average cost?","options":{"A":"The firm's total revenue","B":"The cost of producing one unit","C":"The firm's total profit","D":"The total spending of a firm on all of its output"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-REV-COST-PROFIT","scaffold":"Average cost is the unit cost of production. It can be calculated by dividing total cost by output.","reforge":{"stem":"What does total cost mean?","options":{"A":"The cost of the last unit a firm chooses to produce","B":"The cost of one unit","C":"The firm's profit","D":"All the costs of a firm added together"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-supply"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-SUP-19","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-SUPPLY","stem":"What is investment?","options":{"A":"Buying a good for immediate consumption","B":"Saving money without any future purpose","C":"Purchasing capital goods used to produce future output","D":"Placing money into a savings account in order to earn interest"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-EOS","scaffold":"Investment includes purchasing capital goods used to produce future goods and services. It can also mean buying an asset to generate future income or a profit.","reforge":{"stem":"Which option describes economies of scale?","options":{"A":"Cost advantages from increasing the scale of production","B":"The total revenue of an industry","C":"The fall in total output that follows a reduction in the workforce","D":"A rise in average cost as a firm expands"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-supply"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-SUP-20","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-SUPPLY","stem":"What does productivity measure?","options":{"A":"The number of imports","B":"The amount of tax collected","C":"The total value of goods and services an economy produces","D":"How efficiently factors of production are used"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-PRODUCTIVITY","scaffold":"Productivity is a measure of efficiency in the use of factors of production. It is often expressed as output per unit of input.","reforge":{"stem":"What is average cost?","options":{"A":"All costs added together","B":"The cost of producing one unit","C":"The firm's total income","D":"Total revenue divided by the number of units sold"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-supply"}]},"GCSE-ECON-P2-MARKETFAIL":{"label":"Paper 2: Limitations of Markets","color":"#16a34a","questions":[{"id":"GCSE-P2-MF-01","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-MARKETFAIL","stem":"What is a negative externality of consumption?","options":{"A":"A cost imposed on third parties by consumption","B":"A private benefit received by the consumer","C":"A tax on production paid only by the firm","D":"A benefit shared by everyone once the good is provided"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-EXTERNALITIES","scaffold":"A negative consumption externality occurs when consumption harms people not involved in the transaction, such as passive smoking or pollution. The social cost exceeds the private cost.","reforge":{"stem":"Second-hand smoke affecting people near a smoker is an example of:","options":{"A":"A positive externality of production","B":"A merit good the market under-provides","C":"A negative externality of consumption","D":"A private benefit to the smoker alone"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-marketfail"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-MF-02","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-MARKETFAIL","stem":"Which is a positive externality of consumption?","options":{"A":"A tax on imported goods paid by the buyer","B":"Benefits to others from an individual’s consumption","C":"A production cost that is paid directly by the firm itself","D":"A fall in the revenue earned by the producer"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-EXTERNALITIES","scaffold":"Education and vaccination can create benefits for third parties. Because consumers may consider only their private benefit, the market may provide less than the socially efficient quantity.","reforge":{"stem":"Why can education be under-consumed in a free market?","options":{"A":"Governments cannot afford school places for everyone who wants one","B":"Education is a demerit good that people consume too much of","C":"Education brings no private benefit to the individual who receives it, only costs","D":"Individuals may ignore benefits such as a more skilled and productive society"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-marketfail"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-MF-03","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-MARKETFAIL","stem":"What is the likely effect of a tax on sugary drinks?","options":{"A":"It shifts the demand curve to the right automatically","B":"It removes the external health costs of consumption entirely","C":"It raises firms’ costs and can reduce quantity demanded","D":"It makes sugary drinks cheaper for consumers"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-INTERVENTION","scaffold":"An indirect tax shifts supply up or left, raising the market price and reducing equilibrium quantity if demand responds. It may internalise some health costs but can be regressive.","reforge":{"stem":"An indirect tax on a polluting product is intended partly to:","options":{"A":"Make producers and consumers face more of the social cost","B":"Remove the private benefits the product gives to its buyer entirely","C":"Guarantee the government runs a larger budget deficit","D":"Increase consumption of the product among existing buyers"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-marketfail"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-MF-04","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-MARKETFAIL","stem":"Why may a subsidy be used for a good with positive externalities?","options":{"A":"To raise the price so that consumption falls to the level the market originally chose","B":"To punish the third parties who bear the external cost","C":"To reduce production of the good to the level society wants","D":"To lower the price and encourage consumption towards the socially desirable level"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-INTERVENTION","scaffold":"A subsidy lowers producers’ costs and can shift supply right, reducing price and increasing quantity. It may help goods such as education or healthcare, but it uses public funds.","reforge":{"stem":"A subsidy for vaccinations is most likely to:","options":{"A":"Reduce the positive external benefits the programme creates","B":"Increase uptake by reducing the effective price","C":"Raise the private price paid at the point of use","D":"Prevent people from choosing to be vaccinated"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-marketfail"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-MF-05","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-MARKETFAIL","stem":"Why can property rights sometimes help reduce pollution?","options":{"A":"An owner has an incentive to protect and enforce the value of the resource","B":"Property rights make polluting costless for the firm that causes it","C":"No one is able to monitor a resource once it has an owner","D":"They guarantee that every polluter is identified and made to pay full damages"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-INTERVENTION","scaffold":"Clearly defined and enforceable property rights can give owners an incentive to prevent damage and seek compensation. Monitoring, legal costs and unclear boundaries may limit effectiveness.","reforge":{"stem":"A weakness of using property rights to control river pollution is that:","options":{"A":"Owners always have unlimited resources to enforce their rights through the courts","B":"The policy removes the legal costs of bringing a claim entirely","C":"Monitoring a large shared area and identifying polluters may be difficult","D":"Pollution becomes a positive externality once the river has an owner"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-marketfail"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-MF-06","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-MARKETFAIL","stem":"Which is a merit good?","options":{"A":"A good that only a monopoly is allowed to supply to the market","B":"A good that may be under-consumed because consumers underestimate its benefits","C":"A good whose consumption creates negative externalities and no private benefit at all","D":"A good the government must ban because consumers overvalue it"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-MERIT-GOODS","scaffold":"Merit goods such as education may be under-consumed because of imperfect information or short-term decision-making. Governments may subsidise or provide them.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might the government provide information about the benefits of healthy eating?","options":{"A":"Healthy eating is a public bad that harms other people","B":"It guarantees that unhealthy food is no longer purchased by anyone at all","C":"Information always reduces demand for every good it describes","D":"Consumers may otherwise underestimate private and social benefits"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-marketfail"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-MF-07","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-MARKETFAIL","stem":"What does market failure mean?","options":{"A":"The government collects no revenue from the market at all","B":"Every market in the economy has stopped operating altogether and no trade now takes place","C":"The market allocation of resources is inefficient or does not maximise social welfare","D":"Every firm operating in the market makes a loss on what it sells"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-MARKET-FAILURE","scaffold":"Market failure occurs when private decisions lead to an inefficient outcome, often because of externalities, information gaps, public goods or market power. Government intervention can also fail.","reforge":{"stem":"A market producing too much pollution at the unregulated equilibrium is an example of:","options":{"A":"Market failure because social costs are not fully reflected in the price","B":"A budget surplus caused by higher tax revenue","C":"A rise in the economy’s productive capacity over time","D":"Perfect efficiency, because every cost is already priced into the market and paid"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-marketfail"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-MF-08","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-MARKETFAIL","stem":"Why can government intervention in markets be unsuccessful?","options":{"A":"Governments always have perfect information about the markets they regulate","B":"Regulation carries no administrative cost for the government","C":"Taxes never change the behaviour of producers or consumers","D":"Policies may be poorly designed, costly or create unintended effects"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-GOVT-FAILURE","scaffold":"Government failure can arise from information gaps, implementation costs, unintended incentives and political pressures. Intervention should be judged against both the original market failure and its own costs.","reforge":{"stem":"A pollution tax is set too high and causes a large fall in employment with little environmental gain. This illustrates:","options":{"A":"Perfect targeting of the policy by the government","B":"A possible unintended cost of intervention","C":"The end of scarcity in the labour market","D":"A positive externality and nothing more"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-marketfail"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-MKF-09","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-MARKETFAIL","stem":"What is a negative externality?","options":{"A":"A cost imposed on a third party","B":"A benefit received only by the buyer","C":"A tax paid by the producer","D":"A private profit from selling a product"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-EXTERNALITIES","scaffold":"A negative externality is an external cost imposed on people not directly involved in the transaction, such as pollution affecting nearby residents.","reforge":{"stem":"Which is an example of a positive externality?","options":{"A":"A firm's private advertising cost, which it pays for out of its own profits","B":"Noise from a late-night venue disturbing nearby residents","C":"Training that improves a worker's skills and benefits future employers","D":"Congestion caused by one extra driver joining a busy road"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-marketfail"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-MKF-10","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-MARKETFAIL","stem":"Why might a government subsidise a good with positive externalities?","options":{"A":"To make the external benefits of the good disappear from the market","B":"To encourage consumption closer to the socially efficient level","C":"To reduce the private benefits enjoyed by every consumer","D":"To guarantee that one firm holds a monopoly in the market"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-MERIT-GOODS","scaffold":"A subsidy can lower the price paid by consumers and increase consumption of a good that creates benefits for others, such as education or vaccination.","reforge":{"stem":"Which good is most likely to be considered a demerit good?","options":{"A":"A basic household saving held in a bank account","B":"A service with wider educational benefits for society","C":"A public streetlight that anyone can use freely","D":"A product with health costs from overconsumption"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-marketfail"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-MKF-11","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-MARKETFAIL","stem":"What is a possible effect of overconsumption of a demerit good?","options":{"A":"The good becomes a public good","B":"External costs must be zero","C":"Social costs may exceed private costs","D":"The market produces no private benefit"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-GOVT-FAILURE","scaffold":"Demerit goods may be overconsumed because consumers underestimate their costs or do not consider harms imposed on others. The social cost can exceed the private cost.","reforge":{"stem":"Why can information provision fail to correct market failure?","options":{"A":"Consumers may ignore or misunderstand the information","B":"Information always changes every choice a consumer makes","C":"It makes market supply perfectly elastic","D":"It removes the product's external benefits"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-marketfail"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-MKF-12","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-MARKETFAIL","stem":"Which feature is associated with a public good?","options":{"A":"It is rival and excludable","B":"It creates a private cost only","C":"It is sold only by one private firm","D":"It is non-rival and non-excludable"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-MARKET-FAILURE","scaffold":"Public goods are non-rival, so one person's use does not reduce availability to others, and non-excludable, so it is difficult to prevent non-payers using them.","reforge":{"stem":"Why may private firms underprovide a public good?","options":{"A":"Consumers are unable to value it at all","B":"People may free-ride without paying","C":"Its marginal cost is always high","D":"It has no social benefit at all"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-marketfail"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-MKF-13","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-MARKETFAIL","stem":"Which policy can reduce consumption of a good with negative externalities?","options":{"A":"An indirect tax","B":"A subsidy on consumption","C":"Free advertising for the product","D":"Removing all regulation"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-INTERVENTION","scaffold":"An indirect tax raises firms' costs and can increase the market price, reducing quantity demanded and bringing private incentives closer to social costs.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might information campaigns support healthier choices?","options":{"A":"They turn every good in the market into a merit good","B":"They guarantee that demand for the product falls to zero","C":"They can help consumers understand private and social costs","D":"They remove the need for the government to fund public services"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-marketfail"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-MKF-14","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-MARKETFAIL","stem":"What is an opportunity cost of correcting market failure with government spending?","options":{"A":"The external benefit the spending creates","B":"The next best use of the public funds","C":"The entire revenue earned in the market","D":"Every private investment decision"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-GOVT-FAILURE","scaffold":"Government resources are scarce. Spending to address one market failure means the next best alternative use of those funds is forgone.","reforge":{"stem":"Which problem can arise when governments intervene in markets?","options":{"A":"Scarcity disappears from the economy altogether","B":"All externalities are converted into private costs for the firm","C":"Every consumer in the market ends up better off than before","D":"Government failure from poor information or unintended effects"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-marketfail"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-MKF-15","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-MARKETFAIL","stem":"Why may a market fail to allocate resources efficiently?","options":{"A":"Prices always include every social cost","B":"Consumers always have perfect information","C":"External costs or benefits may be ignored","D":"Government never intervenes"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-MARKET-FAILURE","scaffold":"Market prices may not reflect external costs and benefits. This can lead to overconsumption of goods with negative externalities or underconsumption of goods with positive externalities.","reforge":{"stem":"Which situation shows asymmetric information?","options":{"A":"A buyer knows less about a used car's condition than the seller","B":"A firm publishes every one of its costs for buyers to inspect freely","C":"All buyers in the market receive identical information","D":"A government sets a single tax rate that applies to everyone"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-marketfail"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-MKF-16","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-MARKETFAIL","stem":"What is the correct view of government intervention?","options":{"A":"It always improves the way resources are allocated","B":"It never changes the incentives facing producers","C":"It removes the need for consumers to make any choice at all","D":"It may reduce a market failure but create other costs"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-MERIT-GOODS","scaffold":"Intervention can improve outcomes, but taxes, subsidies, rules and state provision may have administrative costs, unintended effects or an opportunity cost.","reforge":{"stem":"Which policy is designed to increase consumption of a merit good?","options":{"A":"A tax on the good at the point of sale","B":"A subsidy or public provision","C":"A ban on advertising information","D":"A quota set below current use"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-marketfail"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-MKF-17","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-MARKETFAIL","stem":"What are market forces?","options":{"A":"Factors determining prices and availability without direct government intervention","B":"Government payments made to every firm operating in the market, funded out of taxation","C":"The total cost of producing all goods in the economy","D":"A firm's forecast of its own future sales"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-INTERVENTION","scaffold":"Market forces are factors that determine price levels and the availability of goods and services in an economy without direct government intervention.","reforge":{"stem":"What is a tax?","options":{"A":"A voluntary payment made to a private firm","B":"A subsidy paid directly to consumers","C":"A compulsory payment to the government","D":"A market price set by supply"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-marketfail"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-MKF-18","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-MARKETFAIL","stem":"What does allocation of resources describe?","options":{"A":"How firms calculate their profit at the end of each year","B":"How scarce resources and goods are distributed","C":"How taxes are collected from households","D":"How one currency is exchanged for another"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-INTERVENTION","scaffold":"Allocation of resources concerns how scarce resources are distributed among producers and how scarce goods and services are allocated among consumers.","reforge":{"stem":"What is a subsidy?","options":{"A":"A compulsory payment made by households and firms to the government each tax year","B":"A firm's average cost of producing one unit","C":"A tax charged on goods imported from other countries","D":"Government money given directly to firms to encourage production or consumption"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-marketfail"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-MKF-19","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-MARKETFAIL","stem":"What is a tax?","options":{"A":"A payment a government makes to firms","B":"A firm's total revenue","C":"A compulsory payment to government","D":"A voluntary donation to a market"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-MARKET-DEF","scaffold":"A tax is a compulsory payment to the government. It can change incentives and influence the allocation of resources.","reforge":{"stem":"What does efficiency involve?","options":{"A":"Optimal production and distribution of scarce resources","B":"Charging every consumer exactly the same amount","C":"Increasing output regardless of what it costs to produce","D":"Producing output without using any inputs"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-marketfail"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-MKF-20","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-MARKETFAIL","stem":"What is a subsidy?","options":{"A":"A compulsory payment that households must make to the government by law each year","B":"A firm's private cost of producing one extra unit","C":"The price a consumer pays for a final good","D":"Government money given directly to firms to encourage production or consumption"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-MARKET-DEF","scaffold":"A subsidy is an amount of money the government gives directly to firms to encourage production and consumption. It can affect market prices and quantities.","reforge":{"stem":"Which phrase best describes market forces?","options":{"A":"A firm's total cost of production","B":"Factors determining prices and availability without direct government intervention","C":"A price fixed by the government that every seller in the market is required to charge","D":"A compulsory payment made to the tax authority"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-marketfail"}]},"GCSE-ECON-P2-TRADE":{"label":"Paper 2: International Trade","color":"#15803d","questions":[{"id":"GCSE-P2-TRADE-01","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-TRADE","stem":"What is an import?","options":{"A":"A good or service bought from another country","B":"A good or service sold to a buyer in another country","C":"A tax charged on goods produced at home","D":"A benefit payment made by the government"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-EXPORTS-IMPORTS","scaffold":"Imports are goods and services purchased from abroad. Exports are domestic goods and services sold to other countries.","reforge":{"stem":"A UK firm buys computer equipment made in South Korea. For the UK this purchase is:","options":{"A":"An export","B":"A current transfer","C":"An import","D":"A domestic subsidy"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-trade"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-TRADE-02","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-TRADE","stem":"Why can specialisation increase international trade?","options":{"A":"It prevents countries from exchanging goods with each other","B":"Countries concentrate resources on goods they can produce relatively efficiently","C":"It requires every country to produce everything that its own consumers want to buy","D":"It removes the opportunity cost of producing any good at all, everywhere"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-TRADE-BENEFITS","scaffold":"Specialisation allows economies to focus on activities where they have an advantage, potentially raising productivity and output. Trade lets them exchange surplus production for other goods.","reforge":{"stem":"A country specialises in renewable-energy equipment and imports food. The main economic rationale is that:","options":{"A":"Imported food costs the country nothing at all","B":"Trade eliminates the risks of relying on other countries","C":"The country no longer faces any scarcity once it begins trading with other countries","D":"Resources are focused where the country is relatively productive, then exchanged"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-trade"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-TRADE-03","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-TRADE","stem":"Which is a possible benefit of free trade?","options":{"A":"No exposure to shocks originating in overseas markets","B":"An end to imports from lower-cost producers abroad","C":"More choice and lower prices from competition and specialisation","D":"Guaranteed employment in every domestic industry that faces imports"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-TRADE-BENEFITS","scaffold":"Free trade can increase competition, reduce prices, expand choice, provide access to inputs and support specialisation. Some workers and industries may face adjustment costs.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might consumers support fewer trade barriers?","options":{"A":"Imported goods may become cheaper and more varied","B":"All imports are banned from entering the country","C":"Every domestic wage is guaranteed by the government","D":"Domestic firms face no competition from abroad"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-trade"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-TRADE-04","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-TRADE","stem":"What is a tariff?","options":{"A":"A subsidy for exports only","B":"A rise in interest rates","C":"A limit on domestic wages","D":"A tax on imports"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-PROTECTIONISM","scaffold":"A tariff raises the domestic price of an imported good and protects domestic producers, but consumers may pay more and other firms may face higher input costs.","reforge":{"stem":"A tariff on imported steel is most likely to:","options":{"A":"Make imported steel free of charge for UK buyers","B":"Raise the domestic price of steel and protect some domestic producers","C":"Lower the cost of steel for every firm in the country that buys and uses it","D":"Guarantee that the country runs a trade surplus in steel"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-trade"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-TRADE-05","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-TRADE","stem":"Why can a country import goods that it could produce itself?","options":{"A":"Other countries may produce them at a lower opportunity cost","B":"Domestic resources are always unlimited","C":"Imports never require payment","D":"Trade is possible only when domestic production is impossible"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-TRADE-BENEFITS","scaffold":"Comparative advantage is based on opportunity cost, not simply whether a country can produce a good. Trade can benefit both sides when each specialises relatively.","reforge":{"stem":"Comparative advantage means a country:","options":{"A":"Has the highest absolute output in every industry that it operates in","B":"Cannot import from countries that are less productive than itself","C":"Can produce a good at a lower opportunity cost than another country","D":"Must export every good that it is able to produce at home"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-trade"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-TRADE-06","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-TRADE","stem":"Why might a country join a trading bloc using a common currency?","options":{"A":"It prevents any member country from leaving the bloc","B":"It can reduce transaction costs and make price comparisons easier","C":"It removes all differences between the member economies","D":"It guarantees equal wages for workers in every member state of the bloc"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-TRADING-BLOCS","scaffold":"A common currency removes the need to exchange money between members and can reduce exchange-rate uncertainty. Members may lose independent monetary policy and face adjustment challenges.","reforge":{"stem":"A common currency may encourage trade because firms:","options":{"A":"Can ignore prices charged in other member states","B":"No longer need to produce goods for export at all","C":"Are protected from competition from every other firm that is based inside the currency area","D":"Do not need to pay currency-conversion costs for transactions within the currency area"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-trade"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-TRADE-07","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-TRADE","stem":"Why might a UK clothing firm move production abroad?","options":{"A":"It wants to eliminate all of its exports","B":"Globalisation makes domestic production impossible","C":"It may seek lower labour or production costs","D":"It must increase its costs in the UK"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-GLOBALISATION","scaffold":"Outsourcing can lower costs and prices or raise profits, but it may reduce domestic manufacturing employment and create dependence on overseas suppliers.","reforge":{"stem":"A UK retailer buys clothes from a lower-cost overseas factory. One possible effect is:","options":{"A":"Lower prices for consumers but pressure on some domestic textile jobs","B":"No change at all for domestic workers or consumers","C":"An automatic fall in the volume of all imports","D":"Higher prices for consumers and more UK textile jobs in every single case"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-trade"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-TRADE-08","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-TRADE","stem":"Which is a risk of relying heavily on international trade?","options":{"A":"The economy becomes completely independent of economic conditions in every other country","B":"Domestic firms face no uncertainty about their supplies","C":"Consumers lose all choice over the goods available to them","D":"A foreign recession or supply disruption can reduce available exports or imports"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-TRADE-RISKS","scaffold":"Trade creates interdependence. Overseas recessions, wars, transport disruption or exchange-rate changes can affect domestic output, prices and employment.","reforge":{"stem":"A disruption closes a major overseas supplier of components. This shows that trade can:","options":{"A":"Guarantee uninterrupted production at every plant","B":"Expose firms to international supply-chain risks","C":"Remove the need to hold any inventories at all","D":"Make every domestic input cheaper to buy than before"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-trade"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-TRD-09","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-TRADE","stem":"Which is a likely benefit of free trade?","options":{"A":"Greater choice and lower prices for some consumers","B":"A permanent ban on competition from suppliers based overseas","C":"No further need for any domestic production","D":"Higher prices in every market that faces imports"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-TRADE-BENEFITS","scaffold":"Free trade can increase competition, variety and access to lower-cost imports. The effects differ across consumers, workers and producers.","reforge":{"stem":"Why may free trade benefit producers?","options":{"A":"It prevents firms achieving economies of scale","B":"It removes all rival firms from the market","C":"It can give firms access to larger markets","D":"It guarantees higher costs per unit"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-trade"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-TRD-10","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-TRADE","stem":"What is a likely effect of an import tariff?","options":{"A":"Imported goods become cheaper for consumers","B":"The domestic price of the import may rise","C":"All exports to that country stop","D":"The government loses the tariff revenue"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-PROTECTIONISM","scaffold":"A tariff is a tax on imports. It can raise the domestic price, protect local producers and generate government revenue, but consumers may face less choice or higher prices.","reforge":{"stem":"Which policy limits the quantity of a good imported?","options":{"A":"A free trade agreement","B":"A subsidy to consumers","C":"A reduction in customs checks","D":"A quota"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-trade"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-TRD-11","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-TRADE","stem":"What does an import quota do?","options":{"A":"It guarantees cheaper imports","B":"It removes every trade barrier","C":"It sets a maximum quantity of imports","D":"It raises domestic productivity directly"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-PROTECTIONISM","scaffold":"A quota is a quantitative restriction on imports. By limiting foreign supply, it can protect domestic firms but may raise prices and reduce consumer choice.","reforge":{"stem":"Which is a possible drawback of protectionism?","options":{"A":"Consumers may pay higher prices","B":"Domestic firms face more competition","C":"Trade barriers always lower costs","D":"Imports become unlimited"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-trade"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-TRD-12","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-TRADE","stem":"Why can specialisation increase output?","options":{"A":"It eliminates the opportunity cost of every choice made","B":"It makes the resources that are available to the economy completely unlimited","C":"It prevents any exchange between workers and firms","D":"Workers and firms can focus on tasks where they are relatively efficient"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-TRADE-BENEFITS","scaffold":"Specialisation allows resources to concentrate on activities with relatively high productivity. Trade then allows countries or firms to obtain other goods.","reforge":{"stem":"What may happen when countries specialise and trade?","options":{"A":"Every country ends up producing the same goods","B":"Resources can be used more efficiently","C":"Imports become impossible to arrange","D":"Consumer choice must shrink over time"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-trade"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-TRD-13","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-TRADE","stem":"Which is a possible gain from international trade for a large producer?","options":{"A":"Access to a wider market and economies of scale","B":"Guaranteed protection from all foreign competition","C":"Fewer potential sales than before","D":"Higher average costs in every single case"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-TRADE-BENEFITS","scaffold":"International demand can allow firms to increase output. Larger scale may reduce average costs, although firms may also face stronger competition.","reforge":{"stem":"Which is a possible cost of free trade for some workers?","options":{"A":"New overseas markets for domestic exporters","B":"A greater variety of goods available for domestic households to buy","C":"Job losses in industries facing intense import competition","D":"Lower prices for some groups of domestic consumers"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-trade"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-TRD-14","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-TRADE","stem":"How can trade increase consumer choice?","options":{"A":"It forces every firm in the market to sell only a single product","B":"It allows households to buy goods from more suppliers","C":"It bans imported services from the market","D":"It prevents firms from specialising at all"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-TRADE-BENEFITS","scaffold":"International trade gives consumers access to products that may not be made domestically and increases the number of potential suppliers.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might a government protect a new domestic industry?","options":{"A":"To make consumers pay no price for the good","B":"To reduce the productive capacity of the entire domestic industry over time","C":"To eliminate every future import of the good","D":"To give it time to develop before facing full foreign competition"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-trade"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-TRD-15","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-TRADE","stem":"Which policy is most likely to increase imports?","options":{"A":"A strict quota limiting imports","B":"A high tariff on imported goods","C":"A free trade agreement reducing barriers","D":"A ban on all overseas suppliers entering the market"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-PROTECTIONISM","scaffold":"Reducing tariffs, quotas and other barriers can make it easier and cheaper for firms to import goods and services.","reforge":{"stem":"What may be a disadvantage of an import quota?","options":{"A":"It may reduce consumer choice and raise prices","B":"It makes the volume of imports unlimited","C":"It guarantees more competition from overseas firms","D":"It always lowers prices in the domestic market"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-trade"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-TRD-16","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-TRADE","stem":"What is the correct view of international trade?","options":{"A":"Every country must export more than it imports each year","B":"Imports never affect firms producing in the home market","C":"Free trade means that no country keeps any of its own trade regulations at all","D":"Trade can allow countries to obtain goods they cannot produce efficiently"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-TRADE-BENEFITS","scaffold":"Trade allows countries to specialise and exchange goods and services. It can create benefits, but also adjustment costs for some workers and firms.","reforge":{"stem":"Which is a possible effect of trade liberalisation?","options":{"A":"All domestic firms become monopolies in their markets","B":"Lower barriers may increase competition and efficiency","C":"Consumers lose access to imported goods entirely","D":"International exchange stops altogether between the two countries"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-trade"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-TRD-17","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-TRADE","stem":"What are exports?","options":{"A":"Goods and services sold abroad","B":"Goods bought from suppliers abroad","C":"Taxes charged on foreign goods","D":"Money saved by UK households"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-EXPORTS-IMPORTS","scaffold":"Exports are goods and services sold to buyers in other countries. They bring income into the exporting country.","reforge":{"stem":"What are imports?","options":{"A":"Goods sold to buyers abroad","B":"Government spending on new roads and bridges","C":"Goods and services bought from abroad","D":"Domestic investment by firms"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-trade"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-TRD-18","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-TRADE","stem":"What is international trade?","options":{"A":"The exchange of goods and services within one town or city","B":"The exchange of goods and services between countries","C":"A tax charged on imported goods only","D":"A firm's internal transfer of stock between sites"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-TRADING-BLOCS","scaffold":"International trade is the exchange of goods and services between countries. It includes imports and exports.","reforge":{"stem":"What is a free trade agreement?","options":{"A":"A ban on imports from every other country","B":"A tax charged on goods leaving the country","C":"A limit placed by the government on the amount that firms may produce at home","D":"Free movement of goods and services between countries without restrictions"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-trade"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-TRD-19","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-TRADE","stem":"Which description fits the European Union in the supplied definitions?","options":{"A":"A single large private company based in Europe","B":"A group of neighbouring countries that have no economic or political links between them","C":"An economic and political group of European countries with free trade between members","D":"A tariff charged on all goods produced in Europe"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-TRADING-BLOCS","scaffold":"The supplied reference describes the European Union as an economic and political group of countries in Europe that have free trade with each other.","reforge":{"stem":"Which item is an export for the UK?","options":{"A":"A service sold to a customer overseas","B":"A machine bought from a supplier in Germany","C":"A tax paid on imported fuel oil","D":"A household purchase made in the UK"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-trade"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-TRD-20","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-TRADE","stem":"Which option defines imports?","options":{"A":"A subsidy paid to a domestic firm","B":"Goods sold to consumers in overseas markets","C":"A free trade agreement between two states","D":"Goods and services bought from abroad"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-EXPORTS-IMPORTS","scaffold":"Imports are goods and services bought from abroad. They are recorded separately from exports in international trade.","reforge":{"stem":"What is international trade?","options":{"A":"A ban on any production taking place overseas by domestic firms","B":"The exchange of goods and services between countries","C":"A tax charged on imports entering the country","D":"A firm's internal sale between its own divisions"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-trade"}]},"GCSE-ECON-P2-BOP":{"label":"Paper 2: Balance of Payments","color":"#16a34a","questions":[{"id":"GCSE-P2-BOP-01","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-BOP","stem":"Which transaction is a credit on the UK current account?","options":{"A":"A UK firm exports software to a French customer","B":"A UK household buys an imported car","C":"A UK tourist paying for a restaurant meal in Spain","D":"A UK firm imports machinery"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-BOP","scaffold":"Exports earn income from abroad and are recorded as credits. Imports and UK spending overseas are debits.","reforge":{"stem":"A UK consultancy is paid by a business in Canada for its services. This is:","options":{"A":"Interest received from a bank based overseas","B":"A government budget deficit","C":"A current-account credit for the UK","D":"A UK import of goods"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-bop"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-BOP-02","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-BOP","stem":"Which item belongs in trade in services?","options":{"A":"A UK tourist paying for a restaurant meal in Spain","B":"A UK accountancy firm advises an overseas client","C":"A UK supermarket imports fruit","D":"A UK factory exports cars"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-BOP","scaffold":"Trade in services records services bought and sold internationally, such as tourism, finance, transport and professional advice. Goods are physical products.","reforge":{"stem":"A foreign visitor pays a UK hotel. For the UK this is:","options":{"A":"An import of goods","B":"A capital transfer only","C":"An import of a service","D":"An export of a service"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-bop"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-BOP-03","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-BOP","stem":"Which combination makes up the current account?","options":{"A":"Only foreign direct investment","B":"A surplus, because sellers offer far more than buyers want to buy","C":"Trade in goods and services, primary income and secondary income","D":"Only government borrowing"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-BOP","scaffold":"The current account includes trade in goods, trade in services, primary income such as profits and interest, and secondary income such as transfers and aid.","reforge":{"stem":"Profits sent to the UK from a UK-owned company abroad are recorded as:","options":{"A":"Primary income received","B":"A tariff","C":"A household tax payment","D":"Trade in goods"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-bop"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-BOP-04","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-BOP","stem":"A country has goods exports of £200bn, goods imports of £250bn, and a services surplus of £30bn. Ignoring other items, it has a:","options":{"A":"£20bn current account surplus","B":"Balanced current account","C":"£80bn surplus","D":"£20bn current account deficit"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-BOP-CALC","scaffold":"The goods balance is -£50bn and the services balance is +£30bn, giving a combined deficit of £20bn before primary and secondary income.","reforge":{"stem":"Goods trade is -£70bn and services trade is +£50bn, with no other current-account items. The balance is:","options":{"A":"A £20bn surplus","B":"A £20bn deficit","C":"A £120bn deficit","D":"Zero"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-bop"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-BOP-05","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-BOP","stem":"Why might a current account deficit be less concerning during an investment boom?","options":{"A":"Imports may include capital goods that increase future productive capacity","B":"A deficit always means no investment","C":"Capital goods are never imported","D":"Consumers automatically pay the lowest possible price for their water supply in every case"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-BOP-EVAL","scaffold":"A deficit can reflect strong domestic demand or imports of machinery and technology that support future exports. Its sustainability depends on how it is financed and the economy’s competitiveness.","reforge":{"stem":"A country imports advanced machinery to expand factories. This may initially worsen the current account but could later:","options":{"A":"It proves exports are rising faster than imports","B":"Remove the need for workers","C":"Raise productive capacity and export potential","D":"Reduce investment"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-bop"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-BOP-06","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-BOP","stem":"Which policy could help reduce a current account deficit in the long run?","options":{"A":"Increasing demand for imports only","B":"Improving productivity so domestic firms become more competitive","C":"Reduce every service that the firm offers down to a lower quality","D":"Reducing investment in education"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-BOP-POLICY","scaffold":"Supply-side policies can reduce unit costs and improve quality, supporting exports and reducing reliance on imports. Demand management and exchange-rate policies may also affect the deficit.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might research and development support improve the current account?","options":{"A":"It makes imports mandatory","B":"It directly removes all transfers","C":"Making exports more expensive without improving quality","D":"Innovative firms may produce more attractive exports"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-bop"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-BOP-07","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-BOP","stem":"What may happen to a current account deficit after a currency depreciation?","options":{"A":"Trade values cannot change","B":"The current account becomes a budget account","C":"Exports may become cheaper and imports dearer, potentially improving the balance","D":"Everyone in the UK equally, because an appreciation changes the value of money in exactly the same way for all households."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-EXCHANGE-RATE","scaffold":"Depreciation can improve price competitiveness, but the final effect depends on elasticities, contracts and whether the value of exports and imports changes enough.","reforge":{"stem":"A weaker domestic currency may reduce a current account deficit if:","options":{"A":"Foreign demand for exports and domestic demand for imports respond sufficiently to prices","B":"Imports become cheaper","C":"Contractionary fiscal policy — reducing aggregate demand in order to bring inflation back under control","D":"No one changes quantities purchased"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-bop"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-BOP-08","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-BOP","stem":"Why might a government not immediately try to eliminate a current account deficit?","options":{"A":"Deficits can never be financed","B":"The current account measures only tax revenue","C":"It would make entry into the market much easier for every small firm that wants to join","D":"It may reflect strong consumer demand or imports for productive investment"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-BOP-EVAL","scaffold":"A current account deficit is not automatically harmful. Policymakers consider its size, persistence, financing, composition and interaction with growth, inflation and employment.","reforge":{"stem":"Which question is most relevant when evaluating a current account deficit?","options":{"A":"Expansionary fiscal policy — raising aggregate demand to support growth and reduce unemployment","B":"Is it temporary and financing productive investment, or persistent and difficult to finance?","C":"Does it mean the budget is balanced?","D":"Are imports always undesirable?"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-bop"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-BOP-09","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-BOP","stem":"Which item is part of the current account?","options":{"A":"Trade in goods and services","B":"A foreign purchase of UK shares","C":"A loan to a government","D":"A new factory built by an overseas firm"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-BOP","scaffold":"The current account includes trade in goods and services, primary income and secondary income. Financial investment is recorded elsewhere in the balance of payments.","reforge":{"stem":"Which transaction is recorded as international investment rather than current trade?","options":{"A":"A UK firm exporting machinery","B":"A foreign company buying up a UK factory","C":"An overseas company buying a UK factory","D":"Interest received from overseas"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-bop"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-BOP-10","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-BOP","stem":"What does a current account deficit mean?","options":{"A":"Exports and income receipts exceed payments","B":"Current payments exceed current receipts","C":"Government spending exceeds tax revenue","D":"The exchange rate is fixed"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-BOP","scaffold":"A current account deficit occurs when payments for goods, services and income exceed receipts over a period. It is not the same as a government budget deficit.","reforge":{"stem":"What may happen when exports rise faster than imports?","options":{"A":"Foreign investment must stop","B":"The current account must worsen","C":"The budget deficit must rise","D":"The current account may improve"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-bop"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-BOP-11","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-BOP","stem":"Which is an example of a secondary income payment?","options":{"A":"A UK tourist buying a hotel room overseas","B":"A UK firm exporting cars","C":"Foreign aid sent from the UK","D":"A foreign firm buying UK land"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-BOP","scaffold":"Secondary income includes transfers where no direct exchange of goods, services or assets occurs, such as foreign aid and some remittances.","reforge":{"stem":"Which receipt can appear in the current account?","options":{"A":"A UK bank receiving interest from a foreign borrower","B":"A foreign investor buying UK shares","C":"A government borrowing abroad","D":"Deliberately shrinking the size of the working-age population"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-bop"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-BOP-12","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-BOP","stem":"Which transaction is not part of the current account?","options":{"A":"A UK consumer buying a service abroad","B":"A UK worker receiving overseas wages","C":"A UK company exporting software","D":"A foreign company purchasing UK land"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-BOP","scaffold":"Trade, income and transfers belong to the current account. The purchase of land is an asset transaction and is recorded in the financial account.","reforge":{"stem":"Which payment would worsen a country's current account, other things equal?","options":{"A":"Selling more exports","B":"Buying more imports than before","C":"A UK firm exporting software abroad","D":"Receiving remittances"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-bop"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-BOP-13","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-BOP","stem":"What is a possible effect of a persistent current account deficit?","options":{"A":"The country may need capital inflows or borrowing","B":"A deficit always means the economy is in recession","C":"Unemployment must fall","D":"Inflation must be zero"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-BOP-EVAL","scaffold":"A persistent deficit must be financed through borrowing, selling assets or attracting financial investment. This may create future repayment or income outflow pressures.","reforge":{"stem":"Which development could improve a current account deficit?","options":{"A":"Higher import spending with unchanged exports","B":"Lower foreign demand for domestic services","C":"More competitive exports","D":"Larger income payments abroad"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-bop"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-BOP-14","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-BOP","stem":"How can poor-quality exports affect the current account?","options":{"A":"They guarantee higher export revenue","B":"They may reduce export demand and worsen the balance","C":"They make imports unnecessary","D":"Making exports more expensive without improving quality"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-BOP-POLICY","scaffold":"If exports are expensive or poor quality, overseas demand may fall. Export receipts may then decrease relative to payments for imports.","reforge":{"stem":"What may a current account surplus indicate?","options":{"A":"Government spending exceeds revenue","B":"No international transactions occur","C":"It guarantees a fall in domestic productivity","D":"Current receipts exceed current payments"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-bop"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-BOP-15","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-BOP","stem":"A country's exports are £80 billion and imports are £95 billion, with other current items balanced. What is its trade balance?","options":{"A":"A £15 billion surplus","B":"A £175 billion balance","C":"A £15 billion deficit","D":"A £95 billion surplus"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-BOP-CALC","scaffold":"The trade balance is exports minus imports: £80 billion - £95 billion = -£15 billion, so there is a £15 billion deficit.","reforge":{"stem":"Exports are £120 billion and imports are £100 billion. What is the trade balance?","options":{"A":"A £20 billion surplus","B":"A £220 billion deficit","C":"A £20 billion deficit","D":"A £100 billion balance"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-bop"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-BOP-16","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-BOP","stem":"Which policy could improve a current account position?","options":{"A":"Reducing foreign demand for services","B":"Increasing import spending without extra exports","C":"Paying more income abroad","D":"Improving export quality and competitiveness"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-BOP-POLICY","scaffold":"Higher-quality, more competitive exports can raise export receipts. The result also depends on import demand, exchange rates and the price elasticity of trade.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might a current account deficit not be an immediate crisis?","options":{"A":"Deficits never require financing","B":"It may be financed by sustainable inward investment","C":"Making exports more expensive without improving quality","D":"It guarantees higher living standards"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-bop"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-BOP-17","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-BOP","stem":"What is the balance of payments?","options":{"A":"A record of all financial transactions between a country and the rest of the world","B":"It removes any need for the firm to offer product quality, because buyers stop caring","C":"A government tax account only","D":"A measure of unemployment"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-BOP","scaffold":"The balance of payments records all financial transactions between one country and the rest of the world. It includes current, capital and financial transactions.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the current account?","options":{"A":"A monopoly has both buyers and sellers in it, while an oligopoly has only sellers","B":"A firm's bank account","C":"The record of trade, income flows and transfers with the rest of the world","D":"The exchange rate"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-bop"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-BOP-18","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-BOP","stem":"What does the current account record?","options":{"A":"Only foreign investment","B":"Trade in goods and services, income flows and transfers","C":"Only government borrowing","D":"Deliberately shrinking the size of the working-age population"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-BOP","scaffold":"The current account records trade in goods and services, income flows and transfers between a country and the rest of the world.","reforge":{"stem":"What is a balanced current account?","options":{"A":"Exports are zero","B":"Imports are greater than exports","C":"Firms face much less pressure to improve their broadband speeds now","D":"Exports plus income and transfers equal the corresponding outflows"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-bop"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-BOP-19","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-BOP","stem":"What is a current account deficit?","options":{"A":"The market always produces a socially fair outcome for everyone","B":"Government spending exceeds tax revenue","C":"Exports and income inflows are less than imports and outflows","D":"The exchange rate is fixed"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-BOP","scaffold":"A current account deficit occurs when exports plus income and transfers flowing in are less than imports plus the corresponding outflows.","reforge":{"stem":"What is a current account surplus?","options":{"A":"Exports plus inflows exceed imports plus outflows","B":"Government revenue is below spending","C":"A UK tourist paying for a restaurant meal in Spain","D":"Imports exceed exports"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-bop"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-BOP-20","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-BOP","stem":"What does a balanced current account mean?","options":{"A":"The financial account is zero","B":"A stronger pound always benefits every UK business equally, whatever it happens to sell","C":"Government spending equals revenue","D":"Exports plus income and transfers equal imports plus the corresponding outflows"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-BOP","scaffold":"A balanced current account occurs when the total inflows from exports, income and transfers equal the corresponding outflows.","reforge":{"stem":"Which situation is a current account deficit?","options":{"A":"A UK tourist paying for a restaurant meal in Spain","B":"Imports and outflows exceed exports and inflows","C":"Exports exceed imports","D":"No trade takes place"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-bop"}]},"GCSE-ECON-P2-EXR":{"label":"Paper 2: Exchange Rates","color":"#15803d","questions":[{"id":"GCSE-P2-EXR-01","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-EXR","stem":"What is an appreciation of the pound?","options":{"A":"A rise in the value of the pound against another currency","B":"Production - the price creates more venues almost immediately","C":"A rise in UK unemployment","D":"A tax on foreign goods"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-APPREC-DEPREC","scaffold":"Appreciation means sterling buys more foreign currency. Depreciation means it buys less. Floating exchange rates change through demand and supply in the foreign exchange market.","reforge":{"stem":"If £1 buys €1.20 instead of €1.10, sterling has:","options":{"A":"Depreciated against the euro","B":"Had no change in value","C":"Appreciated against the euro","D":"Become inconvertible"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-exr"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-EXR-02","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-EXR","stem":"Which change would increase demand for sterling?","options":{"A":"A deficit means foreign currency is not used","B":"Overseas buyers purchasing more UK exports","C":"UK residents buying more imports","D":"UK tourists buying foreign holidays"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-CURRENCY-DEMAND","scaffold":"Foreign buyers need sterling to purchase UK exports, increasing demand for the currency. UK demand for imports increases supply of sterling as residents exchange it for foreign currency.","reforge":{"stem":"A rise in foreign demand for UK financial assets is likely to:","options":{"A":"Have no exchange-rate effect","B":"Reduce the demand for UK assets","C":"Consumers always have perfect information about prices and incomes","D":"Increase demand for sterling and put upward pressure on its value"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-exr"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-EXR-03","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-EXR","stem":"What is a likely effect of pound depreciation on UK import prices?","options":{"A":"Import prices cannot change","B":"Exchange rate changes have no effect on either exporters or importers at all","C":"Imported goods and raw materials become more expensive in pounds","D":"All imports become cheaper"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-EXCHANGE-RATE","scaffold":"When sterling falls, more pounds are needed to buy foreign currency. Importers may pass higher costs to consumers, creating cost-push inflation.","reforge":{"stem":"A weaker pound raises the cost of imported energy. This may:","options":{"A":"Increase production costs and add to inflationary pressure","B":"Make imported energy free","C":"A weaker pound only ever benefits UK consumers, never firms","D":"Reduce every firm’s costs"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-exr"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-EXR-04","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-EXR","stem":"How can a stronger pound affect UK exporters?","options":{"A":"UK exporters, since their goods become more competitive abroad","B":"Exports are automatically banned","C":"Their imported inputs become more expensive","D":"Their goods become more expensive for overseas buyers"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-EXCHANGE-RATE","scaffold":"Appreciation raises the foreign-currency price of UK exports, potentially reducing demand. It can also lower the sterling cost of imported inputs, benefiting some firms.","reforge":{"stem":"A UK holiday abroad becomes cheaper after sterling appreciates because:","options":{"A":"A weaker pound makes both exports and imports cheaper","B":"Each pound buys more foreign currency","C":"UK wages automatically rise","D":"The pound has depreciated"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-exr"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-EXR-05","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-EXR","stem":"A UK company must pay €1,000 when £1 = €1.25. Approximately how many pounds are needed?","options":{"A":"£800","B":"£1,000","C":"£1,250","D":"£80"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-EXR-CALC","scaffold":"Pounds needed = euros / euros per pound: €1,000 / 1.25 = £800.","reforge":{"stem":"If £1 = $1.25, how many dollars would £400 buy?","options":{"A":"$320","B":"$1,600","C":"$500","D":"$1,250"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-exr"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-EXR-06","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-EXR","stem":"Why might a current account deficit put downward pressure on a currency?","options":{"A":"A deficit means foreign currency is not used","B":"Residents need to sell the domestic currency to buy imports","C":"Exports always increase demand for the currency","D":"Prices always include every external benefit that the good creates"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-CURRENCY-DEMAND","scaffold":"When residents purchase imports, they supply domestic currency in exchange for foreign currency. If this pressure exceeds demand from exports and capital inflows, the currency may depreciate.","reforge":{"stem":"If a country persistently imports much more than it exports, one possible exchange-rate pressure is:","options":{"A":"No need for foreign currency","B":"Sell only to overseas customers and face no competition","C":"Guaranteed appreciation","D":"Downward pressure on its currency from greater supply"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-exr"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-EXR-07","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-EXR","stem":"What is a possible benefit of a currency appreciation?","options":{"A":"Exchange rate changes have no effect on either exporters or importers at all","B":"A rise in import prices","C":"Cheaper imports and lower costs for firms using imported inputs","D":"More expensive imports"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-EXCHANGE-RATE","scaffold":"Appreciation makes foreign goods and inputs cheaper in domestic currency, which can reduce inflation and help consumers. Exporters may lose competitiveness.","reforge":{"stem":"A stronger pound may help UK manufacturers that:","options":{"A":"Buy imported machinery or raw materials","B":"Depend entirely on expensive exports","C":"Have no imported inputs","D":"Sell only to overseas customers and face no competition"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-exr"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-EXR-08","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-EXR","stem":"Why may a depreciation fail to improve the current account quickly?","options":{"A":"Exports become impossible to sell","B":"It will have no effect at all, because the commercial banks simply ignore the base rate entirely","C":"Imports always become cheaper","D":"Contracts and inelastic demand may prevent quantities responding immediately"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-J-CURVE","scaffold":"The value of trade depends on price elasticities and time. In the short run, contracts and inelastic demand can cause the balance to worsen before quantities adjust.","reforge":{"stem":"The J-curve effect suggests a depreciation may initially:","options":{"A":"Imports create unlimited demand for domestic currency","B":"Worsen the current account before improving it later","C":"Have no effect on imports","D":"Make exports more expensive"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-exr"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-EXR-09","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-EXR","stem":"What is a likely effect of an appreciation of the pound?","options":{"A":"Imports become cheaper in pounds","B":"UK exports become cheaper abroad","C":"Imported raw materials cost more","D":"The pound buys less foreign currency"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-EXCHANGE-RATE","scaffold":"An appreciation increases the pound's purchasing power abroad. Imports may become cheaper, while UK exports may become more expensive to foreign buyers.","reforge":{"stem":"What is a likely effect of a depreciation of the pound?","options":{"A":"Foreign holidays become cheaper for UK residents","B":"UK imports become cheaper","C":"UK exports may become more price competitive","D":"Imported components cost less"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-exr"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-EXR-10","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-EXR","stem":"How can a depreciation affect UK exporters?","options":{"A":"Their goods become more expensive for overseas buyers","B":"Their goods may become cheaper in foreign currency","C":"Their imported inputs become cheaper","D":"Their export markets disappear automatically"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-CURRENCY-DEMAND","scaffold":"When sterling depreciates, foreign buyers need less of their currency to purchase a given sterling-priced export. Demand may rise, depending on price elasticity and contracts.","reforge":{"stem":"Which change would increase demand for pounds in a floating exchange market?","options":{"A":"Increase supply of sterling and lower its value","B":"UK residents buying more imports","C":"UK investors buying more overseas assets","D":"Foreign buyers purchasing more UK exports"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-exr"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-EXR-11","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-EXR","stem":"If overseas demand for UK exports rises, what pressure may this place on sterling?","options":{"A":"No possible pressure","B":"Downward pressure from lower currency demand","C":"Upward pressure from higher currency demand","D":"A fixed exchange rate must result"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-CURRENCY-DEMAND","scaffold":"Foreign buyers need pounds to purchase UK exports, so higher export demand can increase demand for sterling and cause appreciation, other things equal.","reforge":{"stem":"Which transaction increases the supply of pounds in foreign exchange markets?","options":{"A":"UK tourists buying goods abroad","B":"Foreign buyers purchasing UK exports","C":"Foreign investors buying UK assets","D":"Overseas firms receiving UK dividends"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-exr"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-EXR-12","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-EXR","stem":"Why might higher UK interest rates appreciate the pound?","options":{"A":"A firm selling mainly imported goods to UK households","B":"They make imports impossible","C":"They reduce demand for sterling","D":"They can attract overseas savings and investment"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-EXCHANGE-RATE","scaffold":"Higher UK interest rates can increase returns on UK financial assets, attracting capital inflows and increasing demand for sterling. The result is not guaranteed.","reforge":{"stem":"How may an appreciation affect UK consumers?","options":{"A":"Every domestic price must rise","B":"Imported goods may become less expensive","C":"No one, since appreciation only ever causes harm","D":"Imported fuel costs more in pounds"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-exr"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-EXR-13","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-EXR","stem":"A UK firm must pay a US supplier $28,000. If £1 falls from $1.40 to $1.25, how much will the payment cost in pounds at the new rate?","options":{"A":"£22,400","B":"£20,000","C":"£35,000","D":"£2,240"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-EXR-CALC","scaffold":"Divide the dollar payment by the new dollar value of one pound: $28,000 / 1.25 = £22,400. The depreciation makes the imported input more expensive in pounds.","reforge":{"stem":"A UK buyer needs to pay €18,000 when £1 = €1.20. How many pounds are needed?","options":{"A":"£21,600","B":"£16,200","C":"£15,000","D":"£1,500"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-exr"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-EXR-14","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-EXR","stem":"What may happen to the current account after a depreciation?","options":{"A":"Exports and imports are both guaranteed to fall","B":"The current account may improve if export and import demand respond sufficiently","C":"Tariffs only affect foreign countries, and never affect UK consumers or businesses at all","D":"Trade is unaffected in every case"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-J-CURVE","scaffold":"Depreciation can increase export demand and reduce import demand, but the current account effect depends on price elasticities, contracts and the time period.","reforge":{"stem":"Which factor affects whether depreciation raises export revenue?","options":{"A":"Improve the current account instantly in every case","B":"The colour of the currency","C":"The country's population alone","D":"The price elasticity of demand for exports"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-exr"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-EXR-15","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-EXR","stem":"Which UK group may lose from a depreciation?","options":{"A":"Firms selling exports","B":"The current account becomes a budget account","C":"Consumers buying imported food and fuel","D":"Firms competing with imports"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-EXCHANGE-RATE","scaffold":"A depreciation raises the sterling price of imports. Households buying imported goods or firms using imported inputs may face higher costs.","reforge":{"stem":"Which producer may benefit from a weaker pound?","options":{"A":"An exporter whose overseas prices fall in foreign currency","B":"A firm buying all inputs from abroad","C":"A weaker pound only ever benefits UK consumers, never firms","D":"A household purchasing a foreign holiday"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-exr"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-EXR-16","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-EXR","stem":"How can a depreciation contribute to inflation?","options":{"A":"Their goods become more expensive for overseas buyers","B":"The money supply must fall","C":"Domestic firms face no cost changes","D":"Imported goods and inputs may become more expensive"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-CURRENCY-DEMAND","scaffold":"A weaker pound raises the sterling price of imported consumer goods, energy and raw materials. Firms may pass higher costs on through prices.","reforge":{"stem":"What mainly determines a floating exchange rate?","options":{"A":"The population size alone","B":"Demand for and supply of the currency","C":"The number of schools","D":"Exports always increase demand for the currency"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-exr"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-EXR-17","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-EXR","stem":"What is a currency?","options":{"A":"The system of money used by a country or group of countries","B":"The price of a house","C":"A tax on imports","D":"Less choice for consumers and a weaker standard of service overall"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-APPREC-DEPREC","scaffold":"Currency is the system of money used in a country or group of countries, such as the pound in the UK or the euro in participating countries.","reforge":{"stem":"What is an exchange rate?","options":{"A":"The rate of inflation","B":"Their goods become cheaper for overseas buyers","C":"The price of one currency in terms of another","D":"The wage paid to a worker"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-exr"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-EXR-18","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-EXR","stem":"What does an exchange rate show?","options":{"A":"To stop responding to information coming from the market","B":"The price of one currency expressed in another currency","C":"The cost of domestic production","D":"The price level of one household"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-APPREC-DEPREC","scaffold":"An exchange rate is the price of one currency in terms of another. It determines how much foreign currency a unit of domestic currency can buy.","reforge":{"stem":"Which item is a currency?","options":{"A":"UK exports become more expensive to UK buyers","B":"A tax payment","C":"An export invoice","D":"The euro used by participating countries"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-exr"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-EXR-19","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-EXR","stem":"Which option defines an exchange rate?","options":{"A":"The total value of imports","B":"A government's tax revenue","C":"The price of one currency in terms of another","D":"Foreign holidays become cheaper for UK residents"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-APPREC-DEPREC","scaffold":"The exchange rate expresses the value of one currency using another currency, for example dollars per pound.","reforge":{"stem":"Which example is a currency?","options":{"A":"The pound sterling used in the UK","B":"A bank rate","C":"A trade deficit","D":"The number of workers in a country"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-exr"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-EXR-20","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-EXR","stem":"What is the pound in the phrase 'the UK currency'?","options":{"A":"A balance of payments account","B":"An exchange-rate graph","C":"A tax on foreign goods","D":"A currency used by the UK"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-APPREC-DEPREC","scaffold":"A currency is the system of money used in a country or group of countries. Sterling is the UK's currency.","reforge":{"stem":"What does the exchange rate tell a trader?","options":{"A":"Sell only to overseas customers and face no competition","B":"How much of one currency can be exchanged for another","C":"The total tax a firm owes","D":"The level of government spending"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-exr"}]},"GCSE-ECON-P2-GLOBAL":{"label":"Paper 2: Globalisation","color":"#16a34a","questions":[{"id":"GCSE-P2-GLOB-01","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-GLOBAL","stem":"What is globalisation?","options":{"A":"Growing economic integration and interdependence between countries","B":"A ban on all international trade","C":"Higher prices for consumers and more UK textile jobs in every single case","D":"A fall in communication between countries"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-GLOBALISATION","scaffold":"Globalisation involves increasing flows of goods, services, capital, technology, information and sometimes labour across borders. Transport and communication have helped accelerate it.","reforge":{"stem":"Which development is evidence of globalisation?","options":{"A":"A country bans all foreign investment","B":"It removes access to overseas suppliers and makes international communication more expensive for firms","C":"A firm designs a product in one country, sources components globally and sells worldwide","D":"Consumers can buy only local products"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-global"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-GLOB-02","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-GLOBAL","stem":"Why can multinational corporations increase globalisation?","options":{"A":"They reduce global connections by making firms serve only their domestic markets","B":"They operate, invest and organise production across several countries","C":"They produce only for one local market","D":"They prevent foreign direct investment"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-MNC","scaffold":"Multinational corporations can move capital, technology, management and production between countries. They may create jobs and investment, but their power and working conditions can be controversial.","reforge":{"stem":"A multinational builds a factory in a developing country. This is an example of:","options":{"A":"A fall in capital flows between countries caused by reduced international investment","B":"Economic isolation","C":"A domestic import tariff","D":"Foreign direct investment"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-global"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-GLOB-03","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-GLOBAL","stem":"How has the internet supported globalisation?","options":{"A":"It removes access to overseas suppliers and makes international communication more expensive for firms","B":"It makes information less available","C":"It allows firms and consumers to communicate, compare prices and trade internationally","D":"It prevents firms advertising abroad"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-GLOBALISATION","scaffold":"Digital communication lowers information and transaction costs. Firms can coordinate supply chains and market products globally, while consumers can compare and buy from overseas.","reforge":{"stem":"Online platforms can increase globalisation because they:","options":{"A":"Connect businesses and customers across national borders","B":"Make all products non-tradable","C":"A country producing everything that it needs entirely by itself","D":"Prevent international sales"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-global"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-GLOB-04","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-GLOBAL","stem":"Which is a possible benefit of globalisation for a developing economy?","options":{"A":"They reduce global connections by making firms serve only their domestic markets","B":"It prevents domestic firms exporting","C":"It removes all environmental costs","D":"Foreign investment may create jobs, skills and access to technology"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-MNC","scaffold":"Globalisation can bring capital, employment, technology transfer and export opportunities. Benefits depend on regulation, local skills, infrastructure and how gains are distributed.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might a developing country welcome a multinational investment?","options":{"A":"It guarantees that every profit earned by the multinational remains inside the host country","B":"It may add capital and jobs that support growth","C":"It eliminates all dependence on trade","D":"It prevents workers gaining skills"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-global"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-GLOB-05","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-GLOBAL","stem":"Which is a possible cost of globalisation for workers in a high-income country?","options":{"A":"Some jobs may be outsourced or face greater international competition","B":"All wages become identical because firms can recruit workers from every country","C":"No industries change","D":"Competition always disappears"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-GLOB-COSTS","scaffold":"Trade and mobile production can create efficiency and lower prices, but workers in industries facing import competition or relocation may lose jobs or bargaining power.","reforge":{"stem":"A factory moves production overseas to lower costs. Workers left in the original country may face:","options":{"A":"Guaranteed higher wages for all remaining workers because overseas production reduces domestic competition","B":"A fall in international competition","C":"Structural unemployment and the need to retrain","D":"No change in employment"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-global"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-GLOB-06","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-GLOBAL","stem":"What is one concern about multinational corporations?","options":{"A":"A fall in capital flows between countries caused by reduced international investment","B":"They may shift profits, exploit weak regulation or create environmental damage","C":"They can never create employment","D":"They are unable to transfer technology"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-MNC","scaffold":"MNCs can bring benefits, but governments may worry about tax avoidance, bargaining power, labour standards, environmental costs and profit repatriation.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might a host government regulate a multinational factory?","options":{"A":"To prevent any technology transfer from leaving the firm's home country and reaching local workers","B":"Because foreign firms cannot produce goods","C":"To prevent any jobs being created","D":"To protect workers and the environment while retaining investment benefits"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-global"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-GLOB-07","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-GLOBAL","stem":"Why is GDP per capita not a complete measure of development?","options":{"A":"The current account balance, which measures trade and financial flows but not schooling","B":"It cannot be calculated from GDP","C":"It does not fully show inequality, health, education or environmental quality","D":"It measures only population"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-GDP-WELLBEING","scaffold":"GDP per capita is an average output or income measure. Development also involves health, education, life expectancy, equality, security and environmental sustainability.","reforge":{"stem":"Which indicator adds information that GDP per capita may miss?","options":{"A":"Life expectancy or educational attainment","B":"The number of imports recorded, without considering health or education outcomes","C":"A firm’s advertising budget","D":"The exchange rate alone"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-global"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-GLOB-08","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-GLOBAL","stem":"What is a likely effect of greater global supply-chain interdependence?","options":{"A":"The economy becomes completely independent of economic conditions in every other country","B":"Trade becomes impossible","C":"Firms no longer need suppliers","D":"A disruption in one country can affect production and prices elsewhere"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-TRADE-RISKS","scaffold":"Global supply chains can lower costs but also transmit shocks quickly. Firms may respond by holding inventories, diversifying suppliers or reshoring some production.","reforge":{"stem":"A port closure delays components used by factories worldwide. This demonstrates that globalisation can:","options":{"A":"Prevent international effects by isolating each country from foreign supply chains","B":"Spread supply disruptions across countries","C":"Remove the need for logistics","D":"Guarantee stable prices"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-global"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-GLOB-09","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-GLOBAL","stem":"What does economic interdependence mean?","options":{"A":"Countries rely on one another through trade and finance","B":"Every country produces all of its goods and services alone without trade or finance","C":"Imports and exports no longer exist","D":"Firms cannot operate internationally"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-GLOBALISATION","scaffold":"Interdependence means economies are connected. A change in one country can affect others through trade, investment, supply chains and financial markets.","reforge":{"stem":"Which example shows economic interdependence?","options":{"A":"A firm operating only in its domestic market with no overseas suppliers or customers","B":"A country ending all imports","C":"A factory relying on components from several countries","D":"A household producing every good itself"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-global"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-GLOB-10","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-GLOBAL","stem":"What is a possible risk of global supply chains?","options":{"A":"Firms need no transport because components can reach factories without logistics","B":"A disruption in one country can affect production elsewhere","C":"All prices become fixed","D":"Countries become immune to shocks"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-TRADE-RISKS","scaffold":"Global supply chains can lower costs but spread disruption. A port closure, war or natural disaster may delay inputs used by firms in many countries.","reforge":{"stem":"Which is a possible benefit of global supply chains?","options":{"A":"They end international competition by allowing firms to use only domestic inputs","B":"They prevent all shortages","C":"They remove the need for inventories","D":"Firms may access specialised inputs at lower cost"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-global"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-GLOB-11","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-GLOBAL","stem":"What is one possible effect of freer movement of labour?","options":{"A":"All wages become identical because firms can recruit workers from every country","B":"Workers can never change country","C":"Firms may access a wider range of skills","D":"Productivity must fall"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-GLOB-COSTS","scaffold":"Labour mobility can help firms fill vacancies and match workers to jobs. It may also create adjustment pressures for some local workers and public services.","reforge":{"stem":"Which outcome may result from greater labour mobility?","options":{"A":"Skills shortages may be eased in some sectors","B":"No worker can find employment abroad because national labour markets remain completely closed","C":"All regional differences disappear","D":"Every industry shrinks"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-global"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-GLOB-12","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-GLOBAL","stem":"Why may multinational firms increase globalisation?","options":{"A":"They avoid all international markets and restrict production to a single domestic town","B":"They produce only for one local town","C":"They prevent trade between countries","D":"They operate and invest across national borders"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-MNC","scaffold":"Multinational firms locate production, sales and investment in multiple countries. Their decisions connect national economies and supply chains.","reforge":{"stem":"Which is a possible concern about multinational firms?","options":{"A":"They cannot employ local workers because every task must be completed by staff from headquarters","B":"They may shift production between countries to lower costs","C":"They never transfer technology","D":"They make trade impossible"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-global"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-GLOB-13","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-GLOBAL","stem":"Which indicator can help measure development beyond GDP per capita?","options":{"A":"Life expectancy","B":"The exchange rate alone","C":"One firm's advertising spending","D":"The number of imported cars"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-GDP-WELLBEING","scaffold":"Development includes health, education and living conditions as well as material output. Life expectancy is one useful but incomplete indicator.","reforge":{"stem":"Which measure adds information about educational development?","options":{"A":"The current account balance, which measures trade and financial flows but not schooling","B":"The inflation rate","C":"Adult literacy or school enrolment","D":"The value of a currency"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-global"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-GLOB-14","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-GLOBAL","stem":"What does sustainable development seek to protect?","options":{"A":"Only current-year output, even if meeting today's targets exhausts resources","B":"The ability of future generations to meet their needs","C":"A permanent trade deficit","D":"Unlimited resource use"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-SUSTAINABILITY","scaffold":"Sustainable development balances current economic and social needs with protection of environmental resources and future living standards.","reforge":{"stem":"Which policy supports environmental sustainability?","options":{"A":"Increasing waste without controls while claiming that future clean-up will solve the problem","B":"Ignoring pollution costs","C":"Using finite resources without investment","D":"Subsidising energy efficiency and renewable generation"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-global"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-GLOB-15","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-GLOBAL","stem":"Why can globalisation increase consumer choice?","options":{"A":"Imports are always banned, so consumers can buy products only from local firms","B":"Only local firms can sell","C":"Consumers can access products from more countries","D":"Trade becomes impossible"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-GLOB-COSTS","scaffold":"International trade and global firms can make a wider range of goods and services available. The benefits may be accompanied by concerns about jobs, inequality and the environment.","reforge":{"stem":"What is one possible cost of globalisation?","options":{"A":"Some workers may face stronger competition from overseas production","B":"Firms cannot specialise because international trade prevents them from developing efficient production","C":"Consumers receive no variety","D":"Supply chains become local only"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-global"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-GLOB-16","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-GLOBAL","stem":"What is the most accurate view of globalisation?","options":{"A":"It brings only benefits, because no economy can be harmed by shared shocks or foreign competition","B":"It brings only costs","C":"It removes all government choices","D":"It can create gains while exposing economies to shared shocks"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-TRADE-RISKS","scaffold":"Globalisation can lower costs, expand markets and spread technology, but it can also transmit crises, increase inequality or create environmental pressure.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might a country diversify its overseas suppliers?","options":{"A":"To end all trade by making the country dependent on one domestic supplier","B":"To reduce the risk from a disruption in one source","C":"To make every input identical","D":"To remove consumer choice"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-global"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-GLOB-17","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-GLOBAL","stem":"What is international trade?","options":{"A":"The exchange of goods and services between countries","B":"A ban on foreign investment that prevents goods and services crossing national borders","C":"A domestic tax system","D":"A firm's internal production"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GE-TRADING-BLOCS","scaffold":"International trade is the exchange of goods and services between countries. It creates economic links and allows specialisation.","reforge":{"stem":"What does a free trade agreement provide?","options":{"A":"A restriction on all imports that prevents participating countries from trading freely","B":"A ban on exports","C":"Movement of goods and services between countries without restrictions","D":"A fixed domestic wage"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-global"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-GLOB-18","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-GLOBAL","stem":"Which option defines a free trade agreement?","options":{"A":"An agreement banning imports and preventing participating countries from exchanging goods and services","B":"An agreement allowing free movement of goods and services without restrictions","C":"A tax on all exports","D":"A limit on international communication"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GE-EXPORTS-IMPORTS","scaffold":"A free trade agreement allows goods and services to move between participating countries without trade restrictions such as tariffs or quotas.","reforge":{"stem":"What is international trade?","options":{"A":"A firm's sales within one town, with no exchange of goods or services across borders","B":"A government budget plan","C":"A tax on domestic income","D":"The exchange of goods and services between countries"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-global"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-GLOB-19","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-GLOBAL","stem":"Which outcome is associated with international trade?","options":{"A":"Domestic markets disappear because every country becomes entirely dependent on foreign suppliers","B":"All countries become self-sufficient","C":"Goods and services are exchanged across national borders","D":"Imports are made illegal"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GE-TRADING-BLOCS","scaffold":"International trade links countries through exchanges of goods and services. It can increase choice and specialisation but may create adjustment costs.","reforge":{"stem":"Which phrase best describes a free trade agreement?","options":{"A":"Free movement of goods and services between participating countries","B":"A restriction on foreign goods that prevents participating countries from trading across borders","C":"A tax on every import","D":"A ban on all overseas sales"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-global"},{"id":"GCSE-P2-GLOB-20","spec":"GCSE-ECON-P2-GLOBAL","stem":"What is a free trade agreement?","options":{"A":"A limit on exports that prevents participating countries from exchanging goods and services freely","B":"A policy of banning imports","C":"A domestic income tax","D":"Free movement of goods and services between countries without restrictions"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GE-EXPORTS-IMPORTS","scaffold":"A free trade agreement removes or reduces restrictions on the movement of goods and services between participating countries.","reforge":{"stem":"Which situation is international trade?","options":{"A":"A government paying benefits to households, with no goods or services crossing a national border","B":"A UK firm selling software to a customer abroad","C":"A household buying a locally made meal","D":"A firm moving money between its own accounts"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-gcse-econ-p2-global"}]}});
